var e={"surgery-office-wound-suturing":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **wound** is a break in the continuity of skin or mucosa and the tissue below.`,`Mechanism types: incised, lacerated, abrasion, puncture, crush, avulsion and bite.`,`The mechanism predicts how much contamination and dead tissue to expect.`,`**Golden period** for primary closure is about **6 hours** on limbs and trunk.`,`Face and scalp can be closed up to **12-24 hours** because of the rich blood supply.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**CDC wound class I-IV**: clean, clean-contaminated, contaminated and dirty or infected.`,`Any fresh traumatic wound is **class III (contaminated)**, with 10-17% infection risk.`,`Healing is by **primary, secondary or tertiary (delayed primary)** intention.`,`A **tetanus-prone wound** is over 6 hours old, a puncture, crush, burn, bite or soil-contaminated.`,`**WHO rabies category III** means transdermal bites, licks on broken skin or any bat exposure.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. **Haemostasis** (minutes): vessel spasm, platelet plug and fibrin clot.`,`2. **Inflammation** (days 1-4): neutrophils, then macrophages, the key cell, clear debris.`,`3. **Proliferation** (day 4 to week 3): type III collagen and granulation tissue form.`,`4. Epithelium seals a sutured wound in about **48 hours**, so keep it dry for 2 days.`,`5. **Remodelling**: type I collagen replaces type III; scar has **20% strength at 3 weeks**.`,`6. A mature scar never regains more than about **80%** of normal strength.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Write down the **exact time of injury**; it decides primary versus delayed closure.`,`Ask the mechanism; a **high-pressure injection** injury is an emergency however small.`,`Ask about glass, wood or metal at the scene; retained foreign body stops healing.`,`For bites, ask which animal, provoked or not, and whether it can be observed 10 days.`,`A small wound over a knuckle after a fight is a **clenched-fist human bite**.`,`Ask the **tetanus vaccination history** and date of the last dose.`,`Ask about diabetes, steroids, anticoagulants, keloid tendency and drug allergies.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Control bleeding with **direct pressure and elevation** for 10 minutes; never clamp blindly.`,`**Examine nerve, tendon and circulation before injecting** the local anaesthetic.`,`Normal **two-point discrimination** at the finger pulp is under 6 mm.`,`Test **FDP** by blocking the PIP joint and **FDS** by holding the other fingers straight.`,`Explore the wound in a bloodless field through the **full range of movement**.`,`Pain out of proportion, crepitus or bullae suggest **necrotising infection**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Most simple lacerations need **no investigation**; diagnosis is by exploration.`,`**X-ray in two planes** for glass or metal, fracture or air in a joint.`,`**Ultrasound** finds wood and thorn, which a normal X-ray does not exclude.`,`Check **capillary glucose** in any adult with a slow-healing or infected wound.`,`Send a pus swab only from an infected wound, before antibiotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Irrigation** prevents infection: **50-100 mL saline per cm** through a 20 mL syringe and 18 G cannula.`,`Do not pour hydrogen peroxide or spirit into the wound; they kill fibroblasts.`,`**Debride** dead and crushed tissue back to bleeding edges, sparingly on the face.`,`Close primarily only if the wound is **clean and under 6 hours** old (face 24 hours).`,`Calculate local anaesthetic in mg: **lignocaine 3 mg/kg plain, 7 mg/kg with adrenaline**.`,`Use **simple interrupted** nylon sutures; enter at 90 degrees with equal bites.`,`Tissue adhesive suits clean, straight, low-tension wounds under 5 cm, especially in children.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Contaminated, puncture, crush and cat or human bite wounds are left open.`,`**Delayed primary closure on day 3-5** is the answer for a contaminated wound.`,`Wash every animal bite with soap and running water for **15 minutes**.`,`No antibiotic is needed for a clean, well-irrigated laceration.`,`Give antibiotics for bites, hand wounds, open fractures, crush wounds and diabetes.`,`Give **Td**, tetanus immunoglobulin and rabies prophylaxis as the tables below decide.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Early: infection, dehiscence, missed tendon or nerve injury, retained foreign body.`,`Late: hypertrophic scar or **keloid**; use silicone gel and sunscreen for months.`,`**LAST** starts with circumoral tingling, metallic taste, tinnitus and twitching.`,`For LAST, stop injecting, give oxygen, midazolam for fits and **20% lipid 1.5 mL/kg**.`,`Trismus or neck stiffness after a neglected wound means **tetanus**; admit to ICU.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer before closing any wound with **tendon, nerve or vessel injury** or an open joint.`,`Also refer clenched-fist injury, high-pressure injection and eyelid margin or vermilion wounds.`,`Review bites and contaminated wounds at **24-48 hours** for infection.`,`Remove sutures: face 4-5 days, trunk 7-10, palm and over joints 12-14.`,`Write the next tetanus and rabies dose dates on the patient's card.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for wounds and suturing`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Lignocaine 1% (10 mg/mL)`,`Max 3 mg/kg plain; 7 mg/kg with adrenaline`,`Lasts 30-60 min plain`,`LAST; aspirate before injecting`],[`Bupivacaine 0.25-0.5%`,`Max 2 mg/kg (150 mg)`,`Lasts 4-8 hours`,`Most cardiotoxic; never Bier block`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS after food`,`3-5 days for bites`,`Diarrhoea, rare cholestatic jaundice`],[`Doxycycline plus metronidazole`,`100 mg BD plus 400 mg TDS oral`,`5 days (penicillin allergy)`,`Not in pregnancy or under 8 years`],[`Td vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Booster by wound type`,`Separate site from immunoglobulin`],[`Tetanus immunoglobulin`,`250 IU IM (500 IU if over 24 h)`,`Single dose`,`Only if under 3 or unknown doses`],[`Anti-rabies vaccine`,`IM days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 or ID 2-2-2-0-2`,`Full schedule`,`Never in the gluteal region`],[`Rabies immunoglobulin`,`hRIG 20 IU/kg or eRIG 40 IU/kg`,`Once, into the wounds`,`Category III only`],[`20% lipid emulsion`,`1.5 mL/kg IV, then 0.25 mL/kg/min`,`Max about 12 mL/kg`,`Only for LA toxicity`]]},{heading:`Tetanus prophylaxis`,columns:[`Wound and vaccine history`,`Td booster`,`Tetanus immunoglobulin`],rows:[[`Clean wound, 3 or more doses`,`Only if 10 years or more`,`No`],[`Clean wound, under 3 or unknown`,`Start full course`,`No`],[`Tetanus-prone, 3 or more doses`,`Only if 5 years or more`,`No`],[`Tetanus-prone, under 3 or unknown`,`Start full course`,`Yes, 250 IU, different site`]]},{heading:`Suture size and removal by site`,columns:[`Site`,`Suture`,`Remove on day`],rows:[[`Face and eyelid`,`5-0 or 6-0 nylon`,`4-5`],[`Scalp`,`3-0 nylon or staples`,`7-10`],[`Trunk`,`3-0 nylon`,`7-10`],[`Upper limb and hand`,`4-0 nylon`,`10`],[`Palm, sole, over a joint`,`3-0 or 4-0 nylon`,`12-14`],[`Lower limb`,`3-0 or 4-0 nylon`,`10-14`]]}],remember:[`Three questions decide everything: how long ago, how dirty, is anything divided.`,`1% lignocaine is 10 mg/mL; say the dose in mg, not mL.`,`Irrigation volume, not the antibiotic, prevents wound infection.`,`Contaminated wound: delayed primary closure on day 3-5, never primary closure.`,`Never inject local anaesthetic into pus; block around it instead.`,`Category III bite: wash 15 minutes, RIG into the wound, vaccinate, leave it open.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - wounds, local anaesthesia and suturing`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Break in continuity of skin or mucosa; type by mechanism - **incised, lacerated, abrasion, puncture, crush, avulsion, bite** - predicts contamination and dead tissue.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**CDC wound class I-IV** (clean under 2% SSI to dirty over 27%; any fresh traumatic wound = III); healing by **primary, secondary, tertiary (delayed primary) intention**; **WHO rabies category I-III**; tetanus-prone vs clean.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Road, farm, glass, knife, animal and human bites; delayed healing with **diabetes, steroids, PAD, smoking**, retained foreign body, dead tissue.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Haemostasis -> inflammation (days 1-4, macrophage key) -> proliferation (type III collagen, granulation, epithelial seal 48 h) -> remodelling (type I collagen; **20% strength at 3 weeks, max 80%**).`],[`Key history`,`**Exact time of injury**, mechanism (high-pressure injection = emergency), foreign body, animal status, clenched-fist, numbness or weakness, **tetanus doses**, diabetes, anticoagulants, keloid, MLC.`],[`Key examination`,`ABC, pressure and elevation for bleeding; **distal neurovascular and tendon check before LA** (2-point discrimination under 6 mm; FDP and FDS tests); explore through full range of movement in a bloodless field.`],[`Investigations`,`Usually none; **X-ray two views** for glass or metal, **ultrasound for wood or thorn**; capillary glucose in slow or infected wounds; pus culture only if infected.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Irrigate 50-100 mL saline per cm** (20 mL syringe, 18 G); debride; close primarily if clean and **under 6 h (face up to 24 h)**; **lignocaine 3 mg/kg plain, 7 mg/kg with adrenaline** (1% = 10 mg/mL); bupivacaine 2 mg/kg.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Contaminated, puncture, crush, cat or human bite, cat III rabies - **delayed primary closure day 3-5**; bites - **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS 3-5 days** (allergy: doxycycline or co-trimoxazole plus metronidazole).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Needle at 90 degrees, equal bites wider at base; **simple interrupted**, vertical mattress (eversion), horizontal mattress, subcuticular; nylon 5-0/6-0 face, 4-0 hand, 3-0 trunk; tissue adhesive for low-tension child wounds.`],[`Special groups`,`Children - LAT gel 20-30 min or EMLA; calculate LA by weight; check story for NAI; reduce LA by a third in elderly, heart failure, liver disease.`],[`Complications`,`Infection, dehiscence, hypertrophic scar or keloid, missed tendon or nerve, retained FB, **LAST**, tetanus, rabies.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Tendon, nerve or vessel injury, open joint or fracture, clenched-fist injury, high-pressure injection, eyelid margin or vermilion, necrotising signs, established tetanus.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Bites and contaminated wounds at **24-48 h**; sutures out face 4-5 d, scalp and trunk 7-10, upper limb 10, palm, sole, joints 12-14, lower limb 10-14; complete rabies and Td dates.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Td 0.5 mL IM**: clean wound booster if 10 years or more, tetanus-prone if **5 years or more**; **TIG 250 IU** only if under 3 or unknown doses; rabies **Essen IM 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 or ID 2-2-2-0-2**, **hRIG 20 IU/kg or eRIG 40 IU/kg** into wounds (NRCP).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Irrigation, not antibiotics, prevents infection**; examine before injecting; never inject LA into pus; **lipid 20% 1.5 mL/kg** for LAST; wash bites 15 minutes.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Fresh laceration - decide closure in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`ABC, pressure, note time of injury`,detail:`Elevate; never clamp blindly`},{label:`Distal neurovascular and tendon check`,detail:`Before any local anaesthetic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Deep structure, open joint, fist bite?`,detail:`Yes - refer for exploration`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Anaesthetise, explore, irrigate, debride`,detail:`Lignocaine max 3 mg/kg (7 with adrenaline); 50-100 mL/cm`},{label:`Clean and under 6 h (face 24 h)?`,detail:`Yes - primary closure; no - delayed primary day 3-5`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Td, TIG, rabies PEP, antibiotic if indicated`,detail:`Bites: co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 3-5 d`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Tetanus and rabies prophylaxis at a glance`,columns:[`Situation`,`Vaccine`,`Immunoglobulin`],rows:[[`Clean wound, 3 or more Td doses`,`Booster if 10 years or more`,`No`],[`Tetanus-prone, 3 or more doses`,`Booster if 5 years or more`,`No`],[`Tetanus-prone, under 3 or unknown`,`Start full Td course`,`TIG 250 IU (500 IU if over 24 h)`],[`Rabies category II`,`ARV IM days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 or ID 0, 3, 7, 28`,`No`],[`Rabies category III`,`ARV as above`,`hRIG 20 IU/kg or eRIG 40 IU/kg into wounds`]]}]},"surgery-office-abscess-ssti":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`An **abscess** is a localised collection of pus walled off by a pyogenic membrane.`,`The rule is **ubi pus, ibi evacua**: where there is pus, let it out.`,`**Cellulitis** is spreading infection of deep dermis and fat with an indistinct edge.`,`**Erysipelas** involves upper dermis and lymphatics, with a sharp raised edge.`,`A **carbuncle** is necrosis of several adjacent hair follicles, classically on a diabetic's nape.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`IDSA first splits **purulent** (boil, carbuncle, abscess) from **non-purulent** (cellulitis, erysipelas).`,`Then grade **mild, moderate or severe**; moderate means systemic (SIRS) features.`,`Severe means failed oral therapy, immunosuppression, hypotension or deeper infection.`,`Purulent usually means staphylococcus and a knife; non-purulent means streptococcus and an antibiotic.`,`Necrotising infection: **type I polymicrobial**, type II group A strep, type III clostridial or Vibrio.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Staphylococcus enters through a breach such as a hair follicle or injection site.`,`2. Its **coagulase** lays down fibrin that walls the organisms off.`,`3. Neutrophils die and their enzymes liquefy tissue into **pus**.`,`4. The rim becomes an **avascular pyogenic membrane**, so antibiotics cannot reach the pus.`,`5. Streptococcal hyaluronidase and streptokinase let infection spread diffusely as cellulitis.`,`6. Local anaesthetic fails in pus because the **acid pH** keeps the drug ionised.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`A throbbing lump over 2-5 days suggests a collection; spreading redness suggests cellulitis.`,`**Pain out of proportion** to what you see is necrotising fasciitis until proved otherwise.`,`Look for the portal of entry: scratch, injection, foot fissure, tinea or ulcer.`,`Ask about fever, rigors and confusion, which move the grade to moderate or severe.`,`Ask about **diabetes**; an abscess or carbuncle is a common first presentation.`,`Recurrent boils or family clusters suggest **CA-MRSA carriage**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check vital signs, mental state and capillary glucose before touching the lump.`,`Test **fluctuation** in two planes at right angles; it may be absent when deep.`,`Feel and listen for **pulsation** near a major vessel; never incise an aneurysm.`,`**Mark the cellulitis margin** with a skin pen and write the date and time.`,`Necrotising signs: **woody oedema beyond the erythema**, bullae, skin anaesthesia and crepitus.`,`In every leg cellulitis, look between the toes for tinea and fissures.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Capillary glucose and HbA1c** in every abscess, carbuncle and recurrent infection.`,`**Bedside ultrasound** shows a hypoechoic cavity with swirling debris if pus is present.`,`Send pus for culture in recurrent, diabetic, facial, hand or failed cases.`,`**LRINEC 6 or more** raises suspicion of necrotising fasciitis; a low score never excludes it.`,`Never delay surgery for imaging when necrotising infection is suspected.`,`Aspirate a cold abscess for ZN stain and **CBNAAT**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Incision and drainage** is the treatment of an abscess; antibiotics alone cannot cure it.`,`Use a **field block** in healthy tissue 1-2 cm outside the red area, never into the cavity.`,`Incise at the most dependent point along Langer lines; deroof a large cavity.`,`**Hilton method**: blade through skin only, then open closed forceps to break loculi.`,`Irrigate, **pack loosely**, and review at 24-48 hours; it heals by secondary intention.`,`No antibiotic is needed for a simple drained abscess in a well patient.`,`For cellulitis, **elevate the limb**, mark the margin and give oral amoxicillin or cloxacillin.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add an antibiotic for surrounding cellulitis over 2 cm, systemic features, face or hand sites.`,`Suspected MRSA abscess: **co-trimoxazole DS or doxycycline**; clindamycin covers MRSA and streptococci.`,`Moderate cellulitis: IV cefazolin, ceftriaxone or clindamycin; switch to oral when improving.`,`Recurrent boils: **mupirocin nasal** for 5 days, chlorhexidine wash and treat household contacts.`,`Treat the portal of entry, such as **clotrimazole** for interdigital tinea.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Lactational breast abscess** under 5 cm: ultrasound-guided aspiration and continue breastfeeding.`,`**Perianal abscess**: drain in theatre near the verge; one third form a fistula.`,`**Felon**: drain early through a high lateral incision; fish-mouth incision is obsolete.`,`**Danger area of the face**: never squeeze; risk of cavernous sinus thrombosis.`,`A neonate with a skin abscess needs treatment as possible sepsis.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Necrotising fasciitis**: resuscitate, give the first antibiotic within 1 hour and refer for debridement.`,`Give at least **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 hours** if hypotensive or lactate 4 or more.`,`**Kanavel signs** mean flexor tenosynovitis and need same-day surgery.`,`**Fournier gangrene** is necrotising infection of perineum and scrotum in a diabetic man.`,`A sinus discharging for weeks needs a cause: foreign body, TB, osteomyelitis or cancer.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for abscess and skin infection`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`5-7 days`,`Take 1 hour before food`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`5-7 days`,`Safe in pregnancy; small penicillin cross-reaction`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS after food`,`5-7 days`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic jaundice`],[`Amoxicillin or penicillin V`,`500 mg TDS or 500 mg QID`,`5 days for cellulitis`,`Targets streptococci only`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg oral TDS-QID`,`5-7 days`,`C. difficile colitis; stop if diarrhoea`],[`Co-trimoxazole DS`,`1-2 tablets BD`,`5-7 days`,`Rash, SJS, hyperkalaemia; poor strep cover`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg BD`,`5-7 days`,`Oesophagitis; not in pregnancy or under 8`],[`Mupirocin 2% nasal`,`Both nostrils BD`,`5 days`,`Resistance with repeated courses`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam plus clindamycin`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly plus 600-900 mg IV`,`With debridement`,`For necrotising infection only`]]},{heading:`IDSA severity and plan`,columns:[`Grade`,`Purulent`,`Non-purulent`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Incision and drainage alone`,`Oral penicillin V, amoxicillin or cephalexin`],[`Moderate`,`Drain, culture, oral anti-MRSA antibiotic`,`IV cefazolin, ceftriaxone or clindamycin`],[`Severe`,`Drain plus IV vancomycin or linezolid`,`Emergency debridement plus broad IV antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Skin infections compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Erysipelas`,`Cellulitis`,`Abscess`,`Necrotising fasciitis`],rows:[[`Layer`,`Upper dermis, lymphatics`,`Deep dermis and fat`,`Pus cavity`,`Superficial fascia`],[`Organism`,`Strep pyogenes`,`Strep, Staph`,`Staph aureus`,`Polymicrobial or group A strep`],[`Clue`,`Sharp raised edge`,`Indistinct edge`,`Fluctuant, collection on ultrasound`,`Pain out of proportion, crepitus`],[`Treatment`,`Penicillin, elevation`,`Amoxicillin or cloxacillin`,`Incision and drainage`,`Debridement plus IV antibiotics`]]}],remember:[`Ubi pus, ibi evacua: no antibiotic crosses the pyogenic membrane.`,`Purulent means staph and a knife; non-purulent means strep and an antibiotic.`,`Hilton method for any abscess near nerves and vessels.`,`Never inject local anaesthetic into pus; block around it.`,`One in three perianal abscesses becomes a fistula-in-ano.`,`Pain out of proportion is the earliest sign of necrotising fasciitis.`,`Every carbuncle gets a glucose and HbA1c.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`SSC 2026: necrotising infection with sepsis - at least 30 mL/kg crystalloid in 3 h, balanced crystalloid preferred over 0.9% saline, antibiotic within 1 hour.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - abscess and SSTI`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Abscess** = walled-off pus within a pyogenic membrane; **cellulitis** = spreading deep dermal and fat infection; **erysipelas** = upper dermis and lymphatics with raised sharp edge.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IDSA 2014: purulent vs non-purulent**, then **mild, moderate (SIRS), severe**; NSTI type I polymicrobial, II group A strep, III clostridial or Vibrio.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**S. aureus (incl. CA-MRSA, PVL)** in purulent; **S. pyogenes** in non-purulent; diabetes, immunosuppression, tinea pedis, lymphoedema, injections.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Coagulase walls off -> neutrophil death liquefies tissue -> avascular pyogenic membrane -> **antibiotics cannot penetrate -> drainage cures**; strep enzymes spread diffusely.`],[`Key history`,`Tempo of spread, **pain out of proportion**, portal of entry, fever and rigors, diabetes, recurrence and family clusters, prior antibiotics, site-specific clues (lactation, perianal, face).`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals and glucose first; pointing, **Paget fluctuation in two planes**, **pulsatility (never incise an aneurysm)**, mark cellulitis margin with time; necrotising signs - woody oedema, bullae, anaesthesia, crepitus.`],[`Investigations`,`**Glucose and HbA1c**; bedside ultrasound for collection; pus culture in recurrent, diabetic, face or hand; **LRINEC 6 or more suspicious, 8 or more likely**; CBNAAT for cold abscess.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Incision, deroofing, Hilton method**, loose pack; no antibiotic for simple drained abscess; cellulitis - **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or penicillin V 500 mg QID 5 days**, elevate; cloxacillin or cephalexin **500 mg QID** if staph.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`MRSA - **co-trimoxazole DS 1-2 BD, doxycycline 100 mg BD, clindamycin 300-450 mg TDS**; IV cefazolin 1-2 g 8-hourly; severe - **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg** or linezolid 600 mg BD.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Field block 1-2 cm outside redness, No. 11 blade along Langer lines, cruciate deroofing, finger breaks loculi, culture from depth, review 24-48 h; **decolonise** with mupirocin nasal BD 5 d plus chlorhexidine wash.`],[`Special groups`,`**Lactational abscess - ultrasound-guided aspiration, continue breastfeeding**; perianal - drain close to verge, one third form fistula; danger-area face boil - never squeeze; neonate - treat as sepsis.`],[`Complications`,`Recurrence, sinus or fistula, lymphangitis, bacteraemia, cavernous sinus thrombosis, **necrotising fasciitis, Fournier gangrene**, toxic shock.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae, sepsis, Kanavel signs, periorbital swelling, Ludwig angina, deep neck, ischiorectal or breast abscess, pulsatile mass.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **24-48 h**; cellulitis margin should retreat by 48-72 h; chronic sinus - look for FB, TB, osteomyelitis, malignancy; recurrent boils - HbA1c, HIV, MRSA screen.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SSC 2026**: in NSTI with sepsis give **at least 30 mL/kg crystalloid, balanced preferred**, antibiotics within 1 h, then **radical debridement** (piperacillin-tazobactam plus clindamycin plus MRSA cover).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Ubi pus, ibi evacua**; purulent = staph and knife, non-purulent = strep and antibiotic; co-trimoxazole and doxycycline miss strep; never inject LA into pus.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Red swollen skin - knife or antibiotic?`,steps:[{label:`Vitals, glucose, sepsis screen`,detail:`SIRS = moderate; hypotension = severe`},{label:`Necrotising signs?`,detail:`Pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae, anaesthesia`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Collection on palpation or ultrasound?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Purulent vs non-purulent`},{label:`Purulent - incise, deroof, Hilton, culture`,detail:`Antibiotic only if cellulitis over 2 cm, systemic, high risk`},{label:`Non-purulent - antibiotic, elevate, mark edge`,detail:`Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS or penicillin V 5 days`},{label:`Review 24-48 h`,detail:`Failure: missed loculus, MRSA, NSTI`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Necrotising soft tissue infection - escalation`,steps:[{label:`Recognise and do not incise in clinic`,detail:`LRINEC 6 or more supports; low score never excludes`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Resuscitate`,detail:`At least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 h (SSC 2026)`},{label:`Antibiotic within 1 hour`,detail:`Pip-taz 4.5 g IV + clindamycin 600-900 mg IV + vancomycin/linezolid`},{label:`Phone surgeon - radical debridement`,detail:`Finger test; repeat look at 24-48 h`,tone:`good`}]}]},"surgery-office-diabetic-foot":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **diabetic foot ulcer** is a skin break below the ankles in a person with diabetes.`,`The triad is **neuropathy, ischaemia and infection**; infection is a complication, not the cause.`,`Ulcers are **neuropathic, ischaemic or neuroischaemic**; neuroischaemic is now the commonest.`,`Neuropathic ulcer: warm foot, pulses present, painless, plantar under callus.`,`Ischaemic ulcer: cold, pulseless, painful, on toe tips, heel or foot borders.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Meggitt-Wagner grade 0-5**: from at-risk intact skin to whole-foot gangrene.`,`**IWGDF/IDSA infection grade 1-4**: uninfected, mild, moderate and severe (with SIRS).`,`Add the suffix **O** when osteomyelitis is present.`,`**SINBAD score** of 3 or more means refer to a multidisciplinary foot team.`,`IWGDF risk 3 (previous ulcer, amputation or dialysis) needs review every 1-3 months.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Hyperglycaemia damages nerves, giving a **stocking-distribution** neuropathy.`,`2. **Sensory loss** removes pain, so the patient keeps walking on a wound.`,`3. **Motor loss** wastes small foot muscles, causing claw toes and prominent metatarsal heads.`,`4. **Autonomic loss** dries and cracks the skin, letting bacteria in.`,`5. **Callus** raises local pressure and an ulcer forms beneath it.`,`6. Distal tibial artery disease starves the wound of oxygen.`,`7. High glucose impairs neutrophils, so infection spreads with muted signs.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask how the ulcer began: new footwear, burn, barefoot walking or home callus cutting.`,`Numbness, burning or losing chappals unnoticed suggest **loss of protective sensation**.`,`**Rest pain** relieved by hanging the leg down means critical ischaemia.`,`**Sudden loss of glucose control** is often the first sign of deep infection.`,`A **previous ulcer or amputation** is the strongest predictor of a new ulcer.`,`A hot, swollen, little-painful foot after a minor twist suggests **Charcot foot**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Remove shoes and socks in every diabetic and look **between the toes**.`,`**10 g monofilament** at the hallux and 1st, 3rd and 5th metatarsal heads.`,`Failure to feel the monofilament at any one site means loss of protective sensation.`,`Feel dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses; both absent means PAD.`,`**ABI under 0.9** is PAD; over 1.3 is unreliable, so use toe pressure.`,`**Probe-to-bone** positive makes osteomyelitis highly likely.`,`Up to half of severe infections have **no fever** and a normal white count.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Glucose, HbA1c, ketones, creatinine and electrolytes in every infected foot.`,`**ESR over 70 mm/hour** strongly supports osteomyelitis.`,`Culture **deep tissue** from the debrided base, never a surface swab.`,`**Plain X-ray** for every deep or infected ulcer; repeat at 2-4 weeks.`,`**MRI** is the imaging of choice for osteomyelitis; bone biopsy is definitive.`,`**Arterial duplex** when pulses are absent or toe pressure is under 30 mmHg.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Offloading** is the single most important treatment for a plantar neuropathic ulcer.`,`The **total contact cast** (non-removable, knee-high) is the gold standard.`,`**Sharp debridement** of callus and slough to a bleeding base at every visit.`,`Never debride a **dry black eschar on an ischaemic foot**; paint it and refer.`,`Choose dressings by exudate; no dressing is superior for healing.`,`**No antibiotic for an uninfected ulcer**; colonisation is not infection.`,`Mild infection: oral **amoxicillin-clavulanate** for 1-2 weeks.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Moderate infection: often IV, such as ceftriaxone plus metronidazole, for 1-3 weeks.`,`Severe infection: **piperacillin-tazobactam** or meropenem, plus vancomycin if MRSA risk.`,`Osteomyelitis: **6 weeks** if infected bone is retained; 2-5 days if fully resected.`,`Critical ischaemia needs **revascularisation** before or with any minor amputation.`,`Give insulin during infection and pause SGLT2 inhibitors and metformin in sepsis.`,`Statin and antiplatelet for PAD; stop smoking.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Wet gangrene, plantar abscess or gas needs **same-day surgical drainage**.`,`Plantar pus tracks proximally along tendon sheaths instead of pointing.`,`**Acute Charcot foot**: immobilise in a cast now; do not treat as cellulitis.`,`An ulcer not 50% smaller after 4 weeks needs reassessment and possible biopsy.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Measure and photograph the ulcer at every visit.`,`Refer to a foot team for SINBAD 3 or more, exposed bone or PAD with ulcer.`,`Healed means **remission**: about 40% recur within 1 year.`,`Lifelong therapeutic footwear, worn over 80% of the day, prevents recurrence.`,`Annual monofilament, pulse and foot checks for every diabetic prevent amputations.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for the diabetic foot`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS or 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`1-2 weeks (mild)`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic jaundice`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`1-2 weeks (mild)`,`Gram-positive cover only`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg oral TDS; 600 mg IV`,`1-2 weeks (mild)`,`C. difficile colitis`],[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`1-2 g IV OD plus 500 mg 8-hourly`,`1-3 weeks (moderate)`,`Metronidazole: no alcohol`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`2-4 weeks (severe)`,`Renal dose adjustment`],[`Vancomycin or linezolid`,`15-20 mg/kg IV or 600 mg BD`,`With severe regimen if MRSA`,`Nephrotoxicity; linezolid low platelets`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750 mg oral OD`,`Up to 6 weeks for bone`,`Tendon rupture; exclude TB first`],[`Aspirin plus atorvastatin`,`75-150 mg OD plus 40-80 mg OD`,`Lifelong`,`Gastric bleeding; myalgia`],[`Pregabalin`,`75 mg BD, up to 300 mg/day`,`Long term`,`Dizziness, oedema; reduce in CKD`]]},{heading:`Meggitt-Wagner grades`,columns:[`Grade`,`Description`,`Management`],rows:[[`0`,`Intact skin, at-risk foot`,`Education, footwear, callus care`],[`1`,`Superficial ulcer`,`Debride, offload, dress`],[`2`,`Deep to tendon, capsule or bone`,`Debride, X-ray, deep culture`],[`3`,`Abscess, osteomyelitis or joint sepsis`,`Admit, drain, bone biopsy`],[`4`,`Forefoot or heel gangrene`,`Revascularise, toe or ray amputation`],[`5`,`Whole-foot gangrene`,`Below-knee amputation`]]},{heading:`IWGDF/IDSA infection grade`,columns:[`Grade`,`Clinical picture`,`Route`],rows:[[`1 Uninfected`,`No signs of infection`,`No antibiotic`],[`2 Mild`,`Erythema 0.5-2 cm, skin only`,`Oral, outpatient`],[`3 Moderate`,`Erythema over 2 cm or deeper tissue`,`Often IV initially`],[`4 Severe`,`Any infection with SIRS`,`Admit, IV, urgent surgery`]]}],remember:[`Offloading heals the ulcer, not the dressing.`,`Pare the callus before grading; the ulcer beneath is always bigger.`,`ABI over 1.3 is not normal; use toe pressure under 30 mmHg.`,`Probe-to-bone positive is close to diagnostic of osteomyelitis.`,`Deep tissue for culture, never a surface swab.`,`Never debride dry black eschar on an ischaemic foot.`,`Healed means remission: lifelong footwear and review.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - diabetic foot ulcer`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Full-thickness skin break **distal to the malleoli** in a person with diabetes; diabetes-related foot disease includes infection, ischaemia, Charcot and gangrene.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Meggitt-Wagner 0-5**; **IWGDF/IDSA infection 1-4 (+O for osteomyelitis)**; University of Texas (A-D); **SINBAD 0-6** (3 or more - foot team); IWGDF risk 0-3 for screening frequency.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Neuropathy + PAD + unperceived trauma**; previous ulcer or amputation (strongest), deformity, barefoot walking, ESRD, smoking, poor vision.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Polyol, AGEs -> sensory loss + motor claw toes + autonomic dry skin -> callus -> haematoma -> ulcer -> infection spreads along plantar compartments; **Monckeberg calcification falsely raises ABI**.`],[`Key history`,`Painless walking on ulcer, claudication and **rest pain**, fever or **sudden loss of glycaemic control**, footwear and habits, previous ulcer, SGLT2i, Charcot (hot swollen foot after twist).`],[`Key examination`,`Shoes off; **10 g monofilament** at hallux and 1st, 3rd, 5th MT heads; 128 Hz fork; pulses; **ABI under 0.9 PAD, over 1.3 unreliable - toe pressure under 30 mmHg**; **probe-to-bone**; describe ulcer after paring callus.`],[`Investigations`,`Glucose, HbA1c, ketones, creatinine; CBC, **ESR over 70** supports OM; **deep tissue culture, never swab**; foot X-ray; MRI if X-ray inconclusive; arterial duplex.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Offload (TCC gold standard)**, sharp debridement, moist dressing by exudate, glycaemic control; mild infection - **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS 1-2 weeks** (or cephalexin, clindamycin).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Moderate - IV co-amoxiclav 1.2 g or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole 1-3 weeks; severe - **pip-taz 4.5 g or meropenem + vancomycin/linezolid** with surgery; OM **6 weeks if bone retained, 2-5 days if resected**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Offloading ladder: non-removable knee-high TCC -> removable walker (made irremovable) -> ankle walker -> forefoot shoe -> felted foam; TCC contraindicated in infection, OM, critical ischaemia, heel ulcer.`],[`Special groups`,`CKD - renal dosing, avoid NSAIDs; ischaemic dry eschar - **do not debride, revascularise**; Charcot - TCC till temperature difference under 2 C.`],[`Complications`,`Abscess, wet gangrene, osteomyelitis, Charcot collapse, amputation, **5-year mortality worse than many cancers**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Crepitus or gas, wet gangrene, plantar abscess, SIRS, **rest pain or toe pressure under 30**, hot painless red foot (Charcot), exposed bone - same-day.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Measure area weekly; **not 50% smaller in 4 weeks - reassess**; healed = remission (recurrence 40% at 1 year); IWGDF risk 3 review every 1-3 months.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Annual foot screen in every diabetic (NP-NCD), education - inspect daily, no barefoot walking, nails straight; therapeutic footwear worn over 80% of day; statin, antiplatelet, stop smoking.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Offloading heals, not dressing**; pare callus before grading; probe-to-bone near-diagnostic; no antibiotic for uninfected ulcer.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Diabetic foot ulcer - OPD sequence`,steps:[{label:`Systemic toxicity, gas, wet gangrene?`,detail:`Yes - same-day admission and surgery`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Perfusion: pulses, ABI, toe pressure`,detail:`Toe pressure under 30 or ABI under 0.5 - vascular referral`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Infection grade (IWGDF 1-4) and probe-to-bone`,detail:`Deep tissue culture, X-ray; ESR over 70`},{label:`Debride callus and slough`,detail:`Not a dry eschar on an ischaemic foot`},{label:`Offload`,detail:`Total contact cast unless infected or ischaemic`},{label:`Weekly measurement`,detail:`50% smaller in 4 weeks = on track`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Antibiotic by IWGDF/IDSA grade`,steps:[{label:`Grade 1 uninfected`,detail:`No antibiotic`},{label:`Grade 2 mild - oral 1-2 weeks`,detail:`Co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS or cephalexin 500 mg QID or clindamycin`},{label:`Grade 3 moderate - IV then oral 1-3 weeks`,detail:`Co-amoxiclav 1.2 g IV or ceftriaxone + metronidazole; pip-taz if Pseudomonas`},{label:`Grade 4 severe - admit, surgery, 2-4 weeks`,detail:`Pip-taz or meropenem + vancomycin/linezolid`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Osteomyelitis`,detail:`6 weeks if bone retained; 2-5 days if all resected`,tone:`decision`}]}]},"surgery-office-minor-procedures":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Office surgery** means LA procedures on superficial, clinically benign lesions without theatre.`,`Typical lesions: lipoma, epidermoid cyst, ingrown toenail, skin tag, wart and diagnostic biopsy.`,`A **lipoma** is mature fat in a thin fibrous capsule.`,`An **epidermoid cyst** is keratin in a squamous-lined cyst, tethered to skin at a punctum.`,`Every specimen removed goes for **histopathology**, and every report is chased.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`The key decision is **office versus refer**.`,`Refer any lump **over 5 cm, deep to fascia, growing fast, painful or recurrent**.`,`Refer lumps in the parotid, anterior neck, groin, scrotum or midline scalp and spine.`,`**Heifetz stage 1** ingrown nail is treated conservatively; stages 2-3 need partial avulsion.`,`Biopsy types: **punch, shave, incisional and excisional**; every suspected melanoma is excisional.`,`**Glasgow 7-point score of 3 or more** means suspected-melanoma referral.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A lipoma shells out bloodlessly through the **avascular plane** around its capsule.`,`2. Retained capsule, cyst wall or nail matrix causes **recurrence**.`,`3. An epidermoid cyst forms when a hair follicle opening is blocked or implanted.`,`4. The ingrown nail edge provokes a foreign-body reaction and then granulation tissue.`,`5. The **lateral horns of the nail matrix** regrow the spike unless destroyed by phenol.`,`6. Liposarcoma arises de novo in deep sites; enucleation contaminates the compartment.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Rapid growth** over weeks or regrowth after excision suggests sarcoma.`,`Cheesy foul discharge through a pore means an epidermoid cyst.`,`For a mole, ask about change in size, shape or colour, itching and bleeding.`,`Before nail surgery ask about **diabetes, PAD and smoking**.`,`Ask about anticoagulants, pacemaker (bipolar only), allergy and keloid tendency.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Lipoma: lobulated, **positive slip sign**, skin freely pinchable, pseudofluctuant.`,`Epidermoid cyst: **punctum** in about half, tethered to skin, mobile over deep tissue.`,`Contract the underlying muscle; a subfascial lump becomes fixed or disappears.`,`Never incise a pulsatile lump or one with a **cough impulse**.`,`Check pedal pulses and monofilament before any toe surgery in a diabetic.`,`Paring a wart shows **black dots**; a corn shows a central core.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Histopathology of every specimen in **10% formalin**, about ten times its volume.`,`Ultrasound for a lump of uncertain nature or depth.`,`**MRI and sarcoma referral** before any biopsy of a large, deep or growing lump.`,`Image any **midline scalp or lumbosacral** swelling before excision.`,`Immunofluorescence specimens go in Michel medium or saline, never formalin.`,`No routine blood tests for minor procedures; INR only if on warfarin.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Take written consent **before** injecting local anaesthetic.`,`Mark a lipoma with the patient **sitting**, then operate lying down.`,`Lipoma: incise over the summit and deliver the capsule intact by blunt dissection.`,`Cyst: elliptical incision **including the punctum**; remove the wall intact.`,`Never excise an **infected cyst**; drain it and excise 6-8 weeks later.`,`Stage 1 ingrown nail: cut **straight across**, pack cotton under the corner, saline soaks.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Ingrown nail stage 2-3: **partial avulsion with phenol matricectomy**.`,`Use a ring block with **plain 1% lignocaine**, 2-3 mL each side of the toe.`,`Apply **80-88% phenol** for 30 seconds three times; recurrence under 5%.`,`Simple whole-nail avulsion alone recurs in **over 60%**.`,`Warts: **salicylic acid** nightly up to 12 weeks, or cryotherapy every 2-3 weeks.`,`Suspected melanoma: excision biopsy with **1-3 mm margin**; never shave or curette.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Haematoma and seroma** are commonest after lipoma excision; use a pressure dressing.`,`A **forgotten digital tourniquet** causes toe gangrene; note its time.`,`Unexpected malignancy on histology means recall and wide re-excision.`,`Wart plume carries viable HPV; wear an N95 mask and use a smoke evacuator.`,`Perioral tingling during injection is early **LA toxicity**; stop and get lipid ready.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review nail surgery at **48 hours**; phenolised groove drains for 2-4 weeks.`,`Remove sutures: face 5 days, trunk 7-10, lower limb 10-14.`,`Refer warts that are extensive, facial or not cleared after 3-4 cryotherapy sessions.`,`Refer a non-healing wart-like lesion in a scar or sinus as possible SCC.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for minor procedures`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Lignocaine 1%`,`Max 3 mg/kg plain; 7 mg/kg with adrenaline`,`Single procedure`,`LAST; plain LA if digit ischaemic`],[`Bupivacaine 0.25-0.5%`,`Max 2 mg/kg (150 mg)`,`Lasts 4-8 hours`,`Cardiotoxic; never IV`],[`EMLA cream`,`Under occlusion 60 min before`,`Single use`,`Methaemoglobinaemia in infants`],[`Salicylic acid 12-26%`,`Nightly after soaking and paring`,`Up to 12 weeks`,`Avoid face, genitals, diabetic feet`],[`Imiquimod 5% cream`,`Bedtime 3 times a week`,`Up to 16 weeks`,`Erosions; not in pregnancy`],[`Phenol 80-88%`,`30 seconds x 3 to nail matrix`,`Once`,`Chemical burn; protect skin`],[`Cephalexin or cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral QID`,`5 days (infected cyst or nail)`,`Not routine after clean surgery`],[`Paracetamol plus ibuprofen`,`1 g 6-hourly plus 400 mg TDS`,`2-3 days`,`Ibuprofen after food`]]},{heading:`Lipoma versus epidermoid cyst`,columns:[`Feature`,`Lipoma`,`Epidermoid cyst`],rows:[[`Contents`,`Mature fat`,`Cheesy keratin`],[`Skin`,`Freely pinchable`,`Tethered at punctum`],[`Sign`,`Slip sign, lobulated edge`,`Punctum in about half`],[`Sites`,`Back, shoulder, neck, arm`,`Scalp, face, back; never palms or soles`],[`Recurrence from`,`Retained capsule`,`Retained cyst wall`],[`Trap`,`Over 5 cm or deep may be sarcoma`,`Cock's peculiar tumour mimics SCC`]]},{heading:`Choosing a skin biopsy`,columns:[`Type`,`Use for`,`Avoid for`],rows:[[`Punch 3-4 mm`,`Rashes and inflammatory disease`,`Suspected melanoma`],[`Shave`,`Skin tag, seborrhoeic keratosis`,`Any possible melanoma`],[`Incisional`,`Large lesion, panniculitis`,`Small excisable lesion`],[`Excisional`,`Every suspected melanoma, 1-3 mm margin`,`Large lesions needing reconstruction`]]}],remember:[`Slip sign and pinchable skin is a lipoma; punctum and tethered skin is a cyst.`,`Recurrence means residual tissue: capsule, cyst wall or nail matrix.`,`Never excise an infected cyst; drain first, excise 6-8 weeks later.`,`Phenol 30 seconds three times: ingrown nail recurrence under 5%.`,`Never shave a possible melanoma; Breslow thickness is lost.`,`Over 5 cm, deep or growing: MRI and sarcoma referral, never enucleate.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - office minor procedures`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Procedures under LA on superficial clinically benign lesions - **lipoma, epidermoid cyst, ingrown toenail, skin tag, wart, skin biopsy**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Office vs refer** (over 5 cm, deep to fascia, fast-growing, painful, recurrent - sarcoma pathway); biopsy types punch, shave, incisional, excisional; **Heifetz 1-3** ingrown toenail; **Glasgow 7-point** (3 or more refer).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Lipoma - mature fat; epidermoid cyst - occluded follicle infundibulum (never palms or soles); ingrown nail - curved cutting, tight shoes; warts - HPV.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Retained capsule, cyst wall or nail-matrix horn -> **recurrence**; melanoma depth (**Breslow**) lost by shave or curettage.`],[`Key history`,`Duration and growth, pain, discharge, multiplicity, pigmented lesion change, diabetes or PAD before nail surgery, anticoagulants, pacemaker (bipolar only), keloid.`],[`Key examination`,`**Slip sign + pinchable skin = lipoma**; **punctum + tethered skin = cyst**; fluctuation, transillumination, deep fixity; ABCDE and ugly duckling; pedal pulses before toe surgery.`],[`Investigations`,`**Histopathology of every specimen** in 10% formalin (10x volume); MRI and sarcoma referral before biopsy of suspicious lumps; imaging before midline scalp or lumbosacral lesions.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Lipoma - incise two-thirds diameter, deliver capsule in avascular plane; cyst - elliptical incision with punctum, intact wall; **infected cyst - drain, excise 6-8 weeks later**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Ingrown nail stage 2-3 - **partial avulsion with phenol 80-88%, 30 s x 3** (recurrence under 5%); Zadik for recurrent; warts - salicylic acid 12-26% up to 12 weeks, then cryotherapy every 2-3 weeks x 3-4.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Ring block 2-3 mL plain lignocaine each side, glove tourniquet with time recorded; punch 3-4 mm, lift with needle; suspected melanoma - **excision with 1-3 mm margin**, never shave.`],[`Special groups`,`Diabetic or ischaemic toe - check perfusion, plain LA; children - EMLA 60 min; pregnancy - TCA for genital warts, no imiquimod or podophyllotoxin.`],[`Complications`,`Haematoma, seroma, infection, recurrence, **forgotten digital tourniquet**, keloid, hypopigmentation after cryotherapy, unexpected malignancy on histology.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Lump over 5 cm or deep, **Glasgow 3 or more**, ulcer with everted edge (SCC, Marjolin), midline lumbosacral or scalp lesion, pulsatile or cough impulse, parotid, neck, groin lumps.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Nail at 48 h then weekly; sutures per site; **chase every histology report**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Recurrence means residual tissue; never excise an infected cyst; never shave a possible melanoma; wart plume carries HPV - N95 and evacuator.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Lump on the skin - office or refer?`,steps:[{label:`Size, depth, growth, pain, recurrence`,detail:`Over 5 cm, deep or growing - MRI and sarcoma pathway`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Pigmented lesion?`,detail:`Glasgow 3 or more - urgent referral; excise 1-3 mm margin, never shave`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Site check`,detail:`Parotid, anterior neck, groin, midline scalp or spine - image or refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Infected?`,detail:`Drain now, excise after 6-8 weeks`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Consent, mark sitting, LA dose in mg`,detail:`Lignocaine with adrenaline max 7 mg/kg`},{label:`Excise completely, histology, chase report`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Ingrown toenail by Heifetz stage`,steps:[{label:`Stage 1 - pain, erythema`,detail:`Cut straight, cotton or floss under corner, warm soaks`},{label:`Stage 2 - infection, discharge`,detail:`Partial avulsion + phenol 80-88% 30 s x 3; cephalexin if cellulitis`},{label:`Stage 3 - granulation, fold hypertrophy`,detail:`Partial avulsion, curette granulation, phenol matricectomy`},{label:`Recurrent or deformed nail`,detail:`Zadik total matrix excision`,tone:`decision`}]}]},"surgery-office-hernia":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **hernia** is protrusion of a viscus through an abnormal opening in its containing wall.`,`It has three parts: the **sac**, its coverings and its contents.`,`Constriction occurs at the **neck** of the sac.`,`Inguinal hernias are **70-75%** of all hernias; indirect is commonest in both sexes.`,`**Divarication of recti** has no fascial defect and is not a hernia.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`By site: inguinal, femoral, umbilical, paraumbilical, epigastric and incisional.`,`By state: **reducible, irreducible, obstructed and strangulated**.`,`**Indirect** hernia lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep ring.`,`**Direct** hernia bulges medial to them, through Hesselbach triangle.`,`**Richter hernia** traps part of the bowel wall and strangulates without obstructing.`,`EHS groin classification: primary or recurrent; lateral, medial or femoral; defect size 1-3.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A weak wall: patent processus vaginalis, ageing, obesity, scars or smoking.`,`2. Raised pressure: chronic cough, constipation, prostatic straining or ascites.`,`3. Contents push into the sac through the defect.`,`4. A tight neck first blocks **venous and lymphatic return**.`,`5. Contents swell, the neck tightens further and **arterial inflow stops**.`,`6. Gangrene and perforation follow within hours.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask whether the lump **disappears on lying down** and returns on coughing.`,`Sudden constant severe pain with a lump that no longer goes back means **strangulation**.`,`Colicky pain, vomiting and constipation suggest an obstructed hernia.`,`Ask about cough, constipation and **prostatic symptoms**, which cause recurrence.`,`A new hernia with weight loss or rectal bleeding needs colorectal cancer excluded.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine **standing first, then lying**, both groins, scrotum and abdomen.`,`**Neck of an inguinal hernia is above and medial** to the pubic tubercle.`,`**Neck of a femoral hernia is below and lateral** to the pubic tubercle.`,`If you cannot get above a scrotal lump, it is coming from the abdomen.`,`**Deep ring occlusion test** controls an indirect hernia but not a direct one.`,`Tense, tender, irreducible lump with **no cough impulse** means strangulation.`,`Do a rectal examination for prostate size and rectal tumour.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Diagnosis of a groin hernia is **clinical**; no imaging is needed if typical.`,`**Dynamic ultrasound** for an uncertain lump or groin pain without a lump.`,`**CT** for obturator, Spigelian or complex incisional hernias.`,`Look for the cause: PSA, chest X-ray, sputum for TB, colonoscopy if needed.`,`In strangulation, imaging must never delay surgical referral.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Every femoral hernia** is referred for surgery at diagnosis.`,`Symptomatic inguinal hernias are referred for elective **tension-free mesh repair**.`,`Watchful waiting suits a minimally symptomatic inguinal hernia in a man.`,`About **70%** of watched hernias cross over to surgery within 5-10 years.`,`Treat cough, constipation and prostatism; stop smoking 4 weeks before surgery.`,`Infant umbilical hernia: reassure, no strapping or coins; most close by 4-5 years.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Lichtenstein mesh repair** is the gold-standard open repair, possible under local anaesthesia.`,`**Laparoscopic repair (TEP or TAPP)** for bilateral, recurrent-after-open and female hernias.`,`Children need **herniotomy alone**, with no mesh.`,`Inguinal hernias in women are repaired because many are actually femoral.`,`A truss is obsolete except in patients unfit for any operation.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Never attempt taxis** of a tender hernia; gangrenous bowel may be reduced.`,`Resuscitate a strangulated hernia: nil by mouth, IV fluids, NG tube and antibiotics.`,`Richter hernia: toxic patient with a femoral lump but no vomiting.`,`Thin elderly woman with obstruction and medial thigh pain suggests **obturator hernia**.`,`After repair: chronic groin pain in 10-12% and recurrence in 1-5%.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer the same hour for a strangulated, obstructed or tender irreducible hernia.`,`Refer within days for femoral hernias and inguinal hernias in infants or girls.`,`After repair, sedentary work in 1-2 weeks and heavy lifting at about 4-6 weeks.`,`Repair a recurrence by the approach not used before.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs around hernia care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`1 g oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day`,`3-7 days after repair`,`Liver toxicity in overdose`],[`Ibuprofen or diclofenac`,`400 mg TDS or 50 mg BD-TDS`,`3-5 days`,`Gastritis, AKI; avoid in CKD`],[`Lignocaine and bupivacaine`,`Max 3 mg/kg and 2 mg/kg`,`During repair`,`Calculate combined dose; LAST`],[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV within 60 min of incision`,`Single dose`,`Only high-risk patients or settings`],[`Lactulose or PEG`,`15-20 mL BD or 17 g OD`,`4-6 weeks`,`Bloating`],[`Tamsulosin`,`0.4 mg OD after a meal`,`Long term`,`Postural hypotension, floppy iris`],[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`2 g IV plus 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`Emergency, before transfer`,`Metronidazole: no alcohol`]]},{heading:`Indirect, direct and femoral hernia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Indirect`,`Direct`,`Femoral`],rows:[[`Typical patient`,`Any age, children, young men`,`Older men`,`Elderly multiparous women`],[`Epigastric vessels`,`Lateral`,`Medial`,`Below inguinal ligament`],[`Neck and pubic tubercle`,`Above and medial`,`Above and medial`,`Below and lateral`],[`Shape`,`Pear-shaped, into scrotum`,`Globular, rarely scrotal`,`Small upper-thigh lump`],[`Strangulation risk`,`Moderate`,`Low`,`Highest`]]},{heading:`Hernia by state`,columns:[`State`,`Features`,`Action`],rows:[[`Reducible`,`Soft, cough impulse, goes back lying down`,`Elective repair`],[`Irreducible`,`Non-tender lump that will not reduce`,`Urgent referral`],[`Obstructed`,`Colic, vomiting, distension, constipation`,`Emergency: NBM, IV fluids, NG tube`],[`Strangulated`,`Tense, tender, no cough impulse, toxic`,`Emergency surgery; never taxis`]]}],remember:[`Judge the neck, not the fundus, against the pubic tubercle.`,`Loss of cough impulse in a tender lump means strangulation.`,`Richter hernia strangulates without obstructing.`,`Never taxis a tender hernia.`,`Every femoral hernia gets surgery; 40% present as emergencies.`,`Children get herniotomy alone; adults get tension-free mesh.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`HerniaSurge 2023: antibiotic prophylaxis for open mesh repair is risk-tiered - not for average-risk patients in low-infection settings, suggested in high-risk patients, recommended for all in high-infection-risk environments.`,`HerniaSurge 2023: with expertise, laparo-endoscopic (TEP/TAPP) repair is also an option for primary unilateral hernia - quicker recovery, less chronic pain.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - hernia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Protrusion of a viscus or part of a viscus through an abnormal opening in the wall of its containing cavity; **sac, coverings, contents**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By site (indirect commonest in both sexes), by **state - reducible, irreducible, obstructed, strangulated**; **EHS groin: P/R, L/M/F, size 1-3**; Richter, Littre, Maydl, Amyand, de Garengeot, sliding.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Patent processus vaginalis, ageing, smoking, collagen defect + raised pressure - **chronic cough, constipation, prostatism, ascites, pregnancy, heavy lifting**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Tight neck -> venous and lymphatic block -> oedema -> arterial block -> **gangrene, perforation**; cough impulse lost when neck occluded; Richter strangulates without obstructing.`],[`Key history`,`Lump reducing on lying, dragging pain, **sudden constant pain + irreducible = strangulation**, obstruction symptoms, cough, LUTS, bowel change in elderly, previous surgery, fitness.`],[`Key examination`,`Stand then lie; **neck relative to pubic tubercle - inguinal above and medial, femoral below and lateral**; get above it?; deep ring occlusion; Zieman; signs of strangulation; DRE, chest, other groin.`],[`Investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis; dynamic ultrasound for occult or uncertain lump; CT for obturator, Spigelian, complex incisional; look for cause (PSA, chest X-ray, colonoscopy if indicated).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Every femoral hernia - operate** (40% present as emergency); symptomatic inguinal - **Lichtenstein tension-free mesh** (day case, LA possible); minimally symptomatic men - watchful waiting acceptable.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**TEP/TAPP** for bilateral, recurrent after open, women, and **primary unilateral where expertise exists (HerniaSurge 2023)**; tissue repair (Shouldice) if mesh unavailable or contaminated.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Lichtenstein: polypropylene mesh ~15 x 7.5 cm overlapping pubic tubercle 2 cm, sutured to shelving edge and internal oblique, tails around cord; ilioinguinal block 2 cm medial to ASIS; **truss obsolete**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child - herniotomy alone**, prompt (preterm, girls sooner); infant umbilical hernia closes by 4-5 years - no strapping; women - laparoscopic (many are femoral); divarication is not a hernia.`],[`Complications`,`Strangulation, obstruction, reduction en masse; post-op haematoma, mesh infection, ischaemic orchitis, **chronic groin pain 10-12%**, recurrence 1-5%.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Tense tender irreducible lump, **no cough impulse**, obstruction - same hour, **never taxis**; any femoral hernia; elderly woman with obstruction and Howship-Romberg (obturator).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Walk same day, sedentary work 1-2 weeks, heavy lifting 4-6 weeks; report fever, scrotal swelling, retention, testicular pain.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**HerniaSurge 2023**: antibiotic prophylaxis tiered by patient risk and setting (**recommended for all in high-infection-risk environments** - cefazolin 2 g within 60 min); laparo-endoscopic repair for primary unilateral hernia where expertise exists.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Judge the neck not the fundus; loss of cough impulse in a tender lump = strangulation; Richter does not obstruct; children herniotomy, adults mesh.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Groin lump - decide the urgency`,steps:[{label:`Tender, tense, irreducible, no cough impulse?`,detail:`Strangulation - NBM, IV fluids, NG tube, refer now; never taxis`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Neck relative to pubic tubercle`,detail:`Below and lateral = femoral - urgent surgery`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Can you get above it?`,detail:`No - inguinoscrotal hernia; yes - scrotal lesion`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Symptomatic inguinal hernia`,detail:`Elective mesh repair; treat cough, constipation, prostatism`},{label:`Minimally symptomatic man`,detail:`Watchful waiting with safety-net (most cross over)`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Choosing the repair`,columns:[`Patient`,`Repair`,`Note`],rows:[[`Child`,`Herniotomy only`,`Wall normal; preterm and girls sooner`],[`Adult primary unilateral`,`Lichtenstein or TEP/TAPP`,`Laparo-endoscopic if expertise (HerniaSurge 2023)`],[`Bilateral or recurrent after open`,`TEP/TAPP`,`Opposite approach to the previous one`],[`Woman`,`Laparo-endoscopic`,`Detects occult femoral hernia`],[`Strangulated femoral`,`McEvedy (high) or laparoscopic`,`Allows bowel resection`]]}]},"surgery-office-breast-lump":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **breast lump** is a discrete swelling distinct from surrounding nodularity and the other breast.`,`The question is always **benign or malignant**, answered only by triple assessment.`,`**Triple assessment** is clinical examination, imaging and core biopsy, each scored 1-5.`,`A lump is benign only when **all three arms are concordantly benign**.`,`Sensitivity exceeds **99%** when all three arms agree.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Benign disease is grouped as **ANDI**: development, cyclical change and involution.`,`**BI-RADS 3** is probably benign (2% or less); 4 is suspicious and needs biopsy.`,`Core biopsy **B3** (uncertain potential) must be excised.`,`**B5a** is in situ and **B5b** is invasive carcinoma.`,`TNM: T1 up to 2 cm, T2 over 2-5 cm, T3 over 5 cm, T4 chest wall or skin.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Most lesions arise in the **terminal duct lobular unit**.`,`2. Cumulative unopposed oestrogen exposure raises cancer risk.`,`3. **BRCA1 and BRCA2** mutations give a lifetime risk up to 70%.`,`4. Invasive cancer breaks the basement membrane and becomes hard and fixed.`,`5. Tumour shortens **Cooper ligaments**, causing skin dimpling.`,`6. Blocked dermal lymphatics cause **peau d'orange**.`,`7. About **75% of lymph drains to the axilla**, the rest to internal mammary nodes.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`A painless, hard, steadily growing lump suggests carcinoma.`,`A lump that swells premenstrually and settles suggests ANDI; re-examine after the period.`,`**Spontaneous single-duct blood-stained discharge** suggests papilloma or carcinoma.`,`Ask about **family history** of breast or ovarian cancer and age at diagnosis.`,`Ask about menarche, menopause, parity, breastfeeding, HRT and chest irradiation.`,`Trauma suggests fat necrosis but never excuses a biopsy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Use a female chaperone and examine the **normal breast first**.`,`Inspect with arms by the side, **arms raised** and **hands pressed on hips**.`,`Palpate with the **flat of the fingers**, including the axillary tail.`,`Test fixity to skin, to pectoralis major (hand on hip) and to chest wall.`,`Examine all axillary groups and the **supraclavicular fossa**.`,`Paget disease starts **on the nipple**; eczema starts on the areola.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Ultrasound under 40 years**, and in pregnancy and lactation at any age.`,`**Mammography plus ultrasound at 40 and over**.`,`**Core needle biopsy** (14-16 G) gives invasion, grade and ER, PR, HER2 status.`,`A normal mammogram never overrules a suspicious lump; 10-15% of cancers are occult.`,`Cyst aspiration is benign only if the lump disappears, fluid is not bloody and it does not refill.`,`Bilateral milky discharge: pregnancy test, **prolactin and TSH**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Fibroadenoma** under 3 cm in a woman under 30, concordantly benign: observe.`,`Cyclical mastalgia: reassure, supportive bra and **topical diclofenac 1% gel**.`,`Evening primrose oil is **no longer recommended**.`,`Symptomatic simple cyst: aspirate and review in 4-6 weeks.`,`**Lactational mastitis**: continue breastfeeding and give cloxacillin for 10-14 days.`,`Periductal mastitis in a smoker: amoxicillin-clavulanate and stop smoking.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Phyllodes tumour** needs wide excision with at least a 1 cm margin.`,`Single-duct bloody discharge from papilloma needs **microdochectomy**.`,`Cancer: breast conservation plus radiotherapy equals mastectomy for survival.`,`**Sentinel node biopsy** for a clinically node-negative axilla.`,`ER-positive: **tamoxifen** premenopausal, **aromatase inhibitor** postmenopausal, for 5-10 years.`,`HER2-positive: **trastuzumab** for 1 year with 3-monthly echocardiography.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`A lump in pregnancy is assessed by ultrasound and core biopsy, not ignored.`,`**Inflammatory carcinoma** mimics mastitis; biopsy if not settling in 1-2 weeks.`,`**Tuberculous mastitis** shows a chronic lump with sinus; core biopsy and CBNAAT.`,`Male breast: concentric disc is **gynaecomastia**; eccentric hard lump may be cancer.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Urgent referral for an unexplained lump at **30 or over**.`,`Urgent referral for unilateral nipple change at 50 or over, or suspicious skin change.`,`India NP-NCD: **clinical breast examination every 5 years** for women 30-65.`,`After cancer: annual mammography, bone health and lymphoedema care.`,`Postmenopausal bleeding on tamoxifen must be reported at once.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in breast disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Diclofenac 1% gel`,`Apply TDS to tender area`,`Painful phase`,`Local irritation`],[`Cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral QID, before food`,`10-14 days (mastitis)`,`Safe in breastfeeding`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`7-10 days (periductal)`,`Diarrhoea`],[`Cabergoline`,`0.25 mg twice weekly, up to 1 mg`,`Until prolactin normal`,`Nausea; valvulopathy at high dose`],[`Tamoxifen`,`20 mg oral OD`,`5-10 years`,`VTE, endometrial cancer, hot flushes`],[`Letrozole or anastrozole`,`2.5 mg or 1 mg oral OD`,`5-10 years`,`Arthralgia, osteoporosis`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV every 6 months`,`3 years`,`Jaw osteonecrosis; check calcium`],[`Trastuzumab`,`IV or SC every 3 weeks`,`1 year`,`Cardiotoxicity`]]},{heading:`Triple assessment`,columns:[`Arm`,`What is done`,`Score`],rows:[[`Clinical`,`Inspection, palpation of breast, axilla, neck`,`P1-P5`],[`Imaging`,`Ultrasound under 40; mammogram plus ultrasound 40+`,`U1-U5 or M1-M5`],[`Pathology`,`Image-guided core biopsy`,`B1-B5`],[`Concordance`,`Compare all three`,`Any discordance means excision`]]},{heading:`Common breast lumps`,columns:[`Lump`,`Typical age`,`Feel`,`Plan`],rows:[[`Fibroadenoma`,`15-30`,`Smooth, highly mobile`,`Observe if small and concordant`],[`Cyst`,`35-55`,`Smooth, tense, sudden`,`Aspirate`],[`Carcinoma`,`Over 40`,`Hard, irregular, tethered`,`Urgent referral`],[`Fat necrosis`,`Any, after trauma`,`Hard, mimics cancer`,`Biopsy`],[`Phyllodes`,`40-50`,`Large, rapidly growing`,`Wide excision, 1 cm margin`]]}],remember:[`Benign only if all three arms are benign.`,`Ultrasound under 40; mammography plus ultrasound at 40 and over.`,`Core biopsy beats FNAC: it shows invasion and receptor status.`,`A normal mammogram never overrules a suspicious lump.`,`Paget disease starts on the nipple; eczema spares it.`,`Cyst rules: disappears, not bloody, does not refill.`,`Tamoxifen before menopause, aromatase inhibitor after.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - breast lump and triple assessment`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Discrete palpable swelling distinct from surrounding nodularity; benign only when **clinical, imaging and pathology are all concordantly benign**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Triple assessment P1-5, U/M1-5 (BI-RADS 0-6), B1-5 (B3 excise, B5a in situ, B5b invasive)**; ANDI for benign disease; AJCC 8e TNM (T4b peau d'orange, T4d inflammatory).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Unopposed oestrogen exposure (early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, HRT, obesity, alcohol), **BRCA1/2**, chest irradiation, atypical hyperplasia.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`TDLU origin; Cooper ligament shortening -> dimpling; dermal lymphatic block -> **peau d'orange**; 75% drainage to axilla; Paget = intraepidermal spread starting on the nipple.`],[`Key history`,`Duration and growth, relation to cycle, pain, **spontaneous single-duct bloody discharge**, nipple change, axillary lump, metastatic symptoms, family history, drugs (galactorrhoea, gynaecomastia).`],[`Key examination`,`Chaperone; inspect arms down, raised, hands on hips; flat-hand palpation of quadrants and tail; fixity to skin and pectoralis; axilla and supraclavicular fossa; **score P1-P5**.`],[`Investigations`,`**Ultrasound under 40 and in pregnancy; mammography + ultrasound at 40 and over**; **image-guided 14 G core biopsy** (ER, PR, HER2); cyst aspiration rules; prolactin and TSH for milky discharge.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Concordant benign = discharge with plan; **any discordance = excision biopsy**; a normal mammogram never overrules a suspicious lump (10-15% occult).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Fibroadenoma under 3 cm, under 30 - observe; cyst - aspirate; cyclical mastalgia - supportive bra, **topical diclofenac**; lactational mastitis - continue feeding, **cloxacillin 500 mg QID 10-14 days**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Cancer (MDT): breast-conserving surgery + RT or mastectomy, SLNB; **tamoxifen 20 mg premenopausal, letrozole 2.5 mg or anastrozole 1 mg postmenopausal 5-10 y**; trastuzumab for HER2 (echo 3-monthly).`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - ultrasound and core biopsy; male - eccentric hard lump = cancer (BRCA2, Klinefelter); **TB mastitis** in India - core, CBNAAT, ATT.`],[`Complications`,`Delayed diagnosis, lymphoedema, tamoxifen VTE and endometrial cancer, AI osteoporosis, trastuzumab cardiotoxicity, febrile neutropenia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Unexplained lump at **30 or over**, nipple change or unilateral discharge at 50 or over, skin change any age, bloody single-duct discharge, nipple eczema starting on nipple, non-settling mastitis.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Fibroadenoma 3-6 months; after cancer **annual mammography**, endocrine adherence, bone health (DEXA, calcium, vitamin D, zoledronic acid).`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NP-NCD: clinical breast examination every 5 years at 30-65** at Ayushman Arogya Mandir; breast awareness; breastfeeding, activity, avoid alcohol.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Benign only if all three benign; core beats FNAC; B3 is excised; Paget starts on the nipple; fat necrosis mimics cancer.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Triple assessment in one line each`,steps:[{label:`Clinical examination - score P1-P5`,detail:`P4-P5 = cancer pathway whatever imaging shows`},{label:`Imaging by age`,detail:`Under 40 or pregnant: ultrasound; 40+: mammography + ultrasound`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Core biopsy (14 G, 3-4 cores)`,detail:`Gives invasion, grade, ER, PR, HER2`},{label:`Concordant?`,detail:`Discordant = excision biopsy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Concordant benign - reassure and review`,tone:`good`},{label:`Concordant malignant - MDT`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Nipple discharge - what it means`,columns:[`Discharge`,`Likely cause`,`Action`],rows:[[`Bilateral multi-duct milky`,`Galactorrhoea`,`Pregnancy test, prolactin, TSH, drug review`],[`Multi-duct green-brown`,`Duct ectasia`,`Triple assessment, stop smoking`],[`Spontaneous single-duct bloody`,`Papilloma or DCIS/carcinoma`,`Imaging, biopsy, microdochectomy`],[`Purulent, lactating`,`Mastitis or abscess`,`Continue feeding, cloxacillin, aspirate abscess`]]}]},"surgery-office-catheter-ngt-injection":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Every procedure follows one sequence: indication, consent, chaperone, asepsis, size, anatomy, confirmation, record.`,`Catheter indications are therapeutic, diagnostic and perioperative.`,`**French size = circumference in mm**; divide by 3 for diameter.`,`Use **14-16 Fr** for adults, 8-10 Fr for children, **20-24 Fr three-way** for clots.`,`Injection routes: intradermal 0.1 mL, subcutaneous 1-2 mL, IM up to 3-4 mL ventrogluteal.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`The one absolute contraindication to urethral catheter is **suspected urethral injury**.`,`Signs of urethral injury: blood at the meatus, butterfly haematoma, high-riding prostate.`,`Catheters: short-term under 14 days, long-term silicone up to 12 weeks, or intermittent self-catheterisation.`,`NG tubes: wide-bore **Ryle 14-18 Fr** for drainage; fine-bore 8-12 Fr for feeding.`,`No nasal tube with **base-of-skull fracture**; use the orogastric route.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The male urethra is 18-20 cm with a fixed subpubic and a mobile prepubic curve.`,`2. Holding the penis upright at 90 degrees straightens the prepubic curve.`,`3. Forcing against the external sphincter creates a **false passage**.`,`4. Bacteria ascend along the catheter and form a **biofilm** within days.`,`5. CAUTI risk rises **3-7% per catheter-day**.`,`6. Gastric aspirate is acidic, so **pH 5.5 or less** confirms the stomach.`,`7. The **sciatic nerve** lies near the dorsogluteal site but not the ventrogluteal site.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Retention: time since last void, prostatic symptoms and drugs such as anticholinergics.`,`Ask about pelvic trauma, stricture and previous difficult catheterisation.`,`Ask about **latex** allergy; use silicone catheters if present.`,`Before NG: head injury, CSF leak, facial fracture, corrosive ingestion or varices.`,`Before injection: check the seven rights, allergy and anticoagulant use.`,`Ask whether the injection is needed at all; oral drugs often suffice.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Retention: a dull suprapubic mass you cannot get below.`,`Look for urethral injury signs before any catheter.`,`Before NG: check nasal patency, **Battle sign, raccoon eyes** and gag reflex.`,`**Ventrogluteal landmark**: palm on greater trochanter, index finger on ASIS; inject in the V.`,`Deltoid site: 2-3 finger-breadths below the acromion, avoiding axillary and radial nerves.`,`Anaphylaxis has hypotension, wheeze and urticaria; vasovagal has bradycardia and quick recovery.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bladder scan** before catheterising uncertain retention.`,`Creatinine and electrolytes after relieving chronic retention.`,`**Retrograde urethrogram** first if urethral injury is suspected.`,`Culture catheter urine only if symptomatic; never routinely dipstick catheter urine.`,`NG position: **aspirate pH 5.5 or less**, or X-ray if no aspirate or pH higher.`,`Never confirm NG position by the **whoosh test**, litmus or bubbling.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Instil **10-20 mL lignocaine 2% gel** in men and wait 3-5 minutes.`,`**Advance to the hilt and see urine** before inflating the balloon.`,`Inflate with **10 mL sterile water**; stop if painful.`,`**Replace the foreskin** after catheterisation to prevent paraphimosis.`,`NG: measure **NEX** (nose, earlobe, xiphisternum), pass along the nasal floor, advance with swallows.`,`IM: 23 G needle at 90 degrees into the ventrogluteal site; do not massage.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Start **tamsulosin 0.4 mg** 2-3 days before a trial without catheter.`,`Treat symptomatic CAUTI for **7 days** (non-fluoroquinolone), after changing an old catheter.`,`Do not treat asymptomatic bacteriuria or give prophylaxis at catheter change.`,`Use the **Z-track** technique for irritant IM drugs.`,`Consider **PEG** if tube feeding is needed beyond 4-6 weeks.`,`Give thiamine before feeding a malnourished patient to prevent refeeding syndrome.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Pain on balloon inflation means it is in the urethra; deflate at once.`,`**Post-obstructive diuresis** over 200 mL/hour needs electrolytes and partial fluid replacement.`,`Coughing or voice change while passing NG means the airway; withdraw.`,`Foot drop after a gluteal injection means **sciatic nerve injury**.`,`**Anaphylaxis**: lie flat, **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** into the thigh, repeat every 5 minutes.`,`Needle-stick: wash, do not squeeze, and start **TLD within 2 hours**, for 28 days.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review every indwelling catheter's need daily; early removal prevents CAUTI.`,`Refer to urology for failed catheterisation, false passage or failed TWOC twice.`,`Needle-stick follow-up tests at 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months.`,`Observe for 30 minutes after vaccines with adrenaline ready.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for catheter, NG tube and injection work`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Lignocaine 2% gel`,`10-20 mL men, 5-6 mL women, urethral`,`Wait 3-5 min`,`Absorption from torn urethra`],[`Tamsulosin`,`0.4 mg oral OD after a meal`,`From 2-3 days before TWOC`,`Postural hypotension, floppy iris`],[`Cephalexin or ceftriaxone`,`500 mg oral 12-hourly or 1-2 g IV OD`,`7 days for CAUTI`,`Only if symptomatic`],[`Oxybutynin`,`2.5-5 mg oral BD-TDS`,`For catheter spasm`,`Confusion in elderly; avoid in dementia`],[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mg IM thigh (6-12 y 0.3 mg; under 6 y 0.15 mg)`,`Repeat every 5 min`,`Never undiluted IV bolus`],[`TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)`,`1 tablet oral OD`,`28 days`,`Start within 2 h, not after 72 h`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin`,`0.06 mL/kg IM (about 500 IU)`,`Within 24 h, plus vaccine`,`Different site from vaccine`],[`Thiamine`,`200-300 mg oral daily`,`First 10 days of refeeding`,`Give before feeds start`]]},{heading:`Injection routes`,columns:[`Route`,`Site`,`Needle and angle`,`Volume`],rows:[[`IM ventrogluteal`,`Centre of V on the hip`,`23 G, 90 degrees`,`Up to 3-4 mL`],[`IM deltoid`,`2-3 fingers below acromion`,`23-25 G, 90 degrees`,`1-2 mL`],[`IM vastus lateralis`,`Anterolateral mid-thigh`,`23-25 G, 90 degrees`,`1 mL infant`],[`Subcutaneous`,`Abdomen, thigh, back of arm`,`4-6 mm pen needle, 90 degrees`,`1-2 mL`],[`Intradermal`,`Forearm; left deltoid for BCG`,`26-27 G, bevel up, 10-15 degrees`,`0.1 mL`]]},{heading:`Catheters and tubes`,columns:[`Device`,`Use`,`Key error to avoid`],rows:[[`Two-way Foley 14-16 Fr`,`Routine drainage and retention`,`Inflating in the urethra`],[`Three-way Foley 20-24 Fr`,`Clot retention and irrigation`,`Using a two-way that blocks`],[`Coude tip`,`Large prostate`,`Rotating the tip`],[`Suprapubic catheter`,`Urethral injury or failed urethral route`,`Inserting through a lower abdominal scar`],[`Ryle tube 14-18 Fr`,`Decompression and drainage`,`Confirming by auscultation`]]}],remember:[`Advance to the hilt and see urine before inflating; replace the foreskin.`,`Inflate with sterile water; deflate passively, never cut the catheter.`,`French size divided by 3 equals diameter in mm.`,`NG position: pH 5.5 or less, or X-ray, never the whoosh test.`,`Ventrogluteal is the safest IM site; vaccines never in the buttock.`,`Anaphylaxis: adrenaline 0.5 mg IM thigh first; antihistamines are not first line.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`IDSA cUTI 2025: symptomatic CAUTI (a complicated UTI) - once improving, 7 days of a non-fluoroquinolone or 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone, not the old 10-14 days.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - catheter, NG tube and injections`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Three bedside procedures with one sequence - **indication, contraindication, consent and chaperone, asepsis, correct size, anatomy, objective confirmation, documentation**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Catheter **Fr = circumference mm (diameter = Fr/3)** - 14-16 adult, 20-24 three-way for clots; short vs long-term vs **intermittent self-catheterisation**; NG wide-bore Ryle/Salem vs fine-bore feeding; ID, SC, IM, IV routes.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Male urethra 18-20 cm, two curves, membranous narrowest after meatus; **CAUTI risk 3-7% per catheter-day** via biofilm; gastric pH under 5.5; sciatic nerve near dorsogluteal site.`],[`Key history`,`Retention history and drugs, **urethral injury, stricture, prostate surgery**, latex allergy; base-of-skull fracture, corrosives; anticoagulants before IM; needle-stick details and source status.`],[`Key examination`,`Palpable bladder; **blood at meatus, butterfly haematoma, high-riding prostate - no catheter**; Battle sign or raccoon eyes - no nasal tube; ventrogluteal V landmark.`],[`Investigations`,`Bladder scan; creatinine after chronic retention; **NG aspirate pH 5.5 or less, else X-ray**; urine culture from sampling port only if symptomatic; PEP baseline serology.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Male: lignocaine gel 10-20 mL wait 3-5 min, penis at 90 degrees, **advance to hilt and see urine before inflating 10 mL water, replace foreskin**; NG: NEX measure, floor of nose, swallow; IM: 23 G 25-38 mm at 90 degrees, no routine aspiration.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Remove catheter early, closed drainage, bag below bladder; **tamsulosin 0.4 mg 2-3 days before TWOC**; no antibiotic for asymptomatic bacteriuria or catheter change.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Symptomatic CAUTI**: change catheter if over 2 weeks, culture, then **7 days (5-7 days if fluoroquinolone) once improving** (IDSA 2025); failed catheter or false passage - urology, suprapubic.`],[`Special groups`,`Anaphylaxis doses: **adrenaline 1:1000 IM thigh 0.5 mg adult and over 12, 0.3 mg 6-12 y, 0.15 mg under 6 y**, repeat every 5 min; anticoagulated - avoid IM; malnourished NG feeding - thiamine, start 10 kcal/kg/day.`],[`Complications`,`False passage, paraphimosis, post-obstructive diuresis (over 200 mL/h), CAUTI, encrustation; NG in airway; sciatic injury, injection abscess; needle-stick BBV (HIV 0.3%, HCV 1.8%, HBV 6-30%).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pain on balloon inflation, no urine; cough, voice change or desaturation passing NG; pH over 5.5; foot drop after gluteal injection; urticaria, stridor, hypotension.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Daily review of catheter indication; NG external length before every use; PEP adherence and serology at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months; observe 30 min after vaccines.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Auto-disable syringes, no recapping, BMW rules (sharps white, plastics red, soiled yellow, glass blue); **PEP TLD 28 days within 2 h (max 72 h)** (NACO); HBIG plus vaccine if non-immune.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IDSA cUTI 2025**: CAUTI is a complicated UTI; once improving give **7 days of a non-fluoroquinolone or 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone** instead of 10-14 days.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Urine before inflating; sterile water not saline; NG pH 5.5 or X-ray, never whoosh test; ventrogluteal safest; adrenaline IM thigh first.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Fever in a catheterised patient`,steps:[{label:`Symptoms beyond cloudy urine?`,detail:`Fever, loin pain, rigors, delirium - else no antibiotic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Catheter in over 2 weeks?`,detail:`Replace, then culture from the new catheter`},{label:`Septic?`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV, admit`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Treat by culture`,detail:`7 days non-fluoroquinolone or 5-7 days fluoroquinolone once improving (IDSA 2025)`},{label:`Remove catheter as early as possible`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Anaphylaxis after an injection`,steps:[{label:`Airway, breathing, circulation, skin`,detail:`Distinguish from vasovagal (slow pulse, recovers lying flat)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Adrenaline 1:1000 IM anterolateral thigh`,detail:`0.5 mg adult; 0.3 mg 6-12 y; 0.15 mg under 6 y`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Lie flat, legs up; oxygen, IV fluids`,detail:`500-1000 mL adult, 10 mL/kg child`},{label:`No improvement in 5 min - repeat`,detail:`After 2 doses - refractory pathway, adrenaline infusion`},{label:`Observe 6-12 h, auto-injector, allergy referral`,tone:`good`}]}]},"surgery-office-preop-postop":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Pre-operative assessment weighs **physiological reserve** against the size of the operation.`,`Its aims: optimise disease, quantify risk, plan drugs and fasting, and support consent.`,`**Fitness is always relative** to the planned operation.`,`Tests are **directed, not routine**; ASA I minor surgery needs none.`,`After surgery, the post-operative day of a symptom narrows its cause.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ASA I-VI**, with suffix E for emergency, which roughly doubles or trebles risk.`,`Surgical risk: low under 1%, intermediate 1-5%, high over 5% cardiac events.`,`**RCRI (Lee)** gives one point each for six risk factors.`,`RCRI 2 or more with poor functional capacity prompts cardiac assessment.`,`Climbing **two flights of stairs = 4 METs** or more; DASI 34 or less flags poor reserve.`,`**Clavien-Dindo I-V** grades post-operative complications.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Surgery triggers a **stress response** with cortisol, catecholamines and insulin resistance.`,`2. ADH and aldosterone retain salt and water for 2-3 days.`,`3. Cytokines cause fever in the first 48 hours and a CRP peak at 48-72 hours.`,`4. **Virchow triad** is complete after surgery, so VTE risk is high.`,`5. Anastomoses are weakest on **days 5-7**, when leaks appear.`,`6. ACE inhibitors cause refractory hypotension; SGLT2 inhibitors cause euglycaemic ketoacidosis.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about angina, breathlessness, previous MI and **coronary stent type and date**.`,`Ask **exercise tolerance** in flights of stairs.`,`Screen for sleep apnoea with **STOP-BANG**; 5 or more is high risk.`,`Take a full drug history, including herbal remedies, SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists.`,`Ask about difficult intubation and **family history of malignant hyperthermia**.`,`Ask about escort and a responsible adult at home for day surgery.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Defer elective surgery if BP is **180/110 mmHg or more**.`,`An ejection systolic murmur to the carotids suggests **aortic stenosis**; get an echo.`,`Assess the airway: mouth opening, **Mallampati class** and thyromental distance.`,`Screen frailty with the **Clinical Frailty Scale**; 5 or more is frail.`,`After surgery, examine the wound with the dressing off, the abdomen, chest and calves.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Full blood count before major surgery; treat **pre-operative anaemia with iron**.`,`**HbA1c under 8.5%** before elective surgery in diabetics.`,`**ECG** at 65 and over for intermediate or high-risk surgery or cardiac disease.`,`Echocardiography only for an unexplained murmur, dyspnoea or suspected heart failure.`,`Coagulation tests only with liver disease, anticoagulants or a bleeding history.`,`Post-op: rising CRP after day 3 suggests a complication; CT for leak.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Continue beta-blockers, statins and aspirin** with a sip of water.`,`**Omit ACE inhibitors, ARBs and metformin** on the day of surgery.`,`**Stop SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days before**; ertugliflozin 4 days.`,`**Fasting 6-4-2**: solids 6 hours, breast milk 4 hours, clear fluids 2 hours.`,`Give **cefazolin 2 g IV within 60 minutes** before incision; stop within 24 hours.`,`Stop smoking ideally 4-8 weeks before surgery.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Warfarin**: stop 5 days before; bridge only for very high thrombotic risk.`,`**DOACs**: stop 24 hours (low bleed risk) or 48 hours (high) before; no bridging.`,`Insulin: give **80% of basal dose**; use IV insulin if more than one meal is missed.`,`**Stress-dose hydrocortisone 100 mg IV** for patients on prednisolone 5 mg or more over 4 weeks.`,`**Enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily** until mobile; 28 days after major abdominopelvic cancer surgery.`,`Defer elective surgery 60 days after MI and 6 months after a drug-eluting stent.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fever timetable: **wind, water, walking, wound, wonder drugs**.`,`**Persistent tachycardia** on day 5-7 after bowel surgery is a leak until proved otherwise.`,`**Pink serosanguinous discharge** around day 7-10 warns of burst abdomen.`,`Cover a burst abdomen with warm saline packs and refer as an emergency.`,`Sudden breathlessness and hypoxia in the first weeks suggest **pulmonary embolism**.`,`A dusky, crepitant wound under 48 hours means necrotising infection.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Postpone for cardiac opinion with unstable angina, decompensated failure or severe aortic stenosis.`,`Never stop dual antiplatelet therapy after a recent stent without the cardiologist.`,`At the first post-op visit, reconcile medicines and restart held drugs on time.`,`Chase the operation note and the **histopathology report**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Peri-operative drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV (3 g if 120 kg+) within 60 min`,`Stop by 24 hours`,`Clindamycin 900 mg if allergic`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC OD`,`Until mobile; 28 days after cancer surgery`,`Bleeding, HIT; 20 mg if CrCl under 30`],[`Warfarin`,`Stop 5 days before`,`Restart evening of surgery`,`Vitamin K 1-2 mg oral if INR 1.5+`],[`Apixaban or rivaroxaban`,`Stop 24-48 h before`,`Restart 24-72 h after`,`No bridging needed`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Stop 3 days before`,`Restart when eating normally`,`Euglycaemic ketoacidosis`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`100 mg IV at induction, then 200 mg/24 h`,`Until eating; then double oral dose`,`Hyperglycaemia`],[`Dexamethasone plus ondansetron`,`4-8 mg IV plus 4 mg IV`,`Single doses for PONV`,`Hyperglycaemia; QT prolongation`],[`Paracetamol plus ibuprofen`,`1 g 6-hourly plus 400 mg 8-hourly`,`3-5 days`,`Laxative with any opioid`]]},{heading:`ASA physical status`,columns:[`Class`,`Meaning`,`Example`],rows:[[`I`,`Healthy`,`Fit non-smoker`],[`II`,`Mild systemic disease`,`Controlled hypertension, smoker`],[`III`,`Severe disease, functional limit`,`Poorly controlled diabetes, COPD`],[`IV`,`Constant threat to life`,`Recent MI, sepsis`],[`V`,`Moribund`,`Ruptured aortic aneurysm`]]},{heading:`Post-operative fever timetable`,columns:[`Day`,`Cause`,`Clue`],rows:[[`0-2 (wind)`,`Atelectasis, inflammatory response`,`Reduced basal breath sounds`],[`3-5 (water)`,`Urinary infection`,`Catheter in place`],[`4-6 (walking)`,`DVT or PE`,`Calf swelling, tachycardia`],[`5-7 (wound)`,`Wound infection, leak, collection`,`Wound redness, tachycardia`],[`After 7 (wonder drugs)`,`Drug fever, line sepsis, C. difficile`,`Diarrhoea, cannula site`]]}],remember:[`Fitness is relative to the operation.`,`Two flights of stairs means 4 METs or more.`,`Fasting 6-4-2.`,`Omit ACE inhibitor and metformin on the day; stop SGLT2 inhibitor 3 days before.`,`Antibiotic prophylaxis: one dose within 60 minutes, stop within 24 hours.`,`Wind, water, walking, wound, wonder drugs.`,`Persistent tachycardia after bowel surgery is a leak until proved otherwise.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - pre-operative fitness and post-operative care`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Structured judgement of **physiological reserve and comorbidity against the magnitude of the operation**, with drug, fasting and prophylaxis plans and informed consent.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ASA I-VI (+E)**; ESC surgical risk low under 1%, intermediate 1-5%, high over 5%; **RCRI 6 items** (0 = 0.4%, 3+ = 11%); **4 METs (two flights) or DASI over 34**; Clavien-Dindo; CDC SSI.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Stress response -> cortisol, catecholamines -> **hyperglycaemia, catabolism**; ADH -> water retention, hyponatraemia; Virchow triad complete; leak day 5-7 in lag phase.`],[`Key history`,`Angina, dyspnoea, **stent type and date**, exercise tolerance, STOP-BANG, recent chest infection, bleeding history, anaesthetic and MH family history, all drugs incl. **SGLT2i, GLP-1 RA**, herbals, pregnancy.`],[`Key examination`,`BP (proceed if primary-care BP under 160/100; **clinic 180/110 or more - defer**), murmur of **aortic stenosis**, heart failure, airway (Mallampati, thyromental under 6 cm), frailty (CFS 5+), post-op wound and calves.`],[`Investigations`,`**Directed, not routine** (NICE NG45); Hb (treat Hb under 13 with iron), creatinine, **HbA1c under 8.5%**, ECG at 65+ or cardiac disease, NT-proBNP in high risk, INR only if indicated.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Continue beta-blocker, statin, aspirin**; omit **ACEi/ARB** on the day, **metformin if more than one meal missed**; **SGLT2i stop 3 days (ertugliflozin 4)**; fasting **6 h solids, 4 h breast milk, 2 h clear**; cefazolin 2 g within 60 min, stop within 24 h.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Warfarin stop 5 days** (bridge only high risk), **DOAC 24-48 h**, clopidogrel 5 d, ticagrelor 3-5 d, prasugrel 7 d; **insulin 80% basal**, VRIII if more than one meal missed; GLP-1 RA - 24 h clear liquids, hold weekly agent 1 week if high risk.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`WHO checklist, ERAS (carbohydrate drink, opioid-sparing analgesia, early feeding and mobilisation), VTE - stockings or IPC + **enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily**, 28 days after abdominopelvic cancer surgery.`],[`Special groups`,`Steroid users (prednisolone 5 mg+ over 4 weeks) - **hydrocortisone 100 mg at induction then 200 mg/24 h**; recent MI 60 days, DES 6 months, stroke 3-9 months - defer elective surgery; frail - delirium prevention.`],[`Complications`,`**Fever timetable - wind (0-2), water (3-5), walking (4-6), wound (5-7), wonder drugs (7+)**; leak, burst abdomen, PE, retention, ileus, delirium, haemorrhage.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Persistent tachycardia day 5-7 (leak), **pink serosanguinous discharge** (dehiscence), sudden dyspnoea (PE), early necrotising wound, euglycaemic DKA on SGLT2i.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Medicine reconciliation (restart ACEi, metformin, anticoagulants on stated day), wound check, suture removal by site, **chase histology**, resume chronic disease targets.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Fitness is relative to the operation; tests are directed; one antibiotic dose within 60 min; persistent tachycardia after bowel surgery is a leak until proved otherwise.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Is this patient fit for elective surgery?`,steps:[{label:`Active cardiac condition?`,detail:`Unstable angina, decompensated HF, arrhythmia, severe AS - postpone`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Surgical risk low?`,detail:`Yes - proceed with directed tests`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Functional capacity 4 METs or DASI over 34?`,detail:`Yes - proceed`,tone:`decision`},{label:`RCRI 2 or more with poor capacity`,detail:`NT-proBNP/troponin, cardiology opinion`},{label:`Optimise and write drug plan`,detail:`BP, glucose (HbA1c under 8.5%), anaemia, stop/continue list`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Peri-operative drug plan`,columns:[`Drug`,`Before surgery`,`Restart`],rows:[[`Beta-blocker, statin, aspirin`,`Continue`,`Continue`],[`ACEi or ARB`,`Omit morning of surgery`,`When eating and creatinine stable`],[`Metformin`,`Omit if more than one meal missed (48 h after contrast or AKI)`,`When eating and eGFR stable`],[`Sulfonylurea`,`Omit on the day`,`When eating normally`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Stop 3 days before (ertugliflozin 4)`,`When eating and drinking normally`],[`Warfarin`,`Stop 5 days; INR under 1.5`,`Evening of surgery or next day`],[`Apixaban, rivaroxaban`,`Stop 24 h (low) or 48 h (high bleed risk)`,`24-72 h after`],[`Prednisolone 5 mg+ over 4 weeks`,`Usual dose + hydrocortisone 100 mg at induction`,`Double oral dose 48 h to 1 week`]]}]},"surgery-office-procedural-sedation-ketamine":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Procedural sedation** relieves anxiety and pain while the patient keeps airway, breathing and circulation.`,`It complements local anaesthesia; it never replaces it.`,`The sedationist must be able to **rescue from one level deeper** than intended.`,`**Ketamine** gives analgesia, sedation and amnesia with preserved airway, breathing and BP.`,`Office sedation is for **ASA I and II** patients only.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**ASA continuum**: minimal, moderate, deep and general anaesthesia.`,`Moderate: purposeful response to voice or light touch; airway needs no help.`,`Deep: purposeful response only to repeated or painful stimulus.`,`**Withdrawal from pain is not purposeful**; it means deep sedation.`,`**Dissociative sedation** with ketamine sits outside the continuum and is all-or-none.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Ketamine is a **non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist**.`,`2. It separates the thalamocortical system from the limbic system, causing **dissociation**.`,`3. The patient looks awake with eyes open and nystagmus, but feels no pain.`,`4. Sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and BP; it is also a bronchodilator.`,`5. **Hypersalivation** on a reactive larynx explains laryngospasm (about 1 in 300).`,`6. Midazolam plus fentanyl is **synergistic for respiratory depression**.`,`7. **Capnography** detects apnoea before pulse oximetry does.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Take an **AMPLE** history, including last oral intake time and type.`,`Ask about heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension and aortic aneurysm.`,`Ask about snoring, sleep apnoea and recent chest infection (laryngospasm risk).`,`**Schizophrenia** is an absolute contraindication to ketamine.`,`**Age under 3 months** is also an absolute contraindication.`,`Confirm a responsible adult escort and supervision for 24 hours.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record baseline pulse, BP, respiratory rate, SpO2 and weight for dosing.`,`Assess the airway: mouth opening, **Mallampati class** and thyromental distance.`,`In children, check tonsil size, nasal obstruction and wheeze.`,`During sedation, record vital signs and depth **every 5 minutes**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**No routine tests** are needed in ASA I-II patients.`,`Check capillary glucose in diabetics and long-fasted children.`,`Monitoring is the essential test: oximetry, BP and **waveform capnography**.`,`Do not delay an emergency procedure for tests that will not change management.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Take written consent and warn parents about the **vacant stare and nystagmus**.`,`Have **two trained people**: the operator and a dedicated sedationist.`,`Do a **SOAPME** check: suction, oxygen, airway, pharmacy, monitors, equipment.`,`**Ketamine IV**: child 1.5-2 mg/kg, adult 1-1.5 mg/kg over 30-60 seconds.`,`**Ketamine IM**: 4-5 mg/kg into the thigh or deltoid.`,`Always combine with good local anaesthesia to reduce the sedative dose.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Top up ketamine IV with 0.5-1 mg/kg every 5-10 minutes if needed.`,`Sub-dissociative analgesia: **0.1-0.3 mg/kg** by slow infusion, never a rapid bolus.`,`Give **midazolam 1-2 mg IV** for adult emergence reactions; not routinely in children.`,`Routine anticholinergic is no longer advised; use only for troublesome secretions.`,`Reverse opioids with **naloxone** and benzodiazepines with **flumazenil**.`,`There is **no antidote** for ketamine or propofol.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Laryngospasm**: stop, suction, oxygen, jaw thrust with **Larson manoeuvre**, then CPAP.`,`If laryngospasm persists, give **succinylcholine 0.5-1 mg/kg IV** with atropine in children.`,`Apnoea usually follows a **rapid IV push**; ventilate with bag and mask.`,`Vomiting happens in 8-12%, usually in recovery; turn to the side and suction.`,`Emergence reactions affect 10-30% of adults but only 1-2% of children.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Discharge when back to baseline and stable for **30 minutes after the last dose**.`,`No driving, machinery, alcohol or legal decisions for 24 hours.`,`Refer ASA III or above, difficult airway, severe OSA or infants to an anaesthetist.`,`Keep ketamine locked with a stock register; it is a controlled drug in India.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for procedural sedation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ketamine IV`,`Child 1.5-2 mg/kg, adult 1-1.5 mg/kg over 30-60 s`,`Effect 5-10 min`,`Laryngospasm, vomiting, emergence reactions`],[`Ketamine IM`,`4-5 mg/kg; top-up 2-2.5 mg/kg`,`Effect 15-30 min`,`More vomiting, longer recovery`],[`Ketamine analgesic`,`0.1-0.3 mg/kg IV over 10-15 min`,`Single dose or infusion`,`Dysphoria if pushed fast`],[`Midazolam`,`0.5-1 mg IV steps to 2.5-5 mg adult`,`Titrate`,`Respiratory depression with opioids`],[`Fentanyl`,`1-1.5 microgram/kg IV`,`Repeat 0.5 microgram/kg`,`Respiratory depression, chest rigidity`],[`Naloxone`,`0.1-0.4 mg IV (child 0.01 mg/kg)`,`Repeat every 2-3 min`,`Short-acting; observe 2 hours`],[`Flumazenil`,`0.2 mg IV, then 0.1 mg/min to 1 mg`,`Titrate`,`Seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users`],[`Ondansetron`,`0.1-0.15 mg/kg IV, max 4 mg`,`Single dose`,`QT prolongation`],[`Succinylcholine`,`0.5-1 mg/kg IV or 3-4 mg/kg IM`,`For laryngospasm`,`Hyperkalaemia, bradycardia`]]},{heading:`Depth of sedation`,columns:[`Level`,`Response`,`Airway`,`Breathing`],rows:[[`Minimal`,`Normal to voice`,`Unaffected`,`Unaffected`],[`Moderate`,`Purposeful to voice or light touch`,`No intervention`,`Adequate`],[`Deep`,`Purposeful only to pain`,`May need support`,`May be inadequate`],[`General anaesthesia`,`Unrousable`,`Often needs support`,`Often inadequate`],[`Dissociative (ketamine)`,`Trance, eyes open`,`Reflexes preserved`,`Usually preserved`]]},{heading:`Laryngospasm rescue steps`,columns:[`Step`,`Action`],rows:[[`1`,`Stop the procedure and suction the pharynx`],[`2`,`100% oxygen with jaw thrust and Larson manoeuvre`],[`3`,`Bag-valve-mask with CPAP`],[`4`,`Succinylcholine 0.5-1 mg/kg IV with atropine in children`],[`5`,`Ventilate or intubate and transfer`]]}],remember:[`Be able to rescue from one level deeper than intended.`,`Withdrawal from pain is not a purposeful response.`,`Give IV ketamine over 30-60 seconds; a rapid push causes apnoea.`,`Ketamine: IV 1-2 mg/kg, IM 4-5 mg/kg.`,`Absolute contraindications: under 3 months and schizophrenia.`,`SOAPME before every sedation: suction, oxygen, airway, pharmacy, monitors, equipment.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - procedural sedation and ketamine`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Drug-induced depression of consciousness allowing a procedure while **airway, breathing and circulation are maintained**; must be able to **rescue one level deeper than intended**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ASA continuum: minimal, moderate (purposeful response to voice or light touch), deep, GA**; withdrawal from pain is not purposeful; **dissociative (ketamine) sits outside**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Ketamine = **non-competitive NMDA antagonist** -> thalamocortical-limbic dissociation; sympathomimetic (BP and HR up), bronchodilator, reflexes preserved but not protective; hypersalivation -> laryngospasm (0.3%).`],[`Key history`,`AMPLE, cardiorespiratory disease, OSA and snoring, recent URI, **psychosis**, age, fasting (not absolute in emergencies), escort.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals and weight, **ASA I-II only in office**, airway (Mallampati, mouth opening, thyromental), tonsils, chest; monitor SpO2, **capnography**, HR, BP every 5 min.`],[`Investigations`,`None routine; glucose in diabetics; pregnancy test if relevant; equipment check (SOAPME) is the essential test.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Ketamine IV 1-1.5 mg/kg adult, 1.5-2 mg/kg child over 30-60 s**, top-up 0.5-1 mg/kg; **IM 4-5 mg/kg**, top-up 2-2.5 mg/kg; sub-dissociative analgesia 0.1-0.3 mg/kg slow infusion.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Midazolam 0.5-1 mg IV steps (to 2.5-5 mg) or 0.03-0.05 mg/kg with ketamine in adults; fentanyl 1-1.5 microgram/kg; reversal naloxone 0.1-0.4 mg, flumazenil 0.2 mg; **no antidote for ketamine or propofol**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Two trained people; suction, oxygen, airways, BVM, SGA, capnography, defibrillator, drugs; lateral recovery in quiet room; discharge 30 min after last dose with escort, no driving 24 h.`],[`Special groups`,`**Absolute contraindications: under 3 months, schizophrenia**; relative - pharyngeal procedures, URI, cardiovascular disease, raised ICP/IOP (now minor), porphyria, thyroid; children - no routine midazolam or anticholinergic.`],[`Complications`,`**Laryngospasm**, apnoea with rapid push, vomiting 8-12% (recovery), emergence reactions (adults 10-30%), hypertension, hypersalivation.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`ASA III+, difficult airway, severe OSA or big tonsils, under 1 year, frail elderly, full stomach elective, deep sedation needed - anaesthetist.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Record drug, dose, depth every 5 min, adverse events, recovery; audit desaturation, laryngospasm, vomiting, rescue.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Ketamine is an NDPS-controlled drug - locked storage and register.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Rescue one level deeper; give IV ketamine over 30-60 s; capnography detects apnoea before oximetry; warn parents about nystagmus and vacant stare.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Laryngospasm during ketamine sedation`,steps:[{label:`Stop procedure, suction, 100% oxygen`,detail:`Stridor or silent obstruction with falling SpO2`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Jaw thrust + Larson manoeuvre`,detail:`Firm pressure in the notch behind the earlobe`},{label:`Bag-mask with CPAP/PEEP`},{label:`Succinylcholine`,detail:`0.5-1 mg/kg IV or 3-4 mg/kg IM, with atropine in children`},{label:`Ventilate or intubate, transfer`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Ketamine dose by route`,columns:[`Route or use`,`Dose`,`Onset and duration`],rows:[[`IV dissociation - adult`,`1-1.5 mg/kg over 30-60 s; top-up 0.5 mg/kg`,`30 s; 5-10 min`],[`IV dissociation - child`,`1.5-2 mg/kg; top-up 0.5-1 mg/kg`,`30 s; 5-10 min`],[`IM dissociation`,`4-5 mg/kg; top-up 2-2.5 mg/kg`,`3-5 min; 15-30 min`],[`Sub-dissociative analgesia`,`0.1-0.3 mg/kg IV over 10-15 min`,`Minutes`],[`Intranasal`,`Analgesia 0.5-1 mg/kg; sedation 3-6 mg/kg`,`Unreliable`]]}]},"surgery-office-spinal-anaesthesia":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Spinal anaesthesia** is local anaesthetic injected into lumbar CSF below the end of the cord.`,`Standard drug: **0.5% hyperbaric bupivacaine 10-15 mg**.`,`It gives a rapid, dense block for surgery **below the umbilicus**.`,`The anaesthetist gives it; the family physician assesses, counsels and manages after-care.`,`The **spinal cord ends at L1-L2** in adults and L3 in neonates.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Neuraxial blocks: **spinal, epidural, combined spinal-epidural and caudal**.`,`By baricity: **hyperbaric** sinks, isobaric stays, hypobaric rises.`,`Dermatomes: **T4 nipple, T6 xiphisternum, T10 umbilicus**, L1 inguinal ligament.`,`Caesarean section needs a **T4** block; perianal surgery needs only S2-S5 (saddle).`,`Motor block is graded on the **modified Bromage scale 0-3**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The drug blocks sodium channels in nerve roots bathed in CSF.`,`2. **Sympathetic fibres block first**, 2-6 segments above the sensory level.`,`3. Venous pooling lowers preload, causing **hypotension**.`,`4. A block reaching T1-T4 cardioaccelerator fibres causes **bradycardia**.`,`5. The diaphragm (C3-C5) is spared, so breathing is preserved in a normal spinal.`,`6. A CSF leak through the dural hole makes the brain sag, causing **PDPH**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Patient refusal** is an absolute contraindication; correct fears of paralysis.`,`Record the exact **time of the last antithrombotic dose**.`,`Ask about bleeding tendency, liver disease and family bleeding disorders.`,`Screen for **severe aortic stenosis**, HOCM and mitral stenosis (fixed cardiac output).`,`Ask about raised intracranial pressure symptoms and infection over the back.`,`Chronic backache and previous simple back surgery are **not contraindications**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Uncorrected **hypovolaemia or shock** is an absolute contraindication.`,`A resting pulse under 60 predicts bradycardia after the block.`,`Listen for an aortic stenosis murmur; get an echo if present.`,`Inspect the back for infection, scoliosis and scars.`,`Record a **baseline leg neurological examination** before the block.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`No routine coagulation screen in a healthy patient with a negative bleeding history.`,`Check **platelets** in pre-eclampsia, sepsis or liver disease; 70,000 or more is generally acceptable.`,`**INR 1.4 or less** is needed after stopping warfarin.`,`Check creatinine for patients on dabigatran or therapeutic LMWH.`,`Bleeding time is obsolete; spine X-ray is not needed.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Large-bore IV cannula, monitoring and full resuscitation kit before the block.`,`Sit or lie the patient with **maximal lumbar flexion**.`,`Full asepsis with cap, mask and gown; let **chlorhexidine dry fully**.`,`Inject at **L3-L4 or L4-L5**, never above L2, with a 25-27G pencil-point needle.`,`Inject only after **free flow of clear CSF**; withdraw if there is leg paraesthesia.`,`Lie the patient flat, with **left uterine tilt** in pregnancy, and check BP every 1-2 minutes.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Hypotension: **phenylephrine** first line at caesarean; ephedrine if bradycardic.`,`Give a **crystalloid co-load** of 10-15 mL/kg as the spinal goes in.`,`Bradycardia: **atropine 0.6 mg** and ephedrine; give adrenaline early if worsening.`,`Intrathecal fentanyl adds analgesia; intrathecal morphine needs **12 hours of hourly monitoring**.`,`A failed block before incision is converted to general anaesthesia, not covered by sedation.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Nausea on the table is hypotension** until proved otherwise.`,`Hand tingling, weak voice or breathlessness means a **high spinal**; prepare to intubate.`,`**PDPH** is postural and fronto-occipital; a pencil-point needle prevents it.`,`Treat PDPH with analgesics and caffeine; an **epidural blood patch** cures it.`,`Severe back pain with prolonged block or new weakness suggests **vertebral canal haematoma**.`,`Haematoma needs MRI and decompression **within 8-12 hours**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Discharge after day surgery at **Bromage 0**, walking and passing urine.`,`Review headache, voiding, fever and leg power on day 1-2.`,`Refer for blood patch if PDPH is moderate-severe or lasts over 24-48 hours.`,`Refer the same day for fever with back pain or new leg weakness.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs around spinal anaesthesia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Bupivacaine 0.5% hyperbaric`,`Caesarean 10-12.5 mg; lower limb 12.5-17.5 mg intrathecal`,`Sensory block 1.5-3 h`,`Hypotension, bradycardia, high spinal`],[`Fentanyl intrathecal`,`10-25 microgram`,`Adds 1-1.5 h`,`Pruritus, nausea`],[`Morphine intrathecal`,`100 microgram (50-150)`,`12-24 h analgesia`,`Delayed respiratory depression at 6-12 h`],[`Phenylephrine`,`25-50 microgram/min or 50-100 microgram IV bolus`,`During the block`,`Reflex bradycardia`],[`Ephedrine`,`6 mg IV boluses`,`Repeat as needed`,`Fetal acidosis with large doses`],[`Atropine`,`0.6 mg IV, repeat to 1.2 mg`,`As needed`,`Tachycardia, confusion in elderly`],[`20% lipid emulsion`,`1.5 mL/kg IV, then 0.25 mL/kg/min`,`Max about 12 mL/kg`,`For LA toxicity only`],[`Caffeine (PDPH)`,`300 mg oral once or twice daily`,`24-48 hours`,`Max 900 mg/day; 300 mg if breastfeeding`],[`Paracetamol plus ibuprofen`,`1 g 6-hourly plus 400 mg 8-hourly`,`3-5 days`,`Ibuprofen after food`]]},{heading:`Spinal versus epidural`,columns:[`Feature`,`Spinal`,`Epidural`],rows:[[`End point`,`Free flow of CSF`,`Loss of resistance`],[`Needle`,`25-27G pencil-point`,`16-18G Tuohy with catheter`],[`Dose`,`Small, 10-15 mg bupivacaine`,`Large, 15-20 mL`],[`Onset`,`2-5 minutes`,`15-20 minutes`],[`Duration`,`Fixed, 1.5-3 h`,`Extendable via catheter`],[`PDPH`,`0.5-2%`,`Rare unless dura punctured`]]},{heading:`Antithrombotic intervals before a spinal (ASRA)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Stop before puncture`],rows:[[`Aspirin or NSAID alone`,`No need to stop`],[`Subcutaneous heparin 5000 units`,`4-6 hours`],[`Prophylactic LMWH`,`12 hours`],[`Therapeutic LMWH`,`24 hours`],[`Warfarin`,`5 days, INR 1.4 or less`],[`Clopidogrel or ticagrelor`,`5-7 days`],[`Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban`,`72 hours`]]}],remember:[`Cord ends at L1-L2: inject at L3-L4 or L4-L5, never above L2.`,`Sympathetic block is 2-6 segments above the sensory level.`,`Nausea on the table is hypotension until proved otherwise.`,`Phenylephrine first at caesarean; ephedrine if bradycardic.`,`Hyperbaric block can be steered by posture only for 15-20 minutes.`,`PDPH: pencil-point needle prevents, blood patch cures, bed rest does nothing.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - spinal anaesthesia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Single small dose of LA (usually **0.5% hyperbaric bupivacaine 10-15 mg**) into lumbar CSF **below the conus (L3-L4 or L4-L5)** for surgery below the umbilicus.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Neuraxial types - spinal, epidural, CSE, caudal; baricity hyper-, iso-, hypobaric; level - **saddle S2-S5, T10 TURP/hip, T6-T8 hernia/appendix, T4 caesarean**; Bromage 0-3.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Sodium-channel block of roots; **sympathetic level 2-6 segments above sensory, motor 2 below**; T5-L1 block -> venodilation -> **hypotension**; T1-T4 + Bezold-Jarisch -> bradycardia; diaphragm spared.`],[`Key history`,`Operation and duration, **exact antithrombotic and last dose time**, bleeding history, fixed cardiac output (AS, HOCM), raised ICP, back infection, neurological disease, refusal (absolute).`],[`Key examination`,`BP, pulse (under 60 predicts bradycardia), hydration, airway, murmur, back skin and landmarks (Tuffier line L4), baseline lower-limb neurology, fundi if ICP suspected.`],[`Investigations`,`No routine coagulation; **platelets 70,000 or more acceptable in obstetrics**; **INR 1.4 or less** after warfarin; creatinine for dabigatran or therapeutic LMWH.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Caesarean **2-2.5 mL heavy bupivacaine (10-12.5 mg) + fentanyl 10-25 microgram**; lower limb 12.5-17.5 mg; saddle 5-7.5 mg sitting 3-5 min; onset 2-5 min, fixed by 15-20 min, lasts 1.5-3 h.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Hypotension: **phenylephrine 50-100 microgram boluses or 25-50 microgram/min** (target 90% of baseline); ephedrine 6 mg if bradycardic; atropine 0.6 mg; adrenaline 10-100 microgram early if HR falling.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Full asepsis with mask, chlorhexidine 0.5% fully dry, **25-27 G pencil-point**, free CSF before injecting, withdraw for paraesthesia; co-load 10-15 mL/kg; test cold to T4 and touch to T5 for CS.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - one-third less drug, left uterine displacement; **ASRA 2025 intervals: prophylactic LMWH 12 h, therapeutic 24 h, DOACs 72 h, clopidogrel 5-7 d, warfarin 5 d with INR 1.4 or less**; aspirin and NSAIDs fine.`],[`Complications`,`Hypotension, bradycardia, **high or total spinal**, PDPH (0.5-2% with pencil-point), retention, TNS, haematoma (1 in 150,000+), meningitis, abscess, cauda equina, nerve injury 1 in 20,000-50,000.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Hand tingling or weak voice (rising block), block lasting too long or returning with back pain (**haematoma - MRI and decompression within 8-12 h**), fever with back pain, non-postural headache.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Bromage 0 and voiding before discharge; intrathecal morphine - RR and sedation hourly 12 h then 2-hourly 12 h; PDPH - analgesia, caffeine up to 48 h, **epidural blood patch 15-20 mL**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Cord ends L1-L2 (L3 neonate); nausea on table = hypotension; phenylephrine first line at CS; posture steers a heavy block only for 15-20 min; the family physician does not give the spinal.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Hypotension after a spinal`,steps:[{label:`Nausea or SBP under 80% of baseline`,detail:`Treat BP before giving an antiemetic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Left uterine displacement, fast crystalloid`,detail:`Co-load 10-15 mL/kg`},{label:`Heart rate normal or high`,detail:`Phenylephrine 50-100 microgram IV`},{label:`Bradycardia`,detail:`Ephedrine 6 mg + atropine 0.6 mg; adrenaline 10-100 microgram if falling`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Target SBP 90-100% of baseline`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Antithrombotics and neuraxial block (ASRA 2025)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Stop before puncture`,`Restart after`],rows:[[`Aspirin, NSAID alone`,`No need`,`-`],[`SC heparin 5000 units`,`4-6 h`,`After block`],[`Prophylactic LMWH`,`12 h`,`12 h`],[`Therapeutic LMWH`,`24 h`,`24 h`],[`Warfarin`,`5 days, INR 1.4 or less`,`-`],[`Clopidogrel / prasugrel`,`5-7 days / 7-10 days`,`-`],[`Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban`,`72 h`,`At least 6 h`]]}]},"surgery-office-blood-transfusion":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Blood transfusion** is IV administration of a specific blood component, prescribed like a drug.`,`**Component therapy** is the rule: red cells, FFP, platelets and cryoprecipitate.`,`Whole blood is used only for exchange transfusion or massive bleeding without components.`,`The commonest fatal reaction is still **the right blood to the wrong patient**.`,`One unit of red cells raises Hb by about **1 g/dL** in a 70 kg adult.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Restrictive threshold: Hb below 7 g/dL** for most stable inpatients.`,`Use **8 g/dL** with cardiovascular disease or orthopaedic surgery; 7.5 in cardiac surgery.`,`Acute MI is the exception: transfuse when **Hb is under 10 g/dL** (AABB 2025).`,`Symptomatic anaemia at any Hb is an indication in its own right.`,`**Massive transfusion**: one blood volume in 24 hours, 10 units in 24 hours, or 4 in 1 hour.`,`Reactions are classified by timing (acute or delayed) and mechanism (immune or not).`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Everyone has natural **IgM anti-A or anti-B** against the ABO antigen they lack.`,`2. In ABO mismatch, IgM fixes complement and lyses donor cells inside vessels.`,`3. As little as **10-15 mL** causes fever, loin pain, hypotension and DIC.`,`4. Free haemoglobin causes haemoglobinuria and acute tubular necrosis.`,`5. **Delayed haemolysis** (3-14 days) is an IgG anamnestic response to Kidd, Rh, Kell or Duffy.`,`6. **TRALI** is donor antibody injuring lung capillaries; **TACO** is volume overload.`,`7. Each unit carries 200-250 mg iron, with no excretory route, so overload follows.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask why transfusion is considered: symptoms, active bleeding or planned surgery.`,`Chronic iron deficiency in a stable patient is treated with **iron, not blood**.`,`Ask about previous transfusions, reactions and any **antibody card**.`,`A D-negative woman of childbearing age must never get D-positive red cells.`,`Heart failure, renal failure and age over 70 predict **TACO**.`,`Ask about religious objection early and plan alternatives.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for pallor, jaundice and signs of bleeding.`,`Assess shock; **class III-IV shock** (over 30% loss) needs red cells.`,`Check for heart failure before transfusing; give one slow unit with furosemide.`,`Record baseline vitals, then at **15 minutes**, hourly, at the end and 4 hours after.`,`Watch the patient directly for the first 15 minutes.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**ABO and Rh D grouping** by forward and reverse grouping, which must agree.`,`**Antibody screen** by the indirect antiglobulin test.`,`Major crossmatch: donor cells against recipient serum; the minor crossmatch is obsolete.`,`**DAT** detects antibody already on red cells, as in haemolytic reactions.`,`After a reaction: repeat group, DAT, LDH, bilirubin, urine Hb and cultures of patient and pack.`,`Chest X-ray and BNP separate TACO (raised BNP) from TRALI (normal BNP).`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Prescribe **one unit at a time**, then reassess.`,`Label the sample at the bedside; never pre-label tubes.`,`Do the **bedside identity check**: the patient states full name and date of birth.`,`Start within 30 minutes of issue and finish within **4 hours**.`,`Only **0.9% saline** in the line; Ringer's lactate clots and dextrose haemolyses.`,`Use a 170-200 micron filter set and run the first 15 minutes slowly.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**FFP 10-15 mL/kg** for bleeding with PT or APTT over 1.5 times normal.`,`Platelets: give below **10,000 prophylactically**, below 50,000 for surgery.`,`**Cryoprecipitate** when fibrinogen is below 1.5 g/L with bleeding (2 g/L obstetric).`,`Massive haemorrhage: red cells, FFP and platelets **1:1:1** with tranexamic acid within 3 hours.`,`Reverse warfarin bleeding with **4-factor PCC plus vitamin K**.`,`Irradiate components for the immunosuppressed and every donation from a relative.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Any reaction: **stop**, keep the vein open with saline via a new set, recheck identity.`,`Acute haemolysis: fluids to keep urine above **1-1.5 mL/kg/hour**, treat DIC, report.`,`**Anaphylaxis**: adrenaline 0.5 mg IM first; later use washed or IgA-deficient products.`,`**TRALI**: oxygen and ventilation, **no diuretic**; **TACO**: sit up, furosemide 40 mg IV.`,`Simple urticaria is the only reaction where the same unit may be restarted.`,`Fever, rash and pancytopenia after blood from a relative suggests **TA-GVHD**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`India mandates five tests: **HIV, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis and malaria**.`,`Professional (paid) donation is illegal; blood is a drug under law.`,`Donor Hb must be **12.5 g/dL** or more; weight 45 kg for 350 mL.`,`Report every reaction to the **Haemovigilance Programme of India**.`,`Patient blood management: treat anaemia with iron, minimise loss, tolerate anaemia.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Blood components and key drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Packed red cells`,`1 unit IV; child 10-15 mL/kg`,`90-120 min, within 4 h`,`Reactions, TACO, iron overload`],[`Fresh frozen plasma`,`10-15 mL/kg IV`,`Within 4 h of thawing`,`TRALI, TACO; not a volume expander`],[`Platelets`,`4-6 RDP or 1 SDP IV`,`Over 30 min`,`Bacterial sepsis; never refrigerate`],[`Cryoprecipitate`,`1 unit per 5-10 kg IV`,`Within 4 h of thawing`,`For low fibrinogen`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g IV over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h`,`Start within 3 h of injury`,`Avoid in active thrombosis`],[`4-factor PCC plus vitamin K`,`25-50 IU/kg IV plus 5-10 mg IV`,`Single dose`,`Thrombosis`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg IV`,`Between units for TACO risk`,`Never in TRALI`],[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mg IM thigh`,`Repeat every 5 min`,`First drug in anaphylaxis`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10 mL IV over 10 min`,`Keep ionised Ca above 1.1`,`Separate line from blood`],[`Deferasirox`,`20-40 mg/kg oral OD`,`When ferritin over 1000`,`Renal and liver toxicity`]]},{heading:`Transfusion reactions`,columns:[`Reaction`,`Timing`,`Key feature`,`Action`],rows:[[`Acute haemolytic`,`Minutes`,`Loin pain, hypotension, dark urine`,`Stop, fluids, DAT, report`],[`Febrile non-haemolytic`,`Within 4 h`,`Temperature rise, no shock`,`Slow, paracetamol, exclude haemolysis`],[`Urticarial`,`Minutes to hours`,`Hives only`,`Antihistamine, restart slowly`],[`Anaphylaxis`,`Minutes`,`Stridor, wheeze, shock`,`IM adrenaline`],[`TRALI`,`Within 6 h`,`Hypoxia, normal JVP`,`Oxygen, no diuretic`],[`TACO`,`Within 6-12 h`,`Raised JVP, hypertension`,`Sit up, furosemide`],[`Delayed haemolytic`,`3-14 days`,`Falling Hb, jaundice, positive DAT`,`Antibody screen and card`]]},{heading:`ABO compatibility`,columns:[`Recipient`,`Red cells`,`Plasma`],rows:[[`O`,`O only`,`O, A, B, AB`],[`A`,`A, O`,`A, AB`],[`B`,`B, O`,`B, AB`],[`AB`,`AB, A, B, O`,`AB only`]]}],remember:[`Transfuse the patient, not the number: Hb 7, or 8 with heart disease.`,`One unit at a time, then reassess.`,`Almost every fatal reaction is a clerical error at the bedside.`,`Only normal saline in the blood line.`,`TRALI has normal JVP and no diuretic; TACO has raised JVP and furosemide.`,`Always irradiate blood donated by a relative.`,`India tests every unit for HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis and malaria.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AABB 2025 (international AMI guideline) and ACC/AHA 2025 ACS: in acute myocardial infarction a liberal strategy - transfuse when Hb is under 10 g/dL - replaces the restrictive threshold.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - blood transfusion and reactions`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`IV administration of a **specific blood component** prescribed like a drug for a defined indication, dose and rate; **component therapy** is the rule.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Components - PRBC, FFP, platelets, cryo; reactions **acute (under 24 h) vs delayed, immune vs non-immune**; massive transfusion = 1 blood volume/24 h, 10 units/24 h or 4 units/1 h; ABC score 2+.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`ABO IgM + complement -> intravascular haemolysis -> DIC, AKI; delayed = anamnestic IgG (Kidd); TRALI = donor anti-HLA/HNA; **TACO = volume overload (commonest transfusion death)**; storage lesion - K up, citrate binds Ca.`],[`Key history`,`Why transfuse (symptoms, bleeding, surgery), cause of anaemia (iron deficiency = iron, not blood), previous reactions and antibodies, pregnancies and anti-D, heart or renal failure (TACO), immunosuppression, objection.`],[`Key examination`,`Pallor, shock class, heart failure; **baseline obs, then at 15 min, hourly, end and 4 h after**; watch the first 15 min directly.`],[`Investigations`,`Hb, **ABO/RhD forward + reverse grouping**, antibody screen (IAT), major crossmatch; DAT in haemolysis; reaction work-up - repeat group, DAT, LDH, bilirubin, haptoglobin, urine Hb, cultures of patient and pack.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Restrictive threshold Hb 7 g/dL; 8 g/dL with cardiovascular disease or orthopaedic surgery; 7.5 g/dL in cardiac surgery; under 10 g/dL in acute MI (AABB 2025)**; symptomatic anaemia at any Hb; clinical decision in acute bleeding.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**One unit at a time** (raises Hb about 1 g/dL; child 10-15 mL/kg), 16-18 G, 170-200 micron filter, **saline only in the line**, start within 30 min, finish within 4 h (90-120 min).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`FFP 10-15 mL/kg if PT/APTT over 1.5x with bleeding; platelets under 10,000 prophylactic, 50,000 surgery, 100,000 neuro; **cryo when fibrinogen under 1.5 g/L (2 in obstetrics)**; MTP 1:1:1 + **TXA 1 g within 3 h**; PCC + vitamin K for warfarin.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Bedside sample labelling; **patient states name and DOB** at identity check; blood warmer only for rapid infusion; irradiated for immunosuppressed and relatives, washed for IgA deficiency.`],[`Special groups`,`D-negative girls and women - D-negative red cells; anti-D 300 microgram at 28 weeks and within 72 h; thalassaemia pre-transfusion Hb 9-10.5, chelate at ferritin over 1000; Jehovah's Witness - iron, TXA, cell salvage.`],[`Complications`,`Acute haemolysis, FNHTR, urticaria, anaphylaxis, TRALI, TACO, bacterial sepsis (platelets), delayed haemolysis, PTP, **TA-GVHD (over 90% fatal)**, iron overload, hyperkalaemia, hypocalcaemia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Fever with rigors or hypotension, **drip-site or loin pain in first 15 min**, dark urine, new dyspnoea within 6 h, wheeze or angioedema, oozing under anaesthesia.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Hb after each unit; delayed haemolysis at 3-14 days; antibody card; report every reaction to **Haemovigilance Programme of India**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Five mandatory tests - HIV, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis, malaria**; voluntary non-remunerated donation (paid donation illegal); donor Hb 12.5, 45 kg for 350 mL; patient blood management - treat anaemia with iron first.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AABB 2025 / ACC/AHA 2025**: after MINT, acute MI is managed with a **liberal threshold - transfuse under 10 g/dL** (non-bleeding anaemia).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Most fatal reactions are clerical; Ringer lactate clots, dextrose haemolyses; TRALI no diuretic, TACO furosemide; always irradiate a relative's blood.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Reaction during transfusion`,steps:[{label:`Stop transfusion, keep vein open with saline via new set`,tone:`warn`},{label:`ABC and recheck patient and pack identity`},{label:`Which picture?`,detail:`Urticaria only / fever only / shock, dyspnoea, loin pain`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urticaria only - antihistamine, restart slowly`,tone:`good`},{label:`Fever 1 C rise, stable - paracetamol`,detail:`Exclude haemolysis and sepsis first`},{label:`Shock, haemoglobinuria, wheeze, hypoxia`,detail:`Adrenaline 0.5 mg IM if anaphylaxis; fluids for urine 1-1.5 mL/kg/h; cultures; report HvPI`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Breathless within 6 hours - TRALI or TACO?`,columns:[`Feature`,`TRALI`,`TACO`],rows:[[`Cause`,`Donor anti-HLA/HNA antibodies`,`Volume overload`],[`BP`,`Low`,`High`],[`JVP and BNP`,`Normal`,`Raised`],[`Fever`,`Common`,`Uncommon`],[`Treatment`,`Oxygen, ventilation, no diuretic`,`Sit up, oxygen, furosemide 40 mg IV`]]}]},"surgery-office-tracheostomy":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Tracheostomy** is an opening in the anterior tracheal wall brought to the skin as a stoma.`,`**Tracheotomy** strictly means the incision into the trachea itself.`,`The standard site is the **2nd-3rd tracheal rings**.`,`It is an elective or semi-elective operation, ideally over a secured airway.`,`The true emergency surgical airway is **cricothyroidotomy**, converted within 3-7 days.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`By site: high (1st ring, condemned), **mid (2nd-3rd rings, standard)**, low (4th ring or below).`,`By urgency: emergency or elective; by duration: temporary or permanent.`,`By technique: open surgical or **percutaneous dilatational** at the ICU bedside.`,`Indications: upper airway obstruction, prolonged ventilation, bronchial toilet, head and neck surgery.`,`**Prolonged ventilation** is now the commonest indication.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A tracheostomy cuts dead space by **70-100 mL** and reduces work of breathing.`,`2. It bypasses the nose, so air is not warmed, humidified or filtered.`,`3. Secretions thicken into crusts and plugs; **humidification is treatment**.`,`4. The glottis cannot close, so cough is weak and voice is lost.`,`5. Cuff pressure over **30 cmH2O** exceeds mucosal perfusion and causes stenosis or fistula.`,`6. The stoma tract takes **5-7 days** to mature; before that, a tube pushed back may make a false passage.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask why the tracheostomy was done and whether that cause has resolved.`,`Find out if the patient is a **laryngectomee**; then the mouth does not connect to the lungs.`,`Note tube type, size and date of last change.`,`Suction needed more than 3-4 hourly means not ready for decannulation.`,`**Any bleed after 48 hours** may be a sentinel bleed from the innominate artery.`,`Coughing on swallowing or food in secretions suggests aspiration or fistula.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Agitation and desaturation** mean a blocked or displaced tube until proved otherwise.`,`Check the flange is centred and tapes admit **one finger**.`,`A suction catheter that passes freely means the tube is patent.`,`Measure cuff pressure with a **manometer**; target 20-30 cmH2O.`,`A tube that **pulsates with the heartbeat** warns of impending innominate fistula.`,`Look for surgical emphysema and granulation at the stoma.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Waveform capnography** confirms the tube is in the airway faster than any X-ray.`,`**Chest X-ray** after every new tracheostomy for pneumothorax and tube position.`,`Check platelets, INR and APTT before an elective tracheostomy.`,`Culture sputum only for clinical infection; colonisation is universal.`,`Flexible laryngoscopy before decannulation to confirm a patent upper airway.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Humidify every patient**: heated humidifier, HME or moist stoma bib.`,`Suction **on demand**, no more than 10-15 seconds and three passes.`,`Clean the **inner cannula** at least twice daily; never leave the outer tube without it.`,`Change tapes with **two people**, one holding the flange throughout.`,`Keep a spare tube of the **same size and one smaller** at the bedside.`,`Nebulise **0.9% saline 3-5 mL** every 4-6 hours to loosen crusts.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Tenacious secretions: hypertonic saline 3% or acetylcysteine, after salbutamol.`,`Treat stoma infection only for cellulitis; colonised stomas need no antibiotics.`,`Granulation tissue: **silver nitrate** to the granulation only.`,`A **speaking valve** goes on only with the cuff fully deflated.`,`First tube change at **day 5-7** (7-10 after percutaneous).`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Blocked tube** is the commonest killer early on.`,`Emergency steps: oxygen to face and stoma, remove inner cannula, pass a suction catheter.`,`If the catheter will not pass, deflate the cuff, then remove the tube.`,`Sentinel or massive bleed: **hyperinflate the cuff**, then finger pressure through the stoma.`,`Late complications: stenosis, tracheomalacia, granuloma and fistulae.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Decannulate stepwise: cuff deflation, downsize, then **cap for 24 hours** including sleep.`,`After removal, the stoma usually closes in **5-7 days**.`,`Refer the same day for bleeding, aspiration, emphysema or a pulsating tube.`,`Refer to ENT for stridor on capping or after decannulation.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in tracheostomy care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Lidocaine 1% with adrenaline`,`5-10 mL infiltration; max 7 mg/kg`,`During procedure`,`Systemic toxicity`],[`Lidocaine 4% topical`,`2-3 mL into trachea`,`Before opening`,`Count in total dose`],[`0.9% saline nebulised`,`3-5 mL`,`Every 4-6 hours`,`Nil significant`],[`Hypertonic saline 3%`,`4 mL nebulised`,`2-4 times daily`,`Bronchospasm; salbutamol first`],[`N-acetylcysteine`,`600 mg oral OD or 3-5 mL 10% nebulised`,`As needed`,`Bronchospasm when nebulised`],[`Salbutamol`,`2.5-5 mg nebulised`,`4-6 hourly as needed`,`Tremor, tachycardia`],[`Glycopyrrolate`,`200 microgram IV/SC 6-8 hourly`,`Review at 1 week`,`Dry mouth, thick secretions`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly`,`5-7 days for stomal cellulitis`,`Not for colonisation`]]},{heading:`Complications by timing`,columns:[`Timing`,`Complications`,`Key prevention`],rows:[[`Immediate`,`Bleeding, pneumothorax, false passage, nerve injury`,`Strict midline dissection, chest X-ray`],[`Early (day 1-7)`,`Blocked tube, displacement, emphysema, infection`,`Humidify, suction, secure tapes`],[`Late`,`Stenosis, granulation, innominate or oesophageal fistula`,`2nd-3rd ring, cuff 20-30 cmH2O`]]},{heading:`Tube types`,columns:[`Tube`,`Use`,`Caution`],rows:[[`Metal Jackson's tube`,`Long-term home care in India`,`Cannot be ventilated`],[`Cuffed PVC`,`Ventilated or aspirating patients`,`Check cuff pressure each shift`],[`Uncuffed PVC`,`Children, safe swallowers`,`Keep a smaller spare`],[`Fenestrated`,`Weaning and speech`,`Plain inner tube for suction`],[`Double-lumen with inner cannula`,`All ward and home patients`,`Remove inner cannula first if blocked`]]}],remember:[`Window is the 2nd-3rd ring: too high causes stenosis, too low hits the innominate artery.`,`Cricothyroidotomy, not tracheostomy, is the emergency surgical airway.`,`Humidification is treatment, not comfort.`,`Cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O.`,`In a breathing emergency, remove the inner cannula first.`,`Speaking valve only with the cuff fully deflated.`,`Any bleed after 48 hours is a sentinel bleed until proved otherwise.`,`Child: vertical slit, stay sutures, uncuffed tube, no cartilage removed.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`DAS 2025 (unanticipated difficult intubation): scalpel-bougie-tube cricothyroidotomy remains the preferred Plan D emergency front-of-neck airway.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - tracheostomy`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Opening in the **anterior tracheal wall at the 2nd-3rd rings** brought to skin as a stoma; emergency surgical airway is **cricothyroidotomy**, converted within 3-7 days.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Site - high (1st ring, condemned), **mid (2nd-3rd, standard)**, low (4th+); emergency vs elective; temporary vs permanent; open vs **percutaneous dilatational**; indications in **four groups**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Indications: **upper airway obstruction, prolonged ventilation (commonest), bronchial toilet, elective cover for head and neck surgery**; TracMan - early (under 4 days) gives no survival benefit.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Dead space falls 70-100 mL; resistance falls; **nose bypassed - humidification essential**; weak cough and voice loss; cuff over 30 cmH2O ischaemia, under 20 leak and VAP; tract matures in 5-7 days.`],[`Key history`,`Indication and whether resolved, **laryngectomy or patent upper airway**, tube type and last change, secretions and suction frequency, bleeding after 48 h, aspiration, voice, home skills.`],[`Key examination`,`Agitation and desaturation = blocked or displaced tube; flange position, tapes one finger, airflow and capnography, **suction catheter passes?**, cuff by manometer 20-30 cmH2O, emphysema, pulsating tube.`],[`Investigations`,`SpO2 and **waveform capnography**; chest X-ray after every new tracheostomy; coagulation before elective; laryngoscopy before decannulation; no routine surveillance cultures.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Humidify (HME, heated humidifier, saline nebulisation 3-5 mL 4-6 hourly), suction on demand (catheter under half tube ID, 100-150 mmHg, 10-15 s), inner cannula cleaned 4-8 hourly, two-person tape change.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Thick secretions - **3% hypertonic saline (salbutamol first)**, N-acetylcysteine; stomal cellulitis only - co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 5-7 d; granulation - silver nitrate; first tube change day 5-7 (7-10 after PDT).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Metal Jackson tube (no ventilation), cuffed PVC (ventilated, aspiration), double-lumen with inner cannula (ward and home), fenestrated, **speaking valve only with cuff fully deflated**; bedside spare same size and one smaller.`],[`Special groups`,`Child - vertical slit, stay sutures, uncuffed, no cartilage excised, **needle cricothyroidotomy under 12 years**; laryngectomee - **oxygenate and ventilate via stoma only**.`],[`Complications`,`Immediate - haemorrhage, pneumothorax, false passage; early - **blocked tube (commonest killer)**, displacement, emphysema; late - stenosis, granuloma, **tracheo-innominate fistula**, TOF.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Any bleed after 48 h (sentinel bleed)**, pulsating tube, food or dye in secretions, emphysema, stridor on capping, early displacement.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Decannulation readiness - indication resolved, FiO2 40% or less, suction no more than 3-4 hourly, safe swallow; deflate cuff, downsize, cap 24 h incl. night, remove; stoma closes 5-7 days.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**DAS 2025**: in CICO, **scalpel-bougie-tube cricothyroidotomy** stays the preferred Plan D; 2nd-generation SGA is Plan B.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`2nd-3rd ring window; remove inner cannula first in an emergency; cuff 20-30 cmH2O; tracheostomy is not the emergency airway - cricothyroidotomy is.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Breathless tracheostomy patient - condensed NTSP`,steps:[{label:`Call help; oxygen to face AND stoma`,detail:`Check bed-head sign - laryngectomy?`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Remove speaking valve or cap, then inner cannula`},{label:`Does a suction catheter pass?`,detail:`Yes - suction and ventilate via tube`,tone:`decision`},{label:`No - deflate cuff, reassess`},{label:`Still not improving - remove the tube`,detail:`Reassess at mouth and stoma`},{label:`Oxygenate by patent route; secondary airway`,detail:`Oral intubation past stoma or 6.0 cuffed tube via stoma over bougie`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Emergency surgical airway options`,columns:[`Option`,`Where`,`Use`],rows:[[`Scalpel cricothyroidotomy`,`Cricothyroid membrane; scalpel-bougie-6.0 tube`,`Adult CICO - DAS 2025 Plan D`],[`Needle cricothyroidotomy`,`14 G cannula, jet O2 15 L/min, 1 s on 4 s off`,`Child under 12; bridge 30-45 min`],[`Tracheostomy`,`2nd-3rd tracheal rings`,`Elective or semi-elective over a secured airway`]]}]},"surgery-office-neck-swellings-thyroid":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A neck swelling is diagnosed by its **triangle**, its movement and the patient's **age**.`,`**Goitre** is any enlargement of the thyroid gland.`,`Over 40 years, a lateral neck mass is **malignant until proved otherwise**.`,`In India, a chronic cervical node is **tuberculosis** until FNAC and CBNAAT say otherwise.`,`A hard node in a tobacco user is **metastatic squamous carcinoma** until proved otherwise.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**WHO goitre grade**: 0 not palpable, 1 palpable not visible, 2 visible.`,`Goitre types: simple, toxic, inflammatory and neoplastic.`,`Sites: midline, anterior triangle, posterior triangle and supraclavicular.`,`Node levels **I-VI** each drain a predictable primary site.`,`**ACR TI-RADS** TR1-TR5 grades nodule ultrasound and sets FNAC size cut-offs.`,`**Bethesda I-VI** reports thyroid FNAC with a risk and an action for each.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Iodine deficiency lowers thyroid hormone, so **TSH rises** and drives gland growth.`,`2. Cycles of hyperplasia and involution form a **multinodular goitre**.`,`3. Some nodules become autonomous, causing toxic multinodular goitre in the elderly.`,`4. In Graves disease, **TSH-receptor antibodies** stimulate the gland and orbital tissue.`,`5. The thyroid moves with swallowing because pretracheal fascia binds it to the larynx.`,`6. A thyroglossal cyst is tethered to the tongue, so it rises on **tongue protrusion**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Sudden pain in a nodule suggests **haemorrhage into a cyst**.`,`A painful tender goitre after a viral illness suggests **de Quervain thyroiditis**.`,`**Hoarseness** with a nodule suggests nerve invasion by cancer.`,`Ask about pressure symptoms, hyper- or hypothyroid symptoms and weight loss.`,`Ask about childhood neck irradiation and family history of thyroid cancer or MEN 2.`,`Ask about tobacco, alcohol, dysphagia, otalgia and voice change in a hard node.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Give a **sip of water**: the thyroid and thyroglossal cyst rise on swallowing.`,`Ask the patient to **protrude the tongue**: only a thyroglossal cyst rises.`,`Palpate from behind; if the lower border is not reached, suspect **retrosternal goitre**.`,`**Pemberton sign**: facial plethora and stridor with arms raised.`,`**Berry sign**: absent carotid pulse beside a thyroid mass suggests malignancy.`,`For any hard node, examine the mouth, tonsils, larynx, nasopharynx and left supraclavicular fossa.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**TSH first** in every thyroid swelling.`,`Low TSH: isotope scan; a **hot nodule is almost never malignant**.`,`**Ultrasound** every nodule and grade it by TI-RADS.`,`Ultrasound-guided **FNAC** at TI-RADS size thresholds, reported in Bethesda.`,`Chronic node: **FNAC with ZN stain and CBNAAT**, plus chest X-ray and HIV test.`,`Follicular carcinoma **cannot be diagnosed on FNAC**; it needs histology.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Iodised salt**: 30 ppm at production, 15 ppm at the household.`,`Physiological goitre of puberty and pregnancy needs only reassurance.`,`Benign (Bethesda II) nodule: repeat ultrasound at **12-24 months**.`,`**Levothyroxine suppression** of benign nodules is not recommended.`,`Graves: **carbimazole** 15-40 mg daily, tapering to maintenance, for 12-18 months.`,`**Propranolol** controls tremor and palpitations until euthyroid.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Use **PTU** in the first trimester and thyroid storm.`,`**Radioiodine** for toxic multinodular goitre, toxic adenoma and relapsed Graves.`,`Before thyroidectomy for Graves: euthyroid first, then **Lugol's iodine for 10 days**.`,`Thyroidectomy for pressure, malignancy, retrosternal goitre or failed medical treatment.`,`TB lymphadenitis: **2HRZE/4HRE** daily for 6 months by weight band.`,`Infected thyroglossal or branchial cyst: antibiotic and aspirate, **never incise**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Agranulocytosis** on carbimazole: stop it and check neutrophils for sore throat or fever.`,`Neck swelling and stridor after thyroidectomy: **open the wound at the bedside**.`,`Post-thyroidectomy tetany: **calcium gluconate 10% 10-20 mL IV** over 10 minutes.`,`Bilateral nerve palsy gives **stridor with a good voice**; unilateral gives hoarseness.`,`Thyroid storm: PTU, then iodine 1 hour later, propranolol and hydrocortisone.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Urgent referral for stridor, rapid growth, hard fixed mass or hoarseness with a nodule.`,`Same-day referral for thyroid storm or sight-threatening Graves eye disease.`,`Levothyroxine: check TSH **6-8 weeks** after any change, then yearly.`,`Never start empirical anti-TB treatment without cytological or microbiological proof.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for thyroid and neck swellings`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Carbimazole`,`15-40 mg oral daily; maintenance 5-15 mg`,`12-18 months in Graves`,`Agranulocytosis; avoid in first trimester`],[`Propylthiouracil`,`100-150 mg oral 8-hourly`,`First trimester, thyroid storm`,`Fulminant hepatotoxicity`],[`Propranolol`,`40 mg oral 8-hourly`,`Until euthyroid`,`Avoid in asthma; use atenolol`],[`Lugol's iodine`,`3-5 drops 8-hourly`,`10 days before surgery`,`Escape if prolonged`],[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day; elderly 25-50 microgram`,`Lifelong if hypothyroid`,`AF, osteoporosis if over-replaced`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg daily, tapered`,`4-6 weeks (de Quervain)`,`Hyperglycaemia`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10-20 mL IV over 10 min`,`For tetany`,`Arrhythmia if given fast`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination`,`By weight band (50-64 kg: 4 tablets)`,`2 months HRZE, 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis; give pyridoxine`]]},{heading:`Bethesda thyroid cytology`,columns:[`Category`,`Diagnosis`,`Malignancy risk`,`Action`],rows:[[`I`,`Nondiagnostic`,`About 13%`,`Repeat FNA`],[`II`,`Benign`,`About 4%`,`Follow-up`],[`III`,`Atypia (AUS)`,`About 22%`,`Repeat FNA or lobectomy`],[`IV`,`Follicular neoplasm`,`About 30%`,`Diagnostic lobectomy`],[`V`,`Suspicious`,`About 74%`,`Surgery`],[`VI`,`Malignant`,`About 97%`,`Surgery by stage`]]},{heading:`Neck swellings by site`,columns:[`Site`,`Swellings`,`Clinching sign`],rows:[[`Midline`,`Thyroglossal cyst, dermoid, isthmus nodule`,`Thyroglossal cyst rises on tongue protrusion`],[`Anterior triangle`,`Thyroid, branchial cyst, carotid body tumour`,`Carotid body tumour moves only side to side`],[`Posterior triangle`,`TB nodes, cystic hygroma, nasopharyngeal metastasis`,`Cystic hygroma transilluminates`],[`Supraclavicular`,`Virchow node, lymphoma`,`Left side suggests abdominal cancer`]]}],remember:[`Tongue protrusion clinches a thyroglossal cyst; Sistrunk removes the central hyoid.`,`TSH first: a hot nodule is almost never malignant.`,`Follicular carcinoma cannot be diagnosed on FNAC.`,`Bilateral nerve palsy: stridor with a good voice.`,`Never open-biopsy a suspected metastatic node; FNAC and panendoscopy first.`,`Levothyroxine on an empty stomach, 4 hours from calcium and iron.`,`Never needle a carotid body tumour.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - neck swellings, goitre and thyroid nodule`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Goitre = any thyroid enlargement (WHO grade 0-2); neck lump sorted by **triangle, movement with swallowing or tongue protrusion, and age**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Goitre - simple, toxic, inflammatory, neoplastic; node levels I-VI; **ACR TI-RADS TR1-5** (FNAC TR3 2.5 cm, TR4 1.5 cm, TR5 1 cm); **Bethesda 2023 I-VI**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Iodine deficiency, goitrogens, autoimmunity (Graves TRAb, Hashimoto anti-TPO); cancer risk - childhood irradiation, family history, MEN 2, extremes of age, male; India - **TB nodes**, tobacco - SCC nodes.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Thyroglossal duct from foramen caecum loops behind hyoid (Sistrunk); TSH-driven hyperplasia -> MNG; papillary BRAF/lymphatic, follicular RAS/blood (**needs capsular invasion - FNAC cannot diagnose**); RLN in TO groove.`],[`Key history`,`Duration and growth, pain (de Quervain, haemorrhage), pressure symptoms, **hoarseness**, thyroid status, B symptoms, tobacco and ENT symptoms, irradiation, drugs (amiodarone, lithium, biotin), pregnancy.`],[`Key examination`,`Swallow and tongue protrusion, palpate from behind, retrosternal extension and **Pemberton**, trachea, **Berry sign**, bruit, eye signs, carotid body tumour (side-to-side only), full ENT exam for any hard node.`],[`Investigations`,`**TSH first** (low - scan, hot nodule not needled); **ultrasound with TI-RADS**; **US-guided FNAC by Bethesda**; nodes - FNAC + ZN + **CBNAAT**; lymphoma - core or excision; pre-op laryngoscopy and calcium.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Iodised salt (30 ppm production, 15 ppm household); benign nodule - **US at 12-24 months, no T4 suppression**; Graves - **carbimazole 15-40 mg -> 5-15 mg for 12-18 months** + propranolol 40 mg TDS.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`PTU 100-150 mg TDS in first trimester and storm; radioiodine (not in pregnancy); thyroidectomy for pressure, Bethesda V-VI, retrosternal, failed medical; DTC - lobectomy or total by risk (ATA 2025).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Pre-op Graves: euthyroid + **Lugol's iodine 0.1-0.3 mL TDS for 10 days**; post-thyroidectomy - check Chvostek and Trousseau; tension haematoma - **SCOOP at bedside**.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - PTU first trimester, raise levothyroxine 30% at confirmation; elderly - start T4 25-50 microgram; child with nodule - urgent referral.`],[`Complications`,`RLN palsy (unilateral hoarse, bilateral stridor), EBSLN pitch loss, hypocalcaemia 24-72 h, haematoma, storm, agranulocytosis on thionamides, hypothyroidism.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Stridor, rapid growth, hard fixed mass, **hoarseness with nodule**, child nodule, hard node in tobacco user, Virchow node, sight-threatening Graves eye disease.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Thionamides - FT4/T3 4-6 weekly; T4 - TSH 6-8 weeks after change, yearly when stable; DTC - thyroglobulin + antibody + US.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NIDDCP iodised salt**; 150 microgram/day adult, 250 in pregnancy; TB - Ni-kshay notification, nutrition support, **6-month HRZE/HRE**; never start ATT without proof.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Tongue protrusion = thyroglossal cyst; TSH first; never open-biopsy a metastatic node - FNAC and panendoscopy; agranulocytosis - stop drug, check neutrophils.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Thyroid nodule work-up`,steps:[{label:`TSH`,detail:`Low - FT4/T3 and scan; hot nodule not needled`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ultrasound - TI-RADS score and neck nodes`},{label:`Meets size threshold?`,detail:`TR3 2.5 cm, TR4 1.5 cm, TR5 1 cm`,tone:`decision`},{label:`US-guided FNAC - Bethesda`,detail:`I repeat; II follow-up; III-IV repeat/molecular/lobectomy; V-VI surgery`},{label:`Benign - US at 12-24 months, no suppression`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Lateral neck lump by age`,root:`Lateral neck lump`,arms:[{label:`Under 20`,steps:[`Reactive node`,`Branchial cyst`,`Cystic hygroma`,`TB node`]},{label:`20-40`,steps:[`TB lymphadenitis (FNAC + CBNAAT)`,`Thyroid nodule`,`Lymphoma`]},{label:`Over 40`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Metastatic SCC until proved otherwise`,`Panendoscopy, never excision first`,`Lymphoma - core or excision`]}]}]},"surgery-office-anorectal-disorders":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Most anal complaints are **piles, fissure, abscess-fistula, pilonidal disease or pruritus**.`,`Diagnosis is by inspection, **digital rectal examination and proctoscopy**.`,`**Haemorrhoids** are enlarged, displaced normal anal vascular cushions.`,`**Anal fissure** is a tear in the anoderm, **90% in the posterior midline**.`,`Never call rectal bleeding piles in a patient with cancer red flags without colonoscopy.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Goligher grade I-IV** for internal piles: bleeding only, up to permanent prolapse.`,`A fissure is **chronic after 6 weeks**, or with sentinel pile, papilla and exposed sphincter.`,`Abscess spaces: **perianal (commonest)**, ischiorectal, intersphincteric and supralevator.`,`**Parks fistula types**: intersphincteric, trans-sphincteric, suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric.`,`Complex fistula: over 30% of external sphincter, anterior in women, recurrent or Crohn.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Above the **dentate line** there is no pain sensation, so banding is painless.`,`2. Below it, somatic nerves make tears and thrombosed piles very painful.`,`3. Straining and hard stools engorge the cushions and break their supports.`,`4. In fissure, pain causes **internal sphincter spasm**, which makes the midline ischaemic.`,`5. Ischaemia stops healing, so treatment must **lower sphincter pressure**.`,`6. Abscess and fistula start in infected **anal glands** (cryptoglandular theory).`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the five questions: **bleeding, pain, lump, discharge and itch**.`,`Bright red blood on the paper after stool is outlet bleeding from piles or fissure.`,`Tearing pain during and for **1-2 hours after stool** suggests fissure.`,`Painless bleeding suggests internal piles; constant throbbing pain with fever suggests abscess.`,`Red flags: age 50+, weight loss, anaemia, change in bowel habit, family history.`,`Ask about diabetes, HIV, Crohn symptoms, TB and sexual history.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine in the **left lateral position** with a chaperone.`,`Gentle inspection diagnoses most fissures, with a **sentinel pile** below.`,`**Avoid DRE and proctoscopy on an acute fissure**; it is agonising.`,`Prolapsed piles show radial clefts; rectal prolapse shows **concentric folds**.`,`**Goodsall rule**: anterior openings run straight; posterior ones curve to the midline.`,`Never probe a fistula forcibly in the clinic.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Most benign anorectal disease needs **no test** beyond examination.`,`**Colonoscopy** for rectal bleeding at 50 or over, or with any red flag.`,`Check glucose and HIV in every perianal abscess.`,`**MRI pelvis** is the gold standard for complex or recurrent fistula.`,`Biopsy any **lateral**, multiple or painless fissure.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Fibre 25-30 g a day** with 2-3 litres of fluid for every pile and fissure.`,`Limit toilet sitting to **3-5 minutes**; use a footstool; no straining.`,`**Warm sitz baths** 10-15 minutes, 2-3 times a day and after stool.`,`Chronic fissure: **diltiazem 2% ointment** twice daily for 6-8 weeks.`,`Topical lidocaine before stool relieves fissure pain.`,`Drain a perianal abscess **the same day**; do not wait for fluctuation.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Rubber band ligation** for grade I-III piles, at least 1 cm above the dentate line.`,`Excise, do not incise, a **thrombosed external pile** within 72 hours.`,`Fissure failing 8 weeks: botulinum toxin or **lateral internal sphincterotomy**.`,`Grade III-IV piles: excisional haemorrhoidectomy.`,`Simple low fistula: fistulotomy; complex: seton, LIFT or flap.`,`Antibiotics after abscess drainage only for cellulitis, diabetes or immunosuppression.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Severe pain, fever or retention after banding suggests **pelvic sepsis**.`,`**Fournier gangrene**: pain out of proportion and crepitus; debride within hours.`,`About **one-third** of drained abscesses form a fistula.`,`Massive rectal bleeding with shock: treat as upper GI bleeding until excluded.`,`Never divide the **anorectal ring** or lay open a Crohn fistula.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review piles and fissure at **6-8 weeks**.`,`Urgent referral for bleeding at 50+, a rectal mass or iron deficiency anaemia.`,`Refer every fistula-in-ano and chronic pilonidal disease electively.`,`Pilonidal disease: keep the natal cleft free of hair.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for anorectal disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ispaghula husk`,`3.5 g sachet in water once or twice daily`,`Long term`,`Not at bedtime; never dry`],[`Polyethylene glycol 3350`,`17 g in 250 mL water OD`,`As needed`,`Bloating, diarrhoea`],[`MPFF (diosmin-hesperidin)`,`3 tablets/day 4 days, then 2/day 3 days`,`Acute attack`,`GI upset`],[`Lidocaine 2-5% topical`,`To anal verge before stool`,`1-2 weeks`,`Contact sensitisation`],[`Diltiazem 2% ointment`,`Pea-sized amount twice daily`,`6-8 weeks`,`Perianal itch`],[`GTN 0.2-0.4% ointment`,`2.5 cm strip twice daily`,`6-8 weeks`,`Headache; never with sildenafil`],[`Botulinum toxin A`,`20-25 units into internal sphincter`,`Once`,`Transient flatus incontinence`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly`,`5-7 days, high-risk only`,`Diarrhoea`],[`Albendazole`,`400 mg single dose, repeat at 2 weeks`,`Whole household`,`Avoid in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Goligher grades of internal piles`,columns:[`Grade`,`Prolapse`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`I`,`Bleeds, no prolapse`,`Fibre, banding or sclerotherapy`],[`II`,`Prolapses, reduces by itself`,`Banding`],[`III`,`Needs manual reduction`,`Banding, then surgery if it fails`],[`IV`,`Permanently prolapsed`,`Excisional haemorrhoidectomy`]]},{heading:`Parks types of fistula-in-ano`,columns:[`Type`,`Course`,`Operation`],rows:[[`Intersphincteric`,`Between the sphincters`,`Fistulotomy`],[`Trans-sphincteric`,`Across external sphincter`,`Fistulotomy if low; seton if high`],[`Suprasphincteric`,`Above puborectalis`,`Seton or flap`],[`Extrasphincteric`,`Rectum above levators to skin`,`Treat cause; often stoma`]]}],remember:[`Piles at 3, 7 and 11 o'clock; fissure at 6 o'clock.`,`Band above the dentate line, never below it.`,`A lateral fissure is never idiopathic; biopsy it.`,`Diltiazem heals fissure as well as GTN with fewer headaches.`,`Drain a perianal abscess the day it is seen.`,`Goodsall rule: anterior straight, posterior curved.`,`No DRE, no diagnosis.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO STI 2024: STI proctitis regimen re-cited to the 2024 WHO update - ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose (gonorrhoea) plus doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 days (21 days for LGV); syphilis benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - anorectal disorders`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Haemorrhoids = enlarged displaced **anal cushions**; fissure = anoderm tear distal to dentate line; abscess-fistula = cryptoglandular sepsis; pilonidal = acquired hair sinus.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Goligher I-IV**; fissure acute vs **chronic (over 6 weeks, sentinel pile, papilla, exposed IAS)**; abscess perianal, ischiorectal, intersphincteric, supralevator; **Parks fistula** 4 types; simple vs complex.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Low fibre, straining, prolonged toilet sitting, pregnancy; fissure - hard stool; fistula - anal glands, Crohn, TB, HIV; pilonidal - hair, sitting, deep cleft.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Dentate line - above painless (band here), below somatic and painful; cushions at **3, 7, 11 o'clock**; fissure = IAS spasm -> posterior midline ischaemia; Goodsall rule for fistula.`],[`Key history`,`Bleeding (bright on paper vs mixed), pain with stool (fissure) or constant throbbing (abscess), lump grade, discharge, itch; **red flags - 50+, weight loss, anaemia, habit change, family history**; sexual history; Crohn or TB clues.`],[`Key examination`,`Left lateral, chaperone, inspect (most fissures seen), strain (radial clefts vs concentric prolapse), **DRE**, proctoscopy, sigmoidoscopy if over 40; no DRE on acute fissure.`],[`Investigations`,`Clinical; Hb and ferritin; **colonoscopy for bleeding at 50+ or red flags, FIT 10 microgram Hb/g or more**; HbA1c and HIV in perianal sepsis; **MRI pelvis for complex fistula**; biopsy atypical fissure.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Fibre 25-30 g, 2-3 L fluid, short toilet time, footstool, sitz baths**; ispaghula 3.5 g BD; PEG 17 g; fissure - **diltiazem 2% BD 6-8 weeks** (GTN 0.2-0.4% if not, headache, never with sildenafil).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Rubber band ligation grade I-III** (1 cm above dentate line), sclerotherapy 5% phenol in almond oil; haemorrhoidectomy grade III-IV; botulinum 20-25 units or **lateral internal sphincterotomy**; fistulotomy for simple, seton/LIFT for complex.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Thrombosed external pile within 72 h - **excise not incise**; perianal abscess - **drain same day**, close to verge, deroof; pilonidal abscess off midline, Karydakis or Bascom definitive.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - conservative; Crohn - seton + anti-TNF, **never lay open**; STI proctitis - **ceftriaxone 1 g IM + doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 d (21 d LGV)**; threadworm - albendazole 400 mg repeat at 2 weeks, whole household.`],[`Complications`,`Fistula after one third of abscesses, incontinence (sphincter injury), stenosis (quack injections), post-banding pelvic sepsis, Fournier gangrene.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Bleeding at 50+ or with red flags, rectal mass, **IDA - scope both ends**, Fournier, massive bleed (treat as upper GI first), lateral or multiple fissures, severe pain or retention after banding.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review piles and fissure at 6-8 weeks; abscess wound at 48-72 h; constipation step-up fibre -> osmotic -> stimulant -> manometry/biofeedback.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO STI 2024**: ceftriaxone **1 g IM** single dose for anorectal gonorrhoea, doxycycline 7 days for chlamydia (azithromycin 1 g in pregnancy).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`No DRE, no diagnosis; band above the dentate line; diltiazem before GTN; lateral fissure is never idiopathic; drain abscess the day it is seen.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Bleeding per rectum in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Shock or massive bleed?`,detail:`Resuscitate; exclude upper GI bleed first`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Red flags?`,detail:`50+, weight loss, anaemia, habit change, mass, family history - colonoscopy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Inspect, DRE, proctoscopy`,detail:`Fissure, piles, prolapse, tumour`},{label:`Treat cause`,detail:`Fibre, sitz baths; banding or diltiazem as indicated`},{label:`Review 6-8 weeks; scope if persists`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Chronic anal fissure ladder`,steps:[{label:`Fibre, fluids, sitz baths, topical lignocaine`},{label:`Diltiazem 2% BD for 6-8 weeks`,detail:`First choice - fewer headaches`},{label:`GTN 0.2-0.4% BD 6-8 weeks`,detail:`Headache; never with PDE-5 inhibitors`},{label:`Botulinum toxin 20-25 units`,detail:`Transient flatus incontinence`},{label:`Lateral internal sphincterotomy`,detail:`Heals over 90%`,tone:`good`}]}]},"surgery-office-varicose-veins-leg-ulcers":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Varicose veins** are tortuous superficial veins **3 mm or more** wide when standing.`,`Reticular veins are 1-3 mm; telangiectasias are under 1 mm.`,`About **70% of chronic leg ulcers** are venous.`,`A venous ulcer sits in the **gaiter area** above the medial malleolus.`,`**No compression without an ABPI**; it excludes arterial disease first.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**CEAP**: Clinical, Etiology, Anatomy and Pathophysiology.`,`C classes run from **C0 no signs** to **C6 active ulcer**.`,`Primary varicose veins are commonest; secondary follow DVT or a pelvic mass.`,`ABPI **0.8-1.3**: full compression is safe.`,`ABPI 0.5-0.8: reduced compression under a specialist; **below 0.5: none**, refer urgently.`,`ABPI over 1.3 means calcified vessels; use toe pressure.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Walking squeezes the **calf muscle pump**, lowering venous pressure to 20-30 mmHg.`,`2. Valve failure or deep obstruction keeps pressure high: **ambulatory venous hypertension**.`,`3. High pressure distends capillaries and traps activated leucocytes.`,`4. Leaked red cells deposit **haemosiderin**, staining the skin brown.`,`5. Chronic inflammation causes eczema and **lipodermatosclerosis**.`,`6. Hypoxic, fibrosed skin breaks down into an ulcer.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Aching, heaviness and swelling **worse at day's end**, relieved by elevation.`,`Ask about prolonged standing at work, pregnancies, obesity and family history.`,`Previous DVT suggests secondary, post-thrombotic disease.`,`Claudication or **rest pain** points to arterial disease; compression may be unsafe.`,`Ask about diabetes, neuropathy and leprosy for sole ulcers.`,`**Nicorandil** can itself cause leg ulcers.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine **standing**, groin to foot, front and back.`,`**Saphena varix** has a cough impulse and disappears on lying down.`,`**Trendelenburg I** (release) tests the saphenofemoral junction.`,`**Trendelenburg II** (hold) tests perforator incompetence.`,`**Perthes positive** means deep vein obstruction; do not strip superficial veins.`,`Venous ulcer edge slopes; arterial is punched out; malignant is rolled and everted.`,`Measure **ABPI** in every patient with skin change or ulcer.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**ABPI** by hand-held Doppler before any compression.`,`**Duplex ultrasound** before intervention; reflux over 0.5 s superficial, over 1 s deep.`,`Duplex for thrombophlebitis; a quarter have coexisting DVT.`,`Check glucose, haemoglobin, albumin and creatinine in chronic ulcers.`,`Swab only clinically infected ulcers; all are colonised.`,`**Biopsy the edge** of an atypical ulcer or one not healed by 12 weeks.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Elevate legs above the heart and raise the foot of the bed at night.`,`Walk, do calf raises, and avoid long standing.`,`**Graduated compression stockings**: 20-30 mmHg for C2-C3, 30-40 mmHg for C4-C6.`,`Active ulcer: **four-layer bandage** giving about 40 mmHg, changed weekly.`,`Clean ulcers with tap water or saline under a simple non-adherent dressing.`,`Emollients for dry skin; avoid neomycin and lanolin creams.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Endovenous thermal ablation** is first line for truncal saphenous reflux.`,`Ablate within 2 weeks of ulcer presentation to speed healing (**EVRA trial**).`,`Correcting reflux after healing roughly halves recurrence (**ESCHAR trial**).`,`**Pentoxifylline 400 mg TDS** with compression improves ulcer healing.`,`Foam sclerotherapy for tributaries; liquid sclerotherapy for thread veins.`,`Stripping, if done, goes only to the knee to spare the saphenous nerve.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Bleeding varix**: lie flat, elevate the leg and press firmly for 10 minutes.`,`Thrombophlebitis 5 cm or more: **fondaparinux 2.5 mg** daily for 45 days.`,`Thrombus within 3 cm of the saphenofemoral junction is treated as DVT.`,`Blue, numb or painful toes under a bandage: remove it at once.`,`A rolled, bleeding edge in a chronic ulcer suggests **Marjolin ulcer**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review ulcers weekly; under 40% smaller in 4 weeks needs reassessment.`,`Repeat ABPI every 3-6 months on compression.`,`After healing, **lifelong compression**; ulcers recur in up to two-thirds without it.`,`Refer all symptomatic C2 and all C3-C6 disease for duplex and intervention.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for varicose veins and leg ulcers`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`MPFF (diosmin-hesperidin)`,`1000 mg oral OD with meals`,`Review at 2-3 months`,`GI upset, headache`],[`Pentoxifylline`,`400 mg oral TDS with food`,`Up to 6 months with compression`,`Nausea; caution with anticoagulants`],[`Sulodexide`,`250 LSU oral BD`,`2-3 months`,`Bleeding risk`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral 8-hourly`,`7-14 days (thrombophlebitis)`,`GI bleed, renal impairment`],[`Fondaparinux`,`2.5 mg SC OD`,`45 days (SVT 5 cm or more)`,`Avoid if CrCl under 20`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`DVT 15 mg BD 21 days, then 20 mg OD`,`At least 3 months`,`Bleeding; take with food`],[`Cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral 6-hourly`,`5-7 days (cellulitis)`,`Take 1 hour before food`],[`Clobetasone 0.05%`,`Topical once daily on eczema`,`1-2 weeks`,`Not on the ulcer bed`],[`Polidocanol`,`0.25-0.5% liquid or 1-3% foam`,`Per session, max 2 mg/kg/day`,`Pigmentation, DVT, visual aura`]]},{heading:`CEAP clinical classes`,columns:[`Class`,`Sign`,`Action`],rows:[[`C0`,`No signs`,`Reassure`],[`C1`,`Thread or reticular veins`,`Sclerotherapy if cosmetic`],[`C2`,`Varicose veins 3 mm or more`,`Duplex; ablate if symptomatic`],[`C3`,`Oedema`,`Compression`],[`C4`,`Pigmentation, eczema, lipodermatosclerosis`,`Compression; refer`],[`C5`,`Healed ulcer`,`Lifelong compression`],[`C6`,`Active ulcer`,`ABPI, four-layer bandage, early ablation`]]},{heading:`Leg ulcer types`,columns:[`Feature`,`Venous`,`Arterial`,`Neuropathic`],rows:[[`Site`,`Gaiter area, medial`,`Toes, heel, lateral malleolus`,`Sole, metatarsal heads`],[`Edge`,`Sloping`,`Punched out`,`Punched out with callus`],[`Pain`,`Mild, eased by elevation`,`Severe, eased by dependency`,`Painless`],[`ABPI`,`0.8 or more`,`Under 0.8`,`May exceed 1.3`],[`Treatment`,`Compression`,`Revascularise, no compression`,`Offloading`]]}],remember:[`No compression without an ABPI.`,`Venous ulcer medial gaiter; arterial ulcer toes, heel and lateral malleolus.`,`Trendelenburg I release tests the junction; II hold tests perforators.`,`Perthes positive: do not strip the superficial veins.`,`Endovenous ablation has replaced stripping for truncal reflux.`,`After healing, lifelong compression stockings.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - varicose veins and chronic leg ulcer`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Dilated tortuous superficial veins 3 mm or more standing** with reflux; venous ulcer = break between knee and ankle for 2 weeks+ with venous disease (70% of leg ulcers).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**CEAP 2020 C0-C6** (C4a pigmentation/eczema, C4b LDS/atrophie blanche, C4c corona); primary vs secondary vs congenital; **ABPI 0.8-1.3 full compression, 0.5-0.8 reduced, under 0.5 none, over 1.3 calcified**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Standing occupations, pregnancy, obesity, family history, previous DVT (post-thrombotic), Klippel-Trenaunay.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Valve failure -> **ambulatory venous hypertension** (stays near 90 mmHg) -> leucocyte trapping, fibrin cuff -> haemosiderin, eczema, LDS -> ulcer in gaiter area (Cockett perforators).`],[`Key history`,`Aching, heaviness, cramps worse evening, relieved by elevation; occupation; previous DVT; claudication or rest pain; diabetes or leprosy; ulcer pain and duration; nicorandil, CCBs.`],[`Key examination`,`Standing exam, saphena varix, tap and cough tests, Trendelenburg I (SFJ) and II (perforators), **Perthes (deep obstruction)**, hand-held Doppler; ulcer edge, pulses, **ABPI in every ulcer**, monofilament.`],[`Investigations`,`**ABPI before compression**; **duplex** - reflux over 0.5 s superficial, over 1 s deep; glucose, Hb, albumin; swab only if infected; **biopsy if atypical or not healed at 12 weeks**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Elevation, walking, calf exercises, weight loss, emollients; **class 1-2 graduated compression**; ulcer - **four-layer bandage about 40 mmHg** if ABPI 0.8+; MPFF 1000 mg daily.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Endovenous thermal ablation first line for truncal reflux**; cyanoacrylate or MOCA; foam sclerotherapy; high tie and strip to knee if no endovenous; **early ablation within 2 weeks for ulcers (EVRA)**; pentoxifylline 400 mg TDS.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Stockings measured in the morning, replaced every 3-6 months, rolled on not folded; tumescent 0.1% lignocaine (35 mg/kg dilute limit).`],[`Special groups`,`Mixed ulcer ABPI 0.5-0.8 - reduced compression under specialist; diabetic or neuropathic - offloading; superficial vein thrombosis 5 cm+ - **fondaparinux 2.5 mg daily 45 days**; within 3 cm of SFJ - treat as DVT.`],[`Complications`,`Bleeding varix, thrombophlebitis, DVT, eczema, LDS, ulcer, **Marjolin ulcer**; after intervention - EHIT, nerve injury (saphenous, sural), recurrence.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Bleeding varix (elevate and press), suspected DVT, SVT near SFJ, ABPI under 0.5 or rest pain, rolled everted edge, cellulitis with sepsis, blue toes under bandage.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Ulcer weekly; **not 40% smaller in 4 weeks - reassess**; ABPI every 3-6 months; **lifelong class 2 compression after healing** (recurrence up to two-thirds).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`No compression without ABPI; venous ulcers above medial malleolus, arterial on toes, heel, lateral malleolus; Perthes positive - do not strip; correcting reflux halves recurrence (ESCHAR), early ablation speeds healing (EVRA).`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Chronic leg ulcer - first visit`,steps:[{label:`Pulses and ABPI`,detail:`Under 0.5 - no compression, urgent vascular`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Classify by site, edge, pain`,detail:`Venous, arterial, neuropathic, malignant`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Venous with ABPI 0.8+`,detail:`Four-layer compression about 40 mmHg, weekly`},{label:`Refer for duplex and early ablation`,detail:`Within 2 weeks (EVRA)`},{label:`Not 40% smaller at 4 weeks or unhealed at 12`,detail:`Reassess and biopsy edge`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Healed - lifelong class 2 stockings`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Venous vs arterial vs neuropathic ulcer`,columns:[`Feature`,`Venous`,`Arterial`,`Neuropathic`],rows:[[`Site`,`Gaiter, above medial malleolus`,`Toes, heel, lateral malleolus`,`Sole, metatarsal heads`],[`Edge`,`Sloping`,`Punched out`,`Punched out with callus`],[`Pain`,`Mild, eased by elevation`,`Severe, rest pain eased by dependency`,`Painless`],[`ABPI`,`0.8 or more`,`Under 0.8`,`May exceed 1.3`],[`Treatment`,`Compression, ablation`,`Revascularise, no compression`,`Offloading`]]}]},"surgery-office-scrotal-swelling-acute-scrotum":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute scrotum** is sudden pain, swelling or tenderness of the scrotum or its contents.`,`In a boy or young man it is **testicular torsion until the scrotum is explored**.`,`Salvage is **over 90% within 6 hours** and under 10% after 24 hours.`,`Painless swellings are sorted by three questions, then by transillumination.`,`Office problems also include undescended testis, phimosis, paraphimosis and hernias.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Acute scrotum: **testicular torsion, appendage torsion and epididymo-orchitis**.`,`Torsion is **extravaginal** in neonates and **intravaginal** (bell-clapper) in adolescents.`,`Cannot get above it: **inguinoscrotal hernia**.`,`Testis not separate: **hydrocele** (transilluminates) or **tumour** (does not).`,`Testis separate: **epididymal cyst** or **varicocele** (bag of worms).`,`Varicocele grades I-III; undescended testis is palpable or impalpable.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. In the **bell-clapper deformity**, the testis hangs free with a horizontal lie.`,`2. Cremaster contraction during sleep, cold or sport spins the testis.`,`3. Twisting first blocks veins, then arterial inflow stops and the testis infarcts.`,`4. A **patent processus vaginalis** causes childhood hernia and hydrocele.`,`5. Left varicocele is common: the left vein enters the renal vein at a right angle.`,`6. The testis drains to **para-aortic nodes**; scrotal skin drains to inguinal nodes.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Sudden severe pain**, often waking from sleep, with vomiting suggests torsion.`,`Adolescents may report only lower abdominal pain; always examine the testes.`,`Gradual pain over days with fever and dysuria suggests epididymo-orchitis.`,`Ask about sexual history, instrumentation and recent mumps.`,`A **painless hard heavy testis** suggests tumour.`,`Ask if the swelling reduces on lying down or varies during the day.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine standing and lying, with cough and Valsalva.`,`**Can you get above it?** If not, it is an inguinoscrotal hernia.`,`**Transilluminate**: hydrocele and epididymal cyst glow; tumour does not.`,`Torsion: **high-riding testis, horizontal lie, absent cremasteric reflex**.`,`**Prehn sign is unreliable** and must not be used to exclude torsion.`,`Appendage torsion: tender upper-pole nodule with a **blue dot** sign.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Suspected torsion needs **no investigation**; TWIST 5 or more goes straight to theatre.`,`TWIST 3-4: urgent Doppler, with a low threshold to explore.`,`Epididymo-orchitis: **first-void urine NAAT** and urine culture.`,`Scrotal ultrasound for any intratesticular mass or impalpable testis in a hydrocele.`,`Tumour: **AFP, beta-hCG and LDH** before orchidectomy.`,`No ultrasound for undescended testis before referral.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Torsion: analgesia, nil by mouth, **immediate surgical exploration**.`,`At surgery, untwist and fix the testis, and **always fix the other side**.`,`Manual detorsion is outward, **like opening a book**, but exploration is still needed.`,`Sexually acquired epididymitis: **ceftriaxone 500 mg IM plus doxycycline** for 10 days.`,`Enteric epididymitis in older men: **levofloxacin 500 mg** daily for 10 days.`,`Scrotal support, ice and NSAIDs for epididymo-orchitis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Tumour: **radical inguinal orchidectomy**; never a scrotal incision or biopsy.`,`Undescended testis: **orchidopexy at 6-12 months**, by 18 months at the latest.`,`Hormonal therapy for undescended testis is **not recommended**.`,`Infant hydrocele usually closes by 1-2 years; operate if it persists.`,`Paediatric inguinal hernia: prompt **herniotomy**.`,`Treat varicocele only for infertility, adolescent hypotrophy or persistent pain.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Physiological phimosis: reassure; **never forcibly retract** the foreskin.`,`Symptomatic phimosis: **betamethasone cream** twice daily for 4-8 weeks.`,`**Lichen sclerosus** (BXO) is the absolute indication for circumcision.`,`**Paraphimosis**: compress the glans, then reduce; dorsal slit if it fails.`,`Bilateral impalpable testes in a newborn suggest a disorder of sex development.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fournier gangrene: crepitus and dusky skin; **debride within hours**.`,`Scrotal trauma with haematocele needs exploration within 72 hours.`,`Right-sided or non-emptying varicocele suggests renal tumour.`,`Epididymitis not improving by 3 days: think abscess, torsion, tumour or TB.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for scrotal and penile conditions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone`,`500 mg IM single dose`,`Once`,`Rash, rare anaphylaxis`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral BD`,`10 days`,`Oesophagitis; not in pregnancy`],[`Levofloxacin`,`500 mg oral OD`,`10 days`,`Tendon rupture, QT prolongation`],[`Ibuprofen plus paracetamol`,`400 mg 8-hourly plus 1 g 6-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`GI bleed, renal impairment`],[`Morphine`,`0.1 mg/kg IV titrated`,`Before transfer`,`Never excludes torsion`],[`Betamethasone 0.05% cream`,`Twice daily to preputial ring`,`4-8 weeks`,`Relapse possible`],[`Clotrimazole 1% cream`,`Twice daily`,`7-14 days`,`Check glucose in adults`],[`Lidocaine 1% plain`,`2-5 mL each side, max 3 mg/kg`,`Penile block`,`Never with adrenaline`],[`IDA (ivermectin, DEC, albendazole)`,`200 microgram/kg, 6 mg/kg, 400 mg`,`Once yearly (MDA)`,`Not in pregnancy or under 2`]]},{heading:`Acute scrotum compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Torsion`,`Appendage torsion`,`Epididymo-orchitis`],rows:[[`Age and onset`,`Teens; sudden, vomiting`,`7-12 years; over hours`,`Sexually active or older; over days`],[`Signs`,`High horizontal testis`,`Blue dot, upper-pole nodule`,`Tender epididymis, fever`],[`Cremasteric reflex`,`Absent`,`Present`,`Present`],[`Doppler flow`,`Absent or reduced`,`Normal`,`Increased`],[`Action`,`Explore and fix both`,`Analgesia, rest`,`Antibiotics, support`]]},{heading:`TWIST score`,columns:[`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`Testicular swelling`,`2`],[`Hard testis`,`1`],[`Absent cremasteric reflex`,`1`],[`Nausea or vomiting`,`1`],[`High-riding testis`,`1`]]}],remember:[`Time is testis: over 90% salvage within 6 hours.`,`TWIST 5 or more: straight to theatre without Doppler.`,`Always fix the other testis; the bell-clapper is bilateral.`,`You cannot get above a hernia; you can get above a hydrocele.`,`Never approach a testicular tumour through the scrotum.`,`Orchidopexy by 6-12 months, 18 months at the latest.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - scrotal swelling and acute scrotum`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute scrotum = acute pain or swelling of scrotal contents; **torsion until explored** in a boy or young man.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Acute - **torsion (intravaginal bell-clapper; extravaginal neonatal), appendage torsion, epididymo-orchitis**; painless - 3 questions (get above? testis separate? transilluminates?); varicocele grade I-III; UDT palpable vs impalpable.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Bell-clapper (bilateral), cold, sleep, exercise; epididymitis - **chlamydia/gonorrhoea under 35**, E. coli older or after instrumentation; mumps; TB; filariasis; tumour risk - cryptorchidism.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Twist blocks veins then arteries -> infarction; **salvage over 90% within 6 h, under 10% after 24 h**; testis drains to para-aortic nodes, scrotum to inguinal; left varicocele - right-angle entry to renal vein.`],[`Key history`,`Sudden pain from sleep or sport, **vomiting**, previous self-resolving episodes, dysuria and discharge, sexual history, mumps, painless heavy testis, infertility, filarial district.`],[`Key examination`,`**High-riding horizontal testis, absent cremasteric reflex**; blue dot (appendage); tender epididymis with reflex present; transillumination; bag of worms; testes in every boy with abdominal pain.`],[`Investigations`,`**TWIST 5+ - theatre without imaging**, 3-4 urgent Doppler; first-void NAAT, MSU; scrotal US for masses; **AFP, beta-hCG, LDH before orchidectomy**; no US before UDT referral.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Torsion - NBM, analgesia, **immediate exploration, bilateral fixation**; manual detorsion outward (open book) as bridge; epididymitis under 35 - **ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + doxycycline 100 mg BD 10 days**; enteric - levofloxacin 500 mg OD 10 days.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Tumour - **radical inguinal orchidectomy**, never scrotal; adult hydrocele - Jaboulay or Lord; varicocele surgery only for infertility, hypotrophy or pain; phimosis - **betamethasone 0.05% BD 4-8 weeks**; circumcision for BXO.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Dorsal penile block **plain lignocaine** at 10 and 2 o'clock; paraphimosis - ice, compression, sugar, reduce, dorsal slit if fails.`],[`Special groups`,`**UDT - refer at 6 months, orchidopexy 6-12 months (by 18)**, no hCG; bilateral impalpable testes in newborn - DSD, 17-OHP; communicating hydrocele - observe to 2 years; infant hernia - prompt herniotomy.`],[`Complications`,`Testicular loss or atrophy, infertility, abscess, chronic orchialgia, Fournier gangrene, missed tumour.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sudden testicular pain, vomiting with lower abdominal pain in adolescent, hard intratesticular mass, Fournier, dusky paraphimosis, right or non-decompressing varicocele.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Epididymitis review day 3, swelling over 4-6 weeks - ultrasound; retractile testis yearly to puberty; testicular self-examination.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`MMR (IAP 9 m, 15 m, 4-6 y; UIP MR lacks mumps); filariasis **IDA MDA** (ivermectin, DEC, albendazole) and MMDP hydrocele surgery (NCVBDC).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Time is testis; Doppler never delays exploration; fix both sides; Prehn sign cannot exclude torsion; never approach a testicular tumour through the scrotum.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute painful scrotum`,steps:[{label:`Sudden pain, vomiting, high horizontal testis?`,detail:`Absent cremasteric reflex - score TWIST`,tone:`decision`},{label:`TWIST 5-7`,detail:`Straight to theatre - explore and fix both`,tone:`warn`},{label:`TWIST 3-4`,detail:`Urgent Doppler; low threshold to explore`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Upper pole nodule, blue dot, reflex present`,detail:`Appendage torsion - analgesia, rest`},{label:`Gradual, fever, dysuria, tender epididymis`,detail:`Epididymo-orchitis - antibiotics by age and risk`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Painless scrotal swelling - three questions`,root:`Scrotal swelling`,arms:[{label:`Cannot get above`,steps:[`Inguinoscrotal hernia`,`Infantile hydrocele`]},{label:`Above, testis not separate`,steps:[`Hydrocele - transilluminates`,`Tumour - does not; inguinal orchidectomy`]},{label:`Above, testis separate`,steps:[`Epididymal cyst - brilliant`,`Varicocele - bag of worms, empties lying`]}]}]},"surgery-office-rif-pain-appendicitis":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute appendicitis** is acute inflammation of the vermiform appendix.`,`It is the **commonest emergency abdominal operation**; peak age 10-30 years.`,`Lifetime risk is about **7-8%**.`,`Perforation occurs in 20-30%, and in over half of under-5s and the elderly.`,`**McBurney point**: junction of lateral third and medial two-thirds of the spinoumbilical line.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Uncomplicated versus complicated** decides treatment.`,`Complicated means gangrene, perforation, mass, abscess or generalised peritonitis.`,`**Alvarado (MANTRELS)**: 4 or less unlikely, 5-6 equivocal, 7 or more probable.`,`WSES prefers the **AIR score** and Adult Appendicitis Score in adults.`,`Paediatric Appendicitis Score of 6 or more suggests appendicitis.`,`No score replaces **serial examination** by the same doctor.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. An **appendicolith** or lymphoid hyperplasia blocks the lumen.`,`2. Mucus collects and intraluminal pressure rises.`,`3. Lymphatic, then venous, flow stops and the wall becomes ischaemic.`,`4. Bacteria invade; the **end-artery** supply makes the tip gangrenous first.`,`5. Early pain is **periumbilical** (visceral, T10); it shifts to the RIF when parietal peritoneum is involved.`,`6. After 3-5 days, omentum may wall it off as an **appendicular mass**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Murphy sequence**: periumbilical pain, then anorexia and vomiting, then RIF pain, then fever.`,`**Vomiting before pain** suggests gastroenteritis instead.`,`A hungry patient rarely has appendicitis.`,`In women, ask the last period, discharge and sudden pain with vomiting.`,`In children, ask about sore throat, red-currant-jelly stool and scrotal pain.`,`In India, ask about typhoid-like fever, TB symptoms and dysentery.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**McBurney point tenderness with guarding** is the most reliable sign.`,`**Rovsing sign**: pressing the left iliac fossa hurts the right.`,`**Psoas sign** suggests a retrocaecal appendix; **obturator sign** a pelvic one.`,`Pain on coughing or heel-drop shows peritoneal irritation.`,`Examine the **testes in every boy** and the hernial orifices.`,`Rigidity, rebound everywhere and tachycardia mean generalised peritonitis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pregnancy test first** in every woman of reproductive age.`,`Raised white count and CRP support it; both normal make it unlikely.`,`**Ultrasound** first in children, young women and pregnancy; non-compressible appendix over 6 mm.`,`**Low-dose CT** for equivocal adults and the elderly.`,`MRI without contrast if ultrasound is inconclusive in pregnancy.`,`Never give purgatives or enemas; do not delay referral for imaging.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Nil by mouth, IV fluids and **early analgesia**; it does not mask signs.`,`Start IV **ceftriaxone plus metronidazole** once the diagnosis is made.`,`**Laparoscopic appendicectomy** is preferred; open surgery is safe where needed.`,`Surgery within 24 hours of admission is safe once antibiotics have started.`,`No post-operative antibiotics for uncomplicated appendicitis.`,`Give **2-3 days** of antibiotics after surgery for complicated disease.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Antibiotics-first** suits CT-proven uncomplicated disease without an appendicolith.`,`About **3 in 10** need appendicectomy by 90 days after antibiotics-first.`,`Appendicular mass: **Ochsner-Sherren regimen** with daily marking of the mass.`,`Abandon conservative care if pulse, pain or the mass increase.`,`Abscess over 3-4 cm: image-guided drainage plus antibiotics.`,`Colonoscopy or interval CT for older patients after a mass.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: the appendix moves upward; perforation raises fetal loss to 20-35%.`,`Laparoscopic appendicectomy is safe in **all trimesters**.`,`Avoid NSAIDs after 20 weeks of pregnancy.`,`Children under 5 perforate early because the omentum is underdeveloped.`,`Elderly patients have blunted signs; consider caecal carcinoma.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Perforation with peritonitis: **30 mL/kg** crystalloid if hypotensive and antibiotics within 1 hour.`,`Positive pregnancy test with RIF pain and shock means **ruptured ectopic**.`,`Swinging fever with tenesmus at day 5-10 suggests **pelvic abscess**.`,`Rigors and jaundice suggest **pylephlebitis**.`,`Always read the histology for neuroendocrine or mucinous tumours.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in appendicitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`1 g IV or oral 6-hourly; child 15 mg/kg`,`As needed`,`Max 4 g/day`],[`Morphine`,`0.1 mg/kg IV titrated`,`As needed`,`Respiratory depression; naloxone ready`],[`Ondansetron`,`4 mg IV 8-hourly`,`As needed`,`QT prolongation`],[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`1-2 g IV OD plus 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`Pre-op; 2-3 days if complicated`,`No alcohol with metronidazole`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`1.2 g IV 8-hourly, then 625 mg oral`,`Step-down`,`Cholestatic jaundice`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8 hourly`,`2-3 days after source control`,`C. difficile, hypokalaemia`],[`Ertapenem`,`1 g IV OD`,`1-3 days, then oral to 7-10 days`,`For antibiotics-first`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral 8-hourly`,`3-5 days after surgery`,`Avoid after 20 weeks pregnancy`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC OD`,`Until mobile`,`Bleeding`]]},{heading:`Alvarado (MANTRELS) score`,columns:[`Component`,`Points`],rows:[[`Migration of pain to RIF`,`1`],[`Anorexia`,`1`],[`Nausea or vomiting`,`1`],[`Tenderness in RIF`,`2`],[`Rebound tenderness`,`1`],[`Elevated temperature 37.3 or more`,`1`],[`Leucocytosis over 10,000`,`2`],[`Shift to left (neutrophils over 75%)`,`1`]]},{heading:`Mimics of appendicitis`,columns:[`Group`,`Diagnosis`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Child`,`Mesenteric adenitis`,`Recent sore throat, high fever`],[`Infant`,`Intussusception`,`Red-currant-jelly stool`],[`Boy`,`Testicular torsion`,`Scrotal pain, high testis`],[`Young woman`,`Ectopic pregnancy`,`Positive pregnancy test, shock`],[`Young woman`,`Ovarian torsion or PID`,`Sudden pain or discharge`],[`Any adult`,`Ureteric colic`,`Loin-to-groin colic, haematuria`],[`Elderly`,`Caecal carcinoma`,`Anaemia, weight loss, mass`]]}],remember:[`Pregnancy test first in every woman of reproductive age.`,`Pain before vomiting is appendicitis; vomiting before pain is gastroenteritis.`,`Psoas sign retrocaecal; obturator sign pelvic.`,`The appendicular artery is an end artery; the tip dies first.`,`Find the base where the three taeniae converge.`,`Analgesia does not mask appendicitis.`,`Mnemonic MANTRELS for the Alvarado score.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`SSC 2026: perforated appendicitis with sepsis - at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid, antibiotic within 1 hour.`,`WSES 2025 (Jerusalem update, JAMA Surg 2026): post-operative antibiotics for complicated appendicitis shortened to 2-3 days after adequate source control.`,`WSES 2025: interval appendicectomy not routine - advised for adults aged 35 or more managed non-operatively for an appendicular abscess (missed neoplasm).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - RIF pain and acute appendicitis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute inflammation of the appendix, usually from **luminal obstruction**; commonest emergency abdominal operation (lifetime 7-8%).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Uncomplicated vs complicated** (gangrene, perforation, mass, abscess, peritonitis); **Alvarado 4 or less unlikely, 5-6 equivocal, 7+ probable**; **AIR 0-4, 5-8, 9-12**; AAS; PAS 6+.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Appendicolith, lymphoid hyperplasia (post-viral, children), parasites, carcinoid; perforation high under 5 and in elderly.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Obstruction -> mucus, rising pressure -> venous then arterial block (**end artery - tip gangrene**) -> perforation; **visceral T10 periumbilical pain -> parietal RIF pain**; position decides signs.`],[`Key history`,`**Murphy sequence - pain, anorexia/vomiting, migration to RIF, fever**; LMP and discharge in women; children - sore throat, intussusception, torsion; India - typhoid, ileocaecal TB, amoebiasis.`],[`Key examination`,`**McBurney tenderness and guarding**, Rovsing, rebound or percussion tenderness, psoas (retrocaecal), obturator (pelvic), RIF mass; **testes in every boy**, pelvic exam when PID possible.`],[`Investigations`,`**Urine beta-hCG first in every woman of reproductive age**; TLC and CRP (both normal - unlikely); **graded-compression US first in children, young women, pregnancy**; low-dose CT in equivocal adults and elderly; MRI in pregnancy.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`NBM, IV fluids, **early analgesia (does not mask)**, **ceftriaxone + metronidazole or co-amoxiclav 1.2 g IV**; **laparoscopic appendicectomy**, within 24 h acceptable once antibiotics started.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Antibiotics-first for CT-proven uncomplicated without appendicolith (about 3 in 10 operated by 90 days); mass - **Ochsner-Sherren**; abscess over 3-4 cm - percutaneous drainage; complicated - **post-op antibiotics 2-3 days (WSES 2025)**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Lanz or gridiron incision, muscle-splitting, find base at taeniae convergence, ligate appendicular artery, inspect 60 cm ileum for Meckel if normal; laparoscopy safe in all trimesters.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - appendix displaced up, perforation raises fetal loss to 20-35%, no NSAIDs after 20 weeks; children - omentum fails to wall off; elderly - early perforation, caecal carcinoma mimic.`],[`Complications`,`Perforation, peritonitis, pelvic or subphrenic abscess, **pylephlebitis**, SSI, adhesive obstruction, stump appendicitis, faecal fistula.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Generalised peritonitis, shock, **positive pregnancy test with RIF pain**, child under 5, pregnancy, elderly, RIF mass with weight loss over 40, swinging fever days 5-10.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Safety-net low-risk discharges (review 12-24 h); wound 7-10 days; **read histology** (NET over 2 cm or at base, mucinous neoplasm - oncology); interval appendicectomy only selected (35+ after abscess).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WSES 2025**: complicated appendicitis - **2-3 days** post-op antibiotics; interval appendicectomy for **adults 35+ after non-operative management of abscess**; **SSC 2026** - 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in septic peritonitis.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Pregnancy test first; a hungry patient rarely has appendicitis; psoas = retrocaecal, obturator = pelvic; analgesia does not mask; no purgatives or enemas.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Right iliac fossa pain - OPD pathway`,steps:[{label:`Woman of reproductive age - urine hCG`,detail:`Positive with pain - ectopic until proved otherwise`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Peritonitis or shock?`,detail:`Resuscitate, antibiotic within 1 h, refer now`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Score: AIR or Alvarado`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Low / intermediate / high`},{label:`Low - safety-net, review 12-24 h`,tone:`good`},{label:`Intermediate - ultrasound or CT, serial exam`},{label:`High - surgical referral, laparoscopic appendicectomy`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Management by severity`,steps:[{label:`Uncomplicated`,detail:`Lap appendicectomy; no post-op antibiotics; or antibiotics-first if CT-proven, no appendicolith`},{label:`Gangrenous or perforated`,detail:`Surgery + 2-3 days post-op antibiotics (WSES 2025)`},{label:`Appendicular mass`,detail:`Ochsner-Sherren; abandon if pulse rises or peritonitis`},{label:`Abscess over 3-4 cm`,detail:`Percutaneous drainage + antibiotics; interval surgery if 35+`},{label:`Generalised peritonitis`,detail:`30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid, pip-taz, emergency source control`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"surgery-office-hand-infections":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Hand infections spread along **closed anatomical compartments**.`,`**Pus in the hand needs a knife**, not just antibiotics.`,`Paronychia and felon are drained in the clinic under digital block.`,`Flexor tenosynovitis, deep space infection and fight bites are **surgical emergencies**.`,`The usual organism is **Staphylococcus aureus**, increasingly MRSA in India.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Superficial**: acute and chronic paronychia, felon, subcuticular abscess.`,`**Deep**: flexor tenosynovitis, web, thenar, midpalmar and hypothenar spaces.`,`Also radial and ulnar bursae, **Parona space**, septic arthritis and bites.`,`**Chronic paronychia** lasts over 6 weeks and is mainly a wet-work dermatitis.`,`**Kanavel signs**: flexed posture, fusiform swelling, sheath tenderness, pain on passive extension.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A minor breach (hangnail, thorn, lancet) lets bacteria into a closed space.`,`2. Pulp septa trap pus, so pressure rises and causes **throbbing pain**.`,`3. Pressure thromboses pulp vessels, causing distal phalanx osteomyelitis that spares the base.`,`4. Pus in a tendon sheath destroys the tendon's gliding surface within hours.`,`5. Thumb and little finger sheaths join the bursae, allowing a **horseshoe abscess**.`,`6. Palmar lymph drains dorsally, so **palmar infections swell the dorsum**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the mechanism: thorn, fish bone, glucometer lancet, manicure, punch or bite.`,`**Throbbing pain that stops sleep** means pus under pressure.`,`A cut over a knuckle after a fight is a **fight bite** until proved otherwise.`,`Ask about diabetes, CKD, HIV, steroids and IV drug use.`,`Wet work suggests chronic paronychia; tingling then vesicles suggest herpetic whitlow.`,`Check tetanus status and, after a bite, rabies history and the animal's status.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Localise by the **point of maximal tenderness**, not the swelling.`,`Felon: tense pulp with swelling stopping at the **DIP crease**.`,`Check all four **Kanavel signs**; pain on passive extension is most useful.`,`Web space: fingers held **abducted**; midpalmar: **loss of palmar concavity**.`,`Examine a fight bite **with the fist clenched**.`,`Pain out of proportion, bullae and crepitus suggest necrotising fasciitis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Capillary glucose and HbA1c** in every hand infection.`,`Pus for Gram stain and culture from every drained collection.`,`**X-ray** for fight bites, foreign bodies, gas and suspected osteomyelitis.`,`Ultrasound finds wood or thorn and sheath fluid.`,`HSV PCR or Tzanck smear for suspected herpetic whitlow.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Elevate in a high arm sling** and splint in the position of safety.`,`Position of safety: wrist extended 20-30, **MCP flexed 70-90**, IP joints straight.`,`Acute paronychia: soaks, **cephalexin or cloxacillin**, lift the fold if pus.`,`Felon: **high lateral incision on the non-pinch side**, or volar midline if pointing.`,`Never use a fish-mouth incision; never inject anaesthetic into infected tissue.`,`All hand bites: wash 15 minutes and give **amoxicillin-clavulanate for 5 days**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Flexor tenosynovitis: admit, **IV cefazolin**, splint and reassess at 12 hours.`,`No improvement in 24 hours means **theatre irrigation** of the sheath.`,`MRSA risk: co-trimoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline; vancomycin IV if severe.`,`Fight bite: X-ray and **same-day exploration and joint washout**; never suture.`,`Chronic paronychia: keep dry, **topical steroid** plus azole if Candida.`,`Herpetic whitlow: **acyclovir 400 mg TDS** for 7 days; never incise.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Delay in tenosynovitis causes tendon necrosis, adhesions and amputation.`,`Distal forearm swelling after thumb or little finger infection means **Parona space** spread.`,`Tropical diabetic hand sepsis needs insulin, IV antibiotics and early debridement.`,`Necrotising fasciitis: resuscitate and debride as an emergency.`,`A single persistent nail fold lesion may be **SCC or amelanotic melanoma**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review every treated hand infection at **24-48 hours**.`,`Refer the same day for tenosynovitis, deep space infection, fight bite or cat bite.`,`Arrange hand therapy after any deep infection; function is the goal.`,`Complete the rabies schedule and tetanus prophylaxis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for hand infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg oral 6-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Diarrhoea, rash`],[`Cloxacillin`,`500 mg oral 6-hourly, before food`,`5-7 days`,`Cholestatic jaundice`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly; IV 1.2 g`,`5 days bite prophylaxis; 10-14 if infected`,`Diarrhoea`],[`Co-trimoxazole DS`,`1 tablet BD`,`5-7 days (MRSA)`,`Hyperkalaemia; avoid in G6PD deficiency`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg oral 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`C. difficile; not alone for bites`],[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`10-14 days total`,`Phlebitis`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12 hourly`,`With deep MRSA infection`,`Nephrotoxicity; infuse over 60 min`],[`Acyclovir`,`400 mg oral TDS`,`7 days`,`Start within 48-72 hours`],[`Lidocaine 1-2% plain`,`Digital block 4-6 mL, max 3 mg/kg`,`Single use`,`Never into infected tissue`],[`Rabies immunoglobulin`,`Human 20 IU/kg or equine 40 IU/kg`,`Day 0, category III`,`Infiltrate into the wound`]]},{heading:`Hand infections by space`,columns:[`Type`,`Key sign`,`Management`],rows:[[`Acute paronychia`,`Red fold with pus`,`Lift fold, partial nail removal`],[`Chronic paronychia`,`Lost cuticle, wet work`,`Keep dry, topical steroid`],[`Felon`,`Tense pulp to DIP crease`,`High lateral or volar incision`],[`Flexor tenosynovitis`,`Four Kanavel signs`,`IV antibiotics, theatre irrigation`],[`Web space`,`Fingers abducted`,`Separate palmar and dorsal incisions`],[`Thenar space`,`Thumb abducted, thenar ballooning`,`Theatre drainage`],[`Midpalmar space`,`Loss of palmar concavity`,`Theatre drainage`]]},{heading:`Bite injuries of the hand`,columns:[`Bite`,`Organisms`,`Key action`],rows:[[`Human (fight bite)`,`Oral anaerobes, Eikenella`,`X-ray, explore joint, never suture`],[`Cat`,`Pasteurella`,`Early antibiotics; infection within 12-24 h`],[`Dog`,`Pasteurella, Capnocytophaga`,`Wash, leave open, rabies PEP`]]}],remember:[`Pus in the hand needs a knife.`,`Kanavel: flexed, fusiform, sheath tenderness, pain on passive extension.`,`Palmar infections swell the dorsum; do not incise the back.`,`Felon: non-pinch side, never a fish-mouth.`,`Examine a fight bite with the fist clenched; never suture it.`,`Never incise a herpetic whitlow.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - hand infections`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Infections of the hand spreading along **closed compartments** - nail fold, pulp, flexor sheath, web and deep palmar spaces; **pus needs a knife**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Superficial - acute and chronic paronychia, felon; deep - **pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis**, web, thenar, midpalmar, radial/ulnar bursae, Parona; septic arthritis, osteomyelitis; bites (WHO rabies I-III).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**S. aureus (MRSA rising)**, strep; human bite - Eikenella, anaerobes; cat and dog - Pasteurella; diabetes (tropical diabetic hand), wet work (chronic paronychia), HSV (whitlow).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pulp septa -> pressure ischaemia -> **diaphysis sequestrates, base spared**; sheath pus destroys tendon in hours; thumb and little finger sheaths -> radial and ulnar bursae -> horseshoe and Parona; palmar infection swells the dorsum.`],[`Key history`,`Mechanism (thorn, lancet, manicure, bite, **fight**), throbbing night pain, occupation, diabetes and immunosuppression, prior antibiotics, tetanus and rabies status, hand dominance.`],[`Key examination`,`Localise by point of maximal tenderness; **Kanavel - flexed posture, fusiform swelling, sheath tenderness, pain on passive extension**; abducted fingers (web), thenar ballooning, lost palmar concavity; fight bite with fist clenched.`],[`Investigations`,`**Glucose and HbA1c in every case**; pus Gram stain and culture; X-ray for fight bite, FB, gas, osteomyelitis; US for collections; Tzanck or HSV PCR for whitlow.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Cellulitis - antibiotic, **elevation in high sling, splint in position of safety**, review 24-48 h; **cephalexin or cloxacillin 500 mg QID 5-7 days**; bites - **co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS 5 days prophylaxis**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`MRSA - co-trimoxazole DS BD, clindamycin, doxycycline; deep or sheath - **IV cefazolin 2 g 8-hourly + vancomycin if MRSA**, theatre drainage, total 10-14 days; OM 4-6 weeks.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Digital block plain lignocaine 4-6 mL total; paronychia - lift fold, remove nail strip; **felon - high lateral incision on non-pinch side or volar midline if pointing, no fish-mouth**; sheath - catheter irrigation A1 to A5.`],[`Special groups`,`Herpetic whitlow - **acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days, never incise**; chronic paronychia - keep dry, potent topical steroid plus azole; diabetic hand - admit, insulin, early debridement.`],[`Complications`,`Tendon necrosis, stiffness, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, spread to forearm, necrotising fasciitis, amputation.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Kanavel signs, deep space signs, Parona swelling, **fight bite**, cat bite with swelling, lymphangitis with sepsis, necrotising signs, diabetic hand, no improvement in 24-48 h.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review drained infections 24-48 h; hand therapy and early active movement; X-ray at 2-3 weeks if pain persists; complete rabies schedule.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Td per wound type; rabies PEP (IM Essen or ID 2-2-2-0-2; RIG into wounds for category III) under NRCP; gloves for wet work and farm work.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Kanavel's four signs; palmar pus swells the dorsum; never suture a fight bite; never incise a herpetic whitlow; splint MCP flexed, IP straight.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Swollen painful finger or hand`,steps:[{label:`Bite or knuckle laceration?`,detail:`Fight bite - X-ray, co-amoxiclav, same-day washout`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Kanavel signs or deep space signs?`,detail:`Admit, IV cefazolin (+ vancomycin), theatre`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Localised pus in fold or pulp?`,detail:`Drain under digital block`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Vesicles?`,detail:`Herpetic whitlow - acyclovir, do not incise`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Cellulitis only`,detail:`Cephalexin, elevate, splint, review 24-48 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Felon vs paronychia vs flexor tenosynovitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Paronychia`,`Felon`,`Flexor tenosynovitis`],rows:[[`Space`,`Nail fold`,`Pulp septa`,`Flexor sheath`],[`Key sign`,`Red fold, pus at margin`,`Tense pulp stopping at DIP crease`,`Four Kanavel signs`],[`Where treated`,`Clinic`,`Clinic`,`Theatre - emergency`],[`Drainage`,`Lift fold, remove nail strip`,`High lateral non-pinch side`,`Catheter irrigation A1-A5`]]}]},"surgery-office-local-anaesthesia-nerve-blocks":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **local anaesthetic** reversibly blocks nerve conduction where it is applied.`,`It has an aromatic ring, an **ester or amide link**, and a tertiary amine.`,`**1% = 10 mg/mL**, 2% = 20 mg/mL, 0.5% = 5 mg/mL.`,`Adrenaline **1:200,000 = 5 microgram/mL**.`,`Lignocaine and bupivacaine are on India's essential medicines list.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Esters** (procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine) are broken down in plasma to PABA.`,`**Amides** (lignocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine) are metabolised in the liver.`,`Amides have an **'i' before -caine**; esters and amides do not cross-react.`,`By duration: short (chloroprocaine), intermediate (lignocaine), long (bupivacaine).`,`Techniques: topical, infiltration, field block, nerve block, Bier's block and neuraxial.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The unionised base crosses the axon membrane.`,`2. Inside, it re-ionises and blocks the **sodium channel from the inner side**.`,`3. **pKa decides onset**, lipid solubility potency, protein binding duration.`,`4. Pain and autonomic fibres block first; motor fibres block last.`,`5. Acidic pus keeps the drug ionised, so **LA fails in infected tissue**.`,`6. Rising plasma levels affect the brain first, then the heart.`,`7. **Bupivacaine** leaves cardiac sodium channels slowly, so arrest is resistant.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Fainting or palpitations after dental injection are usually **vasovagal or adrenaline**, not allergy.`,`Weigh the patient; doses are per kg of lean body weight.`,`Heart failure, liver disease, old age and pregnancy lower the safe dose.`,`Ask about Raynaud, PAD or Buerger disease before using adrenaline in digits.`,`Ask about **G6PD deficiency** before prilocaine, EMLA or benzocaine.`,`Lie every patient down for injections.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record pulse, BP and SpO2 before larger blocks.`,`Never inject through cellulitis; block proximally through clean skin.`,`Check digit circulation and **capillary refill** before using adrenaline.`,`Document **two-point discrimination** and nerve function before the block.`,`Palpate landmarks: palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris, malleoli, Achilles tendon.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Lignocaine max 3 mg/kg plain** (200 mg), **7 mg/kg with adrenaline** (500 mg).`,`**Bupivacaine max 2 mg/kg** (150 mg), with or without adrenaline.`,`Write the maximum dose in mg and mL before drawing up.`,`**Aspirate before injecting** and every 3-5 mL; inject slowly in increments.`,`Buffer with **8.4% bicarbonate 1 mL per 10 mL** lignocaine to reduce sting.`,`Wait 5-10 minutes for lignocaine and 15-20 minutes for bupivacaine.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Digital ring block: 4-6 mL total, at the dorsolateral base on both sides.`,`Wrist block: median nerve **between palmaris longus and FCR**, 3-5 mL.`,`Ankle block: five nerves, **only two deep** (posterior tibial and deep peroneal).`,`**Bier's block**: 0.5% prilocaine 3 mg/kg; cuff up at least 20 minutes.`,`**Never bupivacaine for Bier's block** and never adrenaline in a penile block.`,`Intercostal block gives the **highest plasma level** of any block.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Children: **EMLA** 60 minutes before needles; LAT gel for face lacerations.`,`Infants under 3 months: EMLA no more than 1 g for 1 hour.`,`Pregnancy: lignocaine and bupivacaine are safe at reduced doses.`,`Count dental cartridges in children; toxic doses come quickly.`,`Never send patients home with anaesthetic eye drops.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Early **LAST**: circumoral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus and twitching.`,`Late LAST: seizures, broad QRS, ventricular arrhythmia and asystole.`,`Stop, airway, oxygen, **midazolam** for fits and **20% lipid 1.5 mL/kg**.`,`In LAST arrest, use small adrenaline doses and continue CPR for an hour.`,`Methaemoglobinaemia: **methylene blue 1-2 mg/kg** unless G6PD deficient.`,`White digit after 20-30 minutes: warm, remove tourniquet, **phentolamine 1 mg** locally.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Protect the numb part from heat and injury until feeling returns.`,`Start oral analgesia before a bupivacaine block wears off.`,`Numbness or weakness beyond 48 hours needs review and referral.`,`Admit after any LAST event, anaphylaxis or methaemoglobinaemia.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Local anaesthetics and rescue drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Lignocaine 1-2%`,`Max 3 mg/kg plain; 7 mg/kg with adrenaline`,`30-60 min plain; 1-3 h with adrenaline`,`LAST; aspirate first`],[`Bupivacaine 0.25-0.5%`,`Max 2 mg/kg (150 mg)`,`4-8 hours or more`,`Most cardiotoxic; never Bier's block`],[`Prilocaine 0.5%`,`Max 6 mg/kg; Bier's 3 mg/kg`,`Intermediate`,`Methaemoglobinaemia over about 600 mg`],[`EMLA cream`,`1-2 g per site under occlusion`,`Apply 60 min before`,`Methaemoglobinaemia in infants`],[`LAT gel`,`1-3 mL into wound`,`20-30 min`,`Not on mucosa or end-arteries`],[`20% lipid emulsion`,`1.5 mL/kg IV, then 0.25 mL/kg/min`,`Max about 12 mL/kg`,`For LAST only`],[`Midazolam`,`0.05-0.1 mg/kg IV`,`For LAST seizures`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Methylene blue 1%`,`1-2 mg/kg IV over 5 min`,`Repeat after 1 h if needed`,`Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency`],[`Phentolamine`,`1 mg in 1-10 mL saline locally`,`Once`,`Hypotension`],[`Adrenaline 1:1000`,`0.5 mg IM thigh`,`Repeat after 5 min`,`For anaphylaxis`]]},{heading:`Esters versus amides`,columns:[`Feature`,`Esters`,`Amides`],rows:[[`Examples`,`Procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine`,`Lignocaine, bupivacaine, prilocaine, ropivacaine`],[`Metabolism`,`Plasma pseudocholinesterase`,`Liver`],[`Allergy`,`More common (PABA)`,`Rare`],[`Spelling clue`,`No 'i' before -caine`,`'i' before -caine`]]},{heading:`Ankle block nerves`,columns:[`Nerve`,`Territory`,`Landmark`,`Volume`],rows:[[`Posterior tibial (deep)`,`Sole`,`Behind medial malleolus, behind artery`,`5-7 mL`],[`Deep peroneal (deep)`,`First web space`,`Between EHL and tibialis anterior`,`3-5 mL`],[`Superficial peroneal`,`Dorsum of foot`,`Tibial crest to lateral malleolus`,`5 mL`],[`Saphenous`,`Medial ankle`,`Medial malleolus to tibialis anterior`,`3-5 mL`],[`Sural`,`Lateral foot`,`Lateral malleolus to Achilles tendon`,`3-5 mL`]]}],remember:[`1% is 10 mg/mL; lignocaine 3 or 7 mg/kg; bupivacaine 2 mg/kg.`,`Amides have an 'i' before -caine.`,`pKa onset, lipid solubility potency, protein binding duration.`,`Local anaesthetic fails in pus; block proximally.`,`Never bupivacaine for Bier's block; prilocaine is the choice.`,`LAST: stop, oxygen, benzodiazepine, lipid 1.5 mL/kg.`,`Ankle block: five nerves, two deep.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - local anaesthetics and nerve blocks`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Drugs that **reversibly block voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the axon**, abolishing conduction without loss of consciousness.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Esters** (procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, cocaine - pseudocholinesterase, PABA allergy) vs **amides** ('i' before -caine, hepatic); short, intermediate, long acting; topical to neuraxial.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Unionised base crosses membrane, cation blocks channel; **pKa - onset, lipid solubility - potency, protein binding - duration**; acid pus ionises drug; B and C fibres first, motor last; bupivacaine **fast in, slow out**.`],[`Key history`,`Previous reaction (mostly vasovagal or adrenaline), weight (lean), heart, liver, pregnancy, beta-blockers or tricyclics, PAD or Raynaud (no adrenaline), G6PD and infants (prilocaine), neuropathy.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals and weight; site free of infection; circulation of digit; **baseline two-point discrimination and power**; palpate landmarks (palmaris longus, FCU, EHL, malleoli).`],[`Investigations`,`None for simple blocks; SpO2 and ECG for large volumes; co-oximetry for methaemoglobinaemia; INR before deep blocks on warfarin.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Lignocaine 3 mg/kg plain (200 mg), 7 mg/kg with adrenaline (500 mg)**; **bupivacaine 2 mg/kg (150 mg)**; prilocaine 6 mg/kg (Bier's 0.5% 3 mg/kg); 1% = 10 mg/mL; adrenaline 1:200,000 = 5 microgram/mL.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Mixtures additive (fractions under 1); levobupivacaine or ropivacaine for large volumes; buffer 1 mL 8.4% bicarbonate per 10 mL lignocaine; topical EMLA 60 min, LAT 20-30 min.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Digital ring 4-6 mL or single volar 2-3 mL; wrist - median between PL and FCR, ulnar deep to FCU, radial subcutaneous; **ankle - 5 nerves, only posterior tibial and deep peroneal deep**; Bier's cuff 20 min minimum; intercostal below rib.`],[`Special groups`,`Adrenaline safe in healthy digits (WALANT) - **never penile or Bier's**; reduce dose in elderly, heart failure, liver disease, pregnancy; infants - EMLA max 1 g for 1 h.`],[`Complications`,`**LAST**, vasovagal, allergy, methaemoglobinaemia (prilocaine, benzocaine), digital ischaemia (phentolamine 1 mg), nerve injury, pneumothorax after intercostal.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Perioral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste; seizure, broad QRS, arrhythmia; SpO2 near 85% on oxygen; persistent numbness over 48 h.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Observe 30 min after large blocks; LAST - 2 h after CNS event, 4-6 h after cardiac; protect numb part; analgesia before bupivacaine wears off.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**LAST: stop, airway, oxygen, benzodiazepine, 20% lipid 1.5 mL/kg then 0.25 mL/kg/min, max 12 mL/kg; adrenaline 1 microgram/kg or less; CPR an hour**; methylene blue not in G6PD deficiency.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity`,steps:[{label:`Stop injecting, call help`,detail:`Perioral numbness, tinnitus, twitching`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Airway, 100% oxygen, ventilate`,detail:`Hypoxia and acidosis worsen toxicity`},{label:`Seizures - benzodiazepine`,detail:`Midazolam 0.05-0.1 mg/kg IV`},{label:`20% lipid emulsion`,detail:`1.5 mL/kg over 2-3 min, then 0.25 mL/kg/min; repeat bolus x2; max 12 mL/kg`},{label:`Arrest - prolonged CPR`,detail:`Adrenaline 1 microgram/kg or less; amiodarone; no lignocaine, vasopressin, CCB, beta-blocker`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Maximum doses at a glance`,columns:[`Agent`,`Plain`,`With adrenaline`,`Note`],rows:[[`Lignocaine`,`3 mg/kg (200 mg)`,`7 mg/kg (500 mg)`,`Onset 2-5 min`],[`Bupivacaine`,`2 mg/kg (150 mg)`,`2 mg/kg`,`Most cardiotoxic; never Bier's`],[`Prilocaine`,`6 mg/kg (400 mg)`,`8 mg/kg with felypressin`,`Bier's 0.5% 3 mg/kg; MetHb`],[`Ropivacaine`,`3 mg/kg (200 mg)`,`-`,`Less cardiotoxic, less motor block`],[`Chloroprocaine`,`11 mg/kg`,`14 mg/kg`,`Ester, very short`]]}]}};export{e as default};